The '''2023–2025 Sundhnúkur eruptions''' ({{langx|is|Eldgosin við Sundhnúksgíga 2023–2025}}) are a series of [[volcanic eruption]]s on the [[Southern Peninsula (Iceland)|Reykjanes Peninsula]], near the town of [[Grindavík]], [[Iceland]]. Between December 2023 and August 2025, there have been nine eruptions, following an intense series of earthquakes in November 2023. Although localised, the seismic and volcanic activity has caused significant disruption across the western part of the peninsula, especially for the town of Grindavík. However, the [[Capital Region (Iceland)|Capital Region]], including [[Reykjavík]], has remained physically unaffected. The eruptions were preceded by an intense [[earthquake swarm]] in the [[Eldvörp–Svartsengi|Eldvörp'''–'''Svartsengi]] volcanic system that began on 24 October 2023, caused by a [[Igneous intrusion|magmatic intrusion]] underneath the area. The frequency and intensity of the earthquakes dramatically increased on 10 November 2023, with around 20,000 tremors recorded by that time, the largest of which exceeded [[Seismic magnitude scales|magnitude]] 5.3. Grindavík was subsequently evacuated due to the creation of large-scale [[subsidence]], including the formation of an extensive [[graben|graben valley]], which caused significant damage. This extensional tectonic activity likely altered magma pathways and triggered subsequent eruptions.

The volcanic eruption series at the [[Sundhnúksgígar]] crater chain began on 18 December 2023, with an initial eruption that lasted for three days.<ref name=":28">{{Cite journal |last=Troll |first=Valentin R. |last2=Deegan |first2=Frances M. |last3=Thordarson |first3=Thor |last4=Tryggvason |first4=Ari |last5=Krmíček |first5=Lukáš |last6=Moreland |first6=William M. |last7=Lund |first7=Björn |last8=Bindeman |first8=Ilya N. |last9=Höskuldsson |first9=Ármann |last10=Day |first10=James M. D. |date=2024 |title=The Fagradalsfjall and Sundhnúkur Fires of 2021–2024: A single magma reservoir under the Reykjanes Peninsula, Iceland? |url=https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/abs/10.1111/ter.12733 |journal=Terra Nova |language=en |volume=36 |issue=6 |pages=447–456 |doi=10.1111/ter.12733 |issn=1365-3121|hdl=11012/249930 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> This eruption was preceded by land uplift in the Svartsengi area, which subsequently deflated upon eruption, indicating the accumulation of [[magma]] at a depth of {{convert|4–5|km|abbr=unit}} beneath Svartsengi. This magma source fed the initial eruption as well as all subsequent eruptions in the series. The second eruption occurred on 14 January 2024, lasting approximately two days. This event had a fissure opening less than {{convert|100|m|abbr=unit}} from a nearby town. The eruption breached anti-lava defences and destroyed three homes. Additionally, the eruption formed a new [[graben]], although it was substantially less extensive than the one formed in November 2023. Tragically, just before this eruption, one person was reported missing and presumed to have fallen into a [[Fissure|crack]] caused by seismic activity, resulting in their death. On 8 February 2024, the third eruption caused extensive damage, including the disruption of a [[Water pipelines|hot-water pipeline]] from the [[Svartsengi power station]]. Although the eruption lasted only about two days, it resulted in a loss of [[Water supply|hot water supply]] for several days across the Reykjanes Peninsula. The [[Capital Region (Iceland)|Capital Region]], however, remained unaffected. The fourth eruption started on 16 March 2024 and became the longest in the series, spanning 54 days. A magmatic intrusion had occurred earlier in the month but did not reach the surface. The fifth eruption, which began on 29 May 2024, continued for 24 days. This eruption caused damage to [[power lines]] and cut off several road sections. On 22 August 2024, the sixth eruption commenced, lasting 14 days. It released {{convert|61|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of lava, covering an area of {{convert|15.8|km2|abbr=unit}} and resulting in {{convert|40|cm|abbr=unit}} of land subsidence. Despite being the largest eruption in the series so far, it did not cause any infrastructure damage. The seventh eruption began on 20 November 2024 and extended over 18 days. It quickly engulfed the parking lot of the [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon]] and threatened protective barriers in the area. The eighth eruption commenced on 1 April 2025 and concluded approximately seven hours later the same day, marking the shortest and least intense event in the eruptive series to date. In the days following the eruption, a substantial magmatic [[Dike (geology)|dike]] intruded underground without breaching the surface. The ninth eruption of the series commenced on 16 July 2025 and persisted for roughly 20 days. While it posed no threat to infrastructure, the [[Volcanic gas|gas pollution]] spread unusually far during the eruption's early stages and the measured pollution levels in nearby towns and cities were higher than those typically observed during the previous eruptions in the series. 

Land uplift has resumed since the last eruption and is ongoing, with the current rate of vertical [[Deformation (volcanology)|ground deformation]] lower than that observed before the previous eruption, although magmatic inflow into the [[magma chamber]] remains steady. Since March 2024, each eruption has been preceded by the accumulation of roughly {{convert|17–23|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma in the reservoir. By the end of November 2025, the interval since the previous eruption had become the longest recorded within the eruptive sequence that began in November 2023, exceeding 115 days since the conclusion of the July–August 2025 eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jósefsdóttir |first=Sólrún Dögg |date=2 December 2025 |title=Lengsta goshléið frá upphafi hrinunnar |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252811795d/lengsta-goshleid-fra-upp-hafi-hrinunnar |access-date=11 January 2026 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Currently, the volume of magma beneath the area has reached more than {{convert|23|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, representing the highest threshold observed in the eruptive series to date.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ísleifsson |first=Atli |date=17 March 2026 |title=Mesta kvikusöfnun á milli eldgosa frá upphafi eldgosahrinu |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20262857075d/mesta-kvikusofnun-a-milli-eld-gosa-fra-upp-hafi-eld-gosa-hrinu |access-date=17 March 2026 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] considers a new [[Igneous intrusion|magmatic intrusion]] to be more likely than not to occur, which may culminate in a volcanic eruption.<ref name=":89">{{Cite web |date=19 August 2025 |title=Earthquake in Brennisteinsfjöll – Faster Subsidence in Krýsuvík and Continued Magma Accumulation at Svartsengi |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/earthquake-in-brennisteinsfjoll-faster-subsidence-in-krysuvik-and-continued-magma-accumulation-at-svartsengi |access-date=1 September 2025 |website=Icelandic Meteorological Office |language=en}}</ref>

== Etymology ==
The name "[[Sundhnúkur]]" {{IPA|is|ˈsʏnt.n̥ʏː.kʏr|}} literally translates to "passage peak". It is the highest point in the old crater row in the area, standing at {{convert|134|m|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sundhnúkur |url=https://peakvisor.com/peak/sundhnukur.html |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=PeakVisor |language=en}}</ref> Historically, the name reflects the navigational challenges faced by sailors due to numerous rocks and [[Skerry|skerries]] near the shore. Sundhnúkur served as a crucial visual marker for sailors navigating to the town of Grindavík, as its peak was visible from beyond the shore. Notably, the passage through the rocks is known by a different name, emphasising that Sundhnúkur specifically denotes the peak itself as a [[landmark]] rather than the passage. Sailors of the time also needed to be knowledgeable about various leading marks to safely reach the harbour. The term "[[Sundhnúksgígar]]" {{IPA|is|ˈsʏnt.n̥ʏːks.ciː.ɣar|}}, meaning "passage peak craters", was coined by geologist Jón Jónsson in 1974, highlighting the series of craters associated with this notable landmark.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 November 2023 |title=Af hverju Sundhnúkur? |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/15/af_hverju_sundhnukur/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Sundhnúkahraun við Grindavík – Jón Jónsson |url=https://ferlir.is/sundhnukahraun-vid-grindavik-jon-jonsson/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=Ferlir |language=is}}</ref>

== Background ==
[[File:Reykjanes volcanic systems.png|right|thumb|Map of volcanic systems on the Reykjanes Peninsula. Grindavík is at the south-western end of the Eldvörp'''–'''Svartsengi system (marked here as 2)]]

The Sundhnúksgígar crater row was formed approximately 2420 ± 100 [[Radiocarbon year|radiocarbon years]] before present (BP), as determined by [[Carbon-14|carbon-14 (<sup>14</sup>C)]] [[Carbon-14 dating|dating]] conducted by [[Uppsala University]], using 1950 as the baseline.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Jónsson |first=Jón |date=September 1973 |editor-last=Shcopka |editor-first=Sigfús A. |title=Náttúrufræðingurinn |url=https://timarit.is/page/4271225#page/n27/mode/2up |journal=Hið íslenska náttúrufræðifélag |volume=43 |issue=3–4 |pages=151 |via=[[Timarit.is|Tímarit]]}}</ref> This ancient lava flow was measured to cover an area of {{convert|25.77|km2|abbr=unit}} and has an estimated volume of at least {{convert|520|e6m3||abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Jónsson |first=Jón |url=https://rafhladan.is/handle/10802/4981 |title=Jarðfræðikort af Reykjanesskaga |publisher=Orkustofnun jarðhitadeild |publication-date=April 1978 |pages=138 |language=is |trans-title=Geologic Map of Reykjanes Peninsula |via=Rafhlaðan}}</ref> It originated from a crater row extending nearly {{convert|9|km|abbr=unit}} in length.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sæmundsson |first1=Kristján |last2=Sigurgeirsson |first2=Magnús Á. |date=12 March 2018 |title=Getið þið sagt mér það helsta um eldstöðvakerfið sem kennt er við Svartsengi og Eldvörp? |url=http://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=29508 |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=Vísindavefurinn |language=is}}</ref> The crater row includes two named craters: Sundhnúkur and Melhóll {{IPA|is|ˈmɛl.houːtl̥|}}, with the latter situated nearly {{convert|2|km|abbr=unit}} to the south-west of Sundhnúkur. The lava from this period was a mixture of [[Lava#Pāhoehoe|pāhoehoe]] and [[Lava#{{okina}}A{{okina}}ā|{{okina}}a{{okina}}ā]] lava, indicative of varying eruption dynamics. Additionally, there are likely traces of an even older lava field beneath the circa 2400-year-old lava, though much of it has been obscured by the more recent eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Sundhnúkahraun – Rauðhóll |url=https://ferlir.is/sundhnukahraun-raudholl/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=Ferlir |language=is}}</ref> The town of [[Grindavík]] is located along the south-western boundary of this ancient lava field.<ref name="Jenness Clifton 2009 pp. 715–728">{{cite journal |last1=Jenness |first1=Maria H. |last2=Clifton |first2=Amy E. |date=17 February 2009 |title=Controls on the geometry of a Holocene crater row: a field study from southwest Iceland |journal=[[Bulletin of Volcanology]] |language=en |publisher=Springer Science and Business Media LLC |volume=71 |issue=7 |pages=715–728 |bibcode=2009BVol...71..715J |doi=10.1007/s00445-009-0267-9 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ásgrímssson |first=Þorsteinn |date=11 November 2023 |title=Hver er þessi Sundhnúkagígaröð? |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/hver_er_thessi_sundhnukagigarod/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240317002844/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/hver_er_thessi_sundhnukagigarod/ |archive-date=17 March 2024 |access-date=17 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The most recent [[Shield volcano|shield volcanoes]] to form in this particular area of the Reykjanes region were Sandfellshæð {{IPA|is|ˈsant.fɛtl̥s.haiθ|}} and Þráinsskjöldur {{IPA|is|ˈθrau.ɪns.scœl.tʏr̥|}}, which emerged around 14,000 years ago.<ref name=":66">{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Hermann Nökkvi |date=30 December 2024 |title=Magnús Tumi stillir upp þremur sviðsmyndum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/12/30/magnus_tumi_stillir_upp_thremur_svidsmyndum/ |access-date=30 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> This volcanic activity was triggered by the retreat of glaciers at the conclusion of the [[last glacial period]]. As the ice melted, the Earth's crust underwent [[isostatic rebound]], a process where the reduction in pressure allowed magma to rise more easily to the surface, initiating volcanic activity. Given these factors, it is considered highly unlikely that a shield volcano is currently forming in the region.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sæmundsson |first1=Kristján |last2=Sigurgeirsson |first2=Magnús |date=11 March 2021 |title=Hvað er vitað um dyngjugos á Reykjanesskaga? |url=https://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=81329 |access-date=31 December 2024 |website=Vísindavefurinn |language=is}}</ref>

The [[Reykjanes peninsula|Reykjanes]] {{IPA|is|ˈreiː.ca.nɛs|}} Peninsula, located in south-western [[Iceland]], has entered a new volcanic cycle after a period of approximately 800 years of relative inactivity. This resurgence was marked by heightened seismic events and volcanic activity near the [[hyaloclastite]] mountain [[Þorbjörn (mountain)|Þorbjörn]] {{IPA|is|ˈθɔr.pʲœrtn̥|}} beginning in late 2019.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sigfússon |first1=Ingólfur Bjarni |last2=Aðalbjörnsson |first2=Tryggvi |last3=Sigurðardóttir |first3=Kristín |last4=Kolbeinsson |first4=Jóhann Bjarni |last5=Guðmundsson |first5=Ingvar Haukur |last6=Þórisson |first6=Arnar |date=21 November 2023 |title=Hamfarir í Grindavík gætu boðað nýjan veruleika á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.ruv.is/kveikur/grindavik-reykjanesskagi/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123110330/https://www.ruv.is/kveikur/grindavik-reykjanesskagi/ |archive-date=23 November 2023 |access-date=23 November 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The preceding period of volcanic activity occurred between 780 and 1240 AD, with the final phase, known as the [[Reykjanes Fires]], occurring between 1210 and 1240 AD. This phase resulted in the formation of one of the largest continuous lava fields in [[History of Iceland|Iceland's historical record]], Arnarseturshraun {{IPA|is|ˌar.nar.ˈsɛt.ʏrs.r̥œyːn|}} and Eldvarpahraun {{IPA|is|ˈɛlt.var̥.pa.r̥œyːn|}}.<ref name=":66" /><ref name="icelandicvolcanoes-REY" /> This activity was concentrated primarily west and north of Svartsengi {{IPA|is|ˈsvar̥t.seiːŋ.ɡɪ|}} and Þorbjörn.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurgeirsson |first=Magnús Á. |date=7 November 2023 |title=Er vitað hversu mörg eldgos urðu á Reykjanesskaganum á síðasta eldgosatímabili? |url=http://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=81515 |access-date=11 August 2024 |website=Vísindavefurinn |language=is}}</ref> Volcanic periods on the peninsula typically span 400 to 600 years, during which eruptions occur at six to seven distinct locations, including regions near the [[Capital Region (Iceland)|Capital Region]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Aðalbjörnsson |first1=Tryggvi |last2=Guðmundsson |first2=Ingvar Haukur |date=26 March 2024 |title=Byggð á höfuðborgarsvæðinu gæti stafað gæti stafað ógn af eldsumbrotum í Krýsuvík |url=https://www.ruv.is/kveikur/krysuvik/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":73">{{Cite web |date=21 March 2025 |title=Erfitt að fá skýra mynd af þróun virkninnar til næstu ára á Reykjanesskaganum |url=https://vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/erfitt-ad-fa-skyra-mynd-af-throun-virkninnar-til-naestu-ara-a-reykjanesskaganum |access-date=23 March 2025 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":74" /><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Hjartardóttir |first1=Ásta Rut |last2=Óladóttir |first2=Bergrún Arna |date=March 2025 |title=Náttúruvá á höfuðborgarsvæðinu |url=https://www.vedur.is/media/ads_in_header/VI2024-003_NatturuvaHbsv_ARHBAO_31mars2025.pdf |access-date=17 May 2025 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is, en}}</ref> These periods are composed of multiple phases, each lasting from several years to several decades, typically interspersed with breaks between 100 and 150 years. Furthermore, volcanic activity may shift to different areas of the peninsula every 30 to 50 years. Based on historical patterns, the current cycle is expected to continue until approximately 2400–2600 CE.<ref name=":66" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðjónsson |first=Viðar |date=30 January 2024 |title=Mun líklega gjósa á sex til sjö stöðum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/30/mun_liklega_gjosa_a_sex_til_sjo_stodum/ |access-date=9 February 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240213080440/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/30/mun_liklega_gjosa_a_sex_til_sjo_stodum/|archive-date=13 February 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Ólafur |date=6 May 2025 |title=Líklega 100-150 ár í að næsti hluti Reykjanesskagans fari af stað |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/05/06/lik_leg_a_100_150_ar_i_ad_naesti_hluti_fari_af_stad/ |access-date=7 May 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last1=Óladóttir |first1=Bergrún Arna |last2=Pfeffer |first2=Melissa Anne |last3=Barsotti |first3=Sara |last4=Björnsson |first4=Bogi Brynjar |last5=Titos |first5=Manuel |last6=Gupta |first6=Réne |last7=Stefánsdóttir |first7=Gerður |last8=Tarquini |first8=Simone |last9=Vitturi |first9=Mattia de' Michieli |date=June 2023 |title=Langtímahættumat fyrir Reykjanesskaga vestan Kleifarvatns |url=https://icelandicvolcanoes.is/data/Gosva/ReykjanesGreinager%C3%B0_Final_BAO_ofl_2023_01_20230828.pdf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231123233903/https://icelandicvolcanoes.is/data/Gosva/ReykjanesGreinager%C3%B0_Final_BAO_ofl_2023_01_20230828.pdf |archive-date=23 November 2023 |access-date=24 November 2023 |website=Catalogue of Icelandic Volcanoes |publisher=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref>

Iceland frequently experiences earthquakes due to its position on the [[Mid-Atlantic Ridge]], where the [[Eurasian Plate|Eurasian]] and [[North American Plate|North American]] [[Plate tectonics|tectonic plates]] meet. However, the 2023 seismic swarm was unusually extensive and has been attributed to a roughly {{convert|15|km|abbr=unit}} [[Igneous intrusion|magmatic intrusion]] that originated south-west of Kálffellsheiði {{IPA|is|ˈkaul.fɛtl̥s.ˌheiðɪ|}}, followed the [[Sundhnúksgígar]] crater chain, and terminated offshore just beyond the town of Grindavík {{IPA|is|ˈkrɪn.ta.viːk|}}, reaching a shallowest depth of approximately {{convert|800|m|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":78">{{Cite web |date=11 November 2023 |title=Kvika á 800 metra dýpi eða nær |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/kvika_a_800_metra_dypi_eda_naer/ |access-date=5 January 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":79">{{Cite web |last=Einarsdóttir |first=Gréta Sigríður |date=15 November 2023 |title=Þróun kvikugangs undir Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-15-throun-kvikugangs-undir-grindavik-397218 |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The largest earthquakes originated beneath this crater chain, the strongest of which reached magnitude 5.3.<ref name=":81" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=10 November 2023 |title=LIVEBLOG: Reykjanes Peninsula and Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2023-11-10-liveblog-reykjanes-peninsula-and-grindavik-396249 |access-date=5 January 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Osborne |first=Hannah |date=13 November 2023 |title=Iceland volcano: Eruption feared as 10-mile-long 'magma tunnel' forms beneath town |url=https://www.livescience.com/planet-earth/volcanos/iceland-volcano-eruption-feared-as-10-mile-long-magma-tunnel-forms-beneath-town |access-date=5 January 2025 |website=<nowiki>[Live Science]]</nowiki> |language=en}}</ref> This occurred in the [[Eldvörp–Svartsengi|Eldvörp'''–'''Svartsengi]] volcanic system,<ref name="icelandicvolcanoes-REY">{{cite web |title=Reykjanes |url=https://icelandicvolcanoes.is/?volcano=REY |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231102175523/https://icelandicvolcanoes.is//?volcano=REY |archive-date=2 November 2023 |access-date=15 November 2023 |website=Catalogue of Icelandic Volcanoes |publisher=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]]/[[University of Iceland]]/[[Icelandic Police|National Commissioner of the Icelandic Police]] |language=en}}</ref> which has experienced 16 magmatic intrusions since January 2020 and 12 of them since October 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Einarsson |first=Páll |date=19 February 2024 |title=Af hverju gýs ekki við Svartsengi þar sem kvikan safnast fyrir og landrisið er mest? |url=https://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=86229 |access-date=18 April 2025 |website=Vísindavefurinn |language=is}}</ref> Nine of these intrusions have resulted in volcanic eruptions,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórólfsdóttir |first=Sonja Sif |date=28 December 2023 |title=Kvikuinnskot myndaðist nær Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/28/kvikuinnskot_myndadist_naer_grindavik/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303222256/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/28/kvikuinnskot_myndadist_naer_grindavik/ |archive-date=3 March 2024 |access-date=3 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=16 January 2024 |title=Kvikugangurinn lyfti landinu við Svartsengi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/16/kvikugangurinn_lyfti_landinu_vid_svartsengi/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240418145328/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/16/kvikugangurinn_lyfti_landinu_vid_svartsengi/ |archive-date=18 April 2024 |access-date=18 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Másson |first=Agnar Már |date=9 February 2024 |title=Landris líklega hafið að nýju |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/landris_liklega_hafid_ad_nyju/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240418145317/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/landris_liklega_hafid_ad_nyju/ |archive-date=18 April 2024 |access-date=18 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2024 |title=Kvikuhlaup mögulega hafið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/16/kvikuhlaup_mogulega_hafid/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Halldórsson |first=Skúli |date=29 May 2024 |title=Kvikuhlaup hafið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/29/kvikuhlaup_hafid/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsdóttir |first=Þorgerður Anna |date=22 August 2024 |title=Neyðarstigi lýst yfir vegna yfirvofandi kvikuhlaups |url=https://nyr.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-08-22-neydarstigi-lyst-yfir-vegna-yfirvofandi-kvikuhlaups-420077 |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=20 November 2024 |title=Kvikuhlaup hafið á Sundhnúksgígaröð |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-20-kvikuhlaup-hafid-a-sundhnuksgigarod-428060 |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=1 April 2025 |title=Enn talsverð skjálftavirkni á norðurenda kvikugangsins |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-04-01-enn-talsverd-skjalftavirkni-a-nordurenda-kvikugangsins-440534 |access-date=11 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":85">{{Cite web |last=Snævarr |first=Þorgrímur Kári |date=16 July 2025 |title=Eldgos hafið á Sundhnúksgígaröðinni |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448672 |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> whereas the others did not reach the surface.<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 January 2020 |title=Möguleg kvikusöfnun undir svæðinu við fjallið Þorbjörn á Reykjanesi |url=https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/moguleg-kvikusofnun-undir-svaedinu-vid-fjallid-thorbjorn-a-reykjanesi |access-date=18 April 2025 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2020 |title=Landris hafið að nýju við Þorbjörn á Reykjanesi |url=https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/landris-hafid-ad-nyju-vid-thorbjorn-a-reykjanesi |access-date=18 April 2025 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=3 April 2020 |title=Vísbendingar um nýtt kvikuinnskot |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2020/04/03/visbendingar_um_nytt_kvikuinnskot/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240311231443/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2020/04/03/visbendingar_um_nytt_kvikuinnskot/ |archive-date=11 March 2024 |access-date=11 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 June 2022 |title=Ekkert landris mælist lengur vestur af Þorbirni |url=https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/ekkert-landris-maelist-lengur-vestur-af-thorbirni |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jóhannsson |first=Róbert |date=1 November 2023 |title=Kvikuinnskot vestan við Þorbjörn |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-01-kvikuinnskot-vestan-vid-thorbjorn-395490 |access-date=11 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=10 November 2023 |title=Kvikuinnskot að myndast við Sundhnúkagíga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/10/kvikuinnskot_ad_myndast_vid_sundhnukagiga/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240303222259/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/10/kvikuinnskot_ad_myndast_vid_sundhnukagiga/ |archive-date=3 March 2024 |access-date=3 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Másson |first=Agnar Már |date=2 March 2024 |title=Kvikuinnskot virðist yfirstaðið: Nýr gangur myndast |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/02/kvikuinnskot_virdist_yfirstadid_nyr_gangur_myndast/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240302201647/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/02/kvikuinnskot_virdist_yfirstadid_nyr_gangur_myndast/ |archive-date=2 March 2024 |access-date=3 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Previous volcanic unrest during this period had been predominantly associated with the [[Fagradalsfjall]] {{IPA|is|ˈfaːɣ.ra.dals.fjatl̥|}} system,<ref name="icelandicvolcanoes-FAG">{{cite web |title=Fagradalsfjall |url=https://icelandicvolcanoes.is/?volcano=FAG |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230704214509/https://icelandicvolcanoes.is/?volcano=FAG |archive-date=4 July 2023 |access-date=15 November 2023 |website=Catalogue of Icelandic Volcanoes |publisher=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]]/[[University of Iceland]]/[[Icelandic Police|National Commissioner of the Icelandic Police]] |language=en}}</ref> where three out of four magmatic intrusions led to eruptions.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 March 2021 |title=Eldgos hafið við Fagradalsfjall |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2021/03/19/eldgos_hafid_vid_fagradalsfjall/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=5 January 2022 |title=Jarðskjálftahrina á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.vedur.is/eldfjoll/eldgos-a-reykjanesi/frettir-og-vidvaranir/jardskjalftahrina-a-reykjanesskaga |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 August 2022 |title=Eldgos hafið við Fagradalsfjall |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20222293331d/eldgos-hafid-vid-fagradalsfjall |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 July 2023 |title=Eldgos hófst við fjallið Litla Hrút á Reykjanesskaga klukkan 16:40 í gær. |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/eldgos-hofst-vid-fjallid-litla-hrut-a-reykjanesskaga-klukkan-1640-i-gaer/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> Since 2021, there have been 12 identified volcanic eruptions on the Reykjanes Peninsula.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |date=25 October 2023 |title=Volcanic unrest Grindavík - older updates |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/volcanic-unrest-grindavik-older-updates |access-date=21 November 2023 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=26 February 2024 |title=Volcano-tectonic activity on the Reykjanes Peninsula since 2019: Overview and associated hazards |url=https://en.vedur.is/volcanoes/fagradalsfjall-eruption/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Parks |first1=Michelle |last2=Drouin |first2=Vincent |last3=Sigmundsson |first3=Freysteinn |last4=Hjartardóttir |first4=Ásta R. |last5=Geirsson |first5=Halldór |last6=Pedersen |first6=Gro B. M. |last7=Belart |first7=Joaquin M. C. |last8=Barsotti |first8=Sara |last9=Lanzi |first9=Chiara |last10=Vogfjörd |first10=Kristín |last11=Hooper |first11=Andrew |last12=Ófeigsson |first12=Benedikt |last13=Hreinsdóttir |first13=Sigrún |last14=Gestsson |first14=Einar Bessi |last15=Þrastarson |first15=Ragnar H. |last16=Einarsson |first16=Páll |last17=Tolpekin |first17=Valentyn |last18=Rotheram-Clarke |first18=Drew |last19=Gunnarsson |first19=Sydney R. |last20=Óskarsson |first20=Birgir V. |last21=Pinel |first21=Virginie |date=15 May 2025 |title=2023–2024 inflation-deflation cycles at Svartsengi and repeated dike injections and eruptions at the Sundhnúkur crater row, Reykjanes Peninsula, Iceland |journal=Earth and Planetary Science Letters |volume=658 |article-number=119324 |doi=10.1016/j.epsl.2025.119324 |issn=0012-821X|doi-access=free |bibcode=2025E&PSL.65819324P }}</ref>

