=== October 2023 earthquakes ===
An intense [[earthquake swarm]] began near Svartsengi, north of Grindavík, on the night of 24 October 2023. By the morning of 25 October, the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO) had reported over 1,000 earthquakes, including events of magnitudes 3.9 and 4.5 at around {{convert|5|km|abbr=unit}} depth. The swarm, interpreted as being triggered by stress changes related to previous intrusive magmatic activity on the Reykjanes Peninsula, continued through 26 and 27 October, with about 5,800 earthquakes recorded since its onset. On 28 October, ground uplift near Svartsengi, likely due to a magmatic intrusion, was detected, and by 29 October, deformation rates were higher than during similar events in 2020 and 2022. [[Seismic activity]] had decreased by 29 October, but over 7,000 earthquakes had occurred since the beginning of the swarm. On 30 October, GPS data confirmed that deformation was still ongoing, and modelling suggested that the magmatic intrusion was occurring at about {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} depth, with around 1,300 earthquakes detected on the Reykjanes Peninsula in the previous 24 hours. On 31 October, an intense earthquake swarm beneath the mountain [[Þorbjörn (mountain)|Þorbjörn]], with a maximum magnitude of 3.7, indicated continued magma movement. IMO and Civil Defence closely monitored the situation, noting that rapid changes were possible, but that magma movements could also fade without leading to an eruption.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":2">{{Cite news |last=Birkebaek |first=Johannes |date=27 October 2023 |editor-last=Solsvik |editor-first=Terje |title=Iceland hit by thousands of small earthquakes in volcano warning |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/iceland-hit-by-thousands-small-earthquakes-volcano-warning-2023-10-27/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231105023605/https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/iceland-hit-by-thousands-small-earthquakes-volcano-warning-2023-10-27/ |archive-date=5 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |work=[[Reuters]] |language=en}}</ref>

The first clear signs of a new magma intrusion near the Svartsengi area, located north-west of Þorbjörn on the Reykjanes Peninsula, were detected in late October 2023. This phenomenon, identified through the latest GPS data and [[InSAR]] images derived from [[Satellite data collection|satellite data]], showed accelerated land uplift centred near the Blue Lagoon, approximately {{convert|1.5|km|abbr=unit}} north-west of Þorbjörn. It was the fifth occurrence of such uplift in the area since 2020, suggesting increased pressure likely due to magma intrusion. The rapid uplift and associated fracturing raised the likelihood of renewed seismic activity in the area that could potentially allow magma to move closer to the Earth's surface. [[Geological modelling|Geophysical models]] were used to estimate the depth and size of the intrusion, although experts emphasised that the unrest might still subside without producing a volcanic eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |date=28 October 2023 |title=Landris hafið nærri Svartsengi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/10/28/landris_hafid_naerri_svartsengi/ |access-date=13 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240313032836/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/10/28/landris_hafid_naerri_svartsengi/|archive-date=13 March 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Karlsson |first=Ari Páll |date=28 October 2023 |title=Landris mælist nærri Bláa lóninu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-10-28-landris-maelist-naerri-blaa-loninu-395225/ |access-date=13 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ćirić |first=Jelena |date=30 October 2023 |title=Land Rising Faster on Reykjanes than Before Past Eruptions |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/nature-travel/land-rising-faster-on-reykjanes-than-before-past-eruptions/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223846/https://www.icelandreview.com/nature-travel/land-rising-faster-on-reykjanes-than-before-past-eruptions/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en-US}}</ref>

=== November 2023 earthquakes ===
In November 2023, seismic activity around the mountain Þorbjörn remained intense, with over 800 earthquakes recorded on 1 November, including a magnitude 3.7 quake. GPS and satellite data confirmed ongoing uplift due to a [[Igneous intrusion|magma intrusion]] at a depth of {{convert|4–5|km|abbr=unit}} north-west of Mt. Þorbjörn in the Svartsengi area. On 2 November, approximately 400 earthquakes were recorded, with the largest being magnitude 2.8, continuing the seismic trend. By 3 November, a magnitude 4.3 earthquake occurred, and the area continued to experience significant seismic activity, though there were no clear signs of magma moving closer to the surface. Seismicity dropped on 4 November, but uplift continued, with rockfall risks from larger earthquakes. On 6 November, 1,300 earthquakes were detected, with uplift reaching {{convert|7|cm|abbr=unit}} since the start of the inflation, and magma inflow into a [[Layered intrusion|sill-type intrusion]] at around {{convert|5|km|abbr=unit}} depth estimated at {{convert|7|m3/s|abbr=unit}}. Seismic activity remained episodic, with another 900 earthquakes on 7 November. On 8 November, approximately 1,200 earthquakes were recorded, and magma accumulation persisted at {{convert|5|km|abbr=unit}} depth north-west of Þorbjörn with no signs of surface eruption. On 9 November, seismic activity spiked, with 1,400 earthquakes and seven above magnitude 4.0, including a magnitude 4.8. Nearly 800 earthquakes occurred on 10 November, continuing the pattern of magma accumulation and seismic release, but IMO reported at that stage no clear indications that magma was forcing its way to the surface.<ref name=":0" />

Around 15:00 UTC on 10 November 2023, a significant wave of seismic activity began approximately {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} north-east of Grindavík. As a result, the civil protection phase was raised from Uncertainty Phase to Alert Phase later that afternoon. By 23:00 UTC, the Icelandic Meteorological Office (IMO) assessed that a magma intrusion had likely extended beneath the town. This event resulted from a substantial magmatic intrusion that spanned {{convert|15|km|abbr=unit}}, with its shallowest part less than about {{convert|1|km|abbr=unit}} beneath the surface, and was likely fed by magma from a reservoir beneath Svartsengi.<ref name=":78" /><ref name=":79" /> A [[state of emergency]] was declared shortly thereafter, and the evacuation of Grindavík commenced.<ref name=":0" /> The largest of the earthquakes to date reached magnitude 5.3 on 10 November 2023.<ref name=":81">{{Cite web |date=10 November 2023 |title=M 5.3 - 3 km SSE of Vogar, Iceland |url=https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eventpage/us7000la6d/executive |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111015508/https://earthquake.usgs.gov/earthquakes/eventpage/us7000la69/executive |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=26 November 2023 |website=[[United States Geological Survey]] |publisher= |language=en-US}}</ref> By this time, over 22,000 earthquakes had been recorded since the beginning of the swarm in October 2023.<ref name="Guardian-2023-11-10">{{cite news |last=Bryant |first=Miranda |date=9 November 2023 |title=Geothermal spa closes in Iceland as guests flee after series of earthquakes |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/09/iceland-blue-lagoon-geothermal-resort-closes-seismic-storm-volcanic-eruption-fears |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231113190152/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/09/iceland-blue-lagoon-geothermal-resort-closes-seismic-storm-volcanic-eruption-fears |archive-date=13 November 2023 |access-date=13 November 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> The Icelandic Meteorological Office (IMO) predicted that an eruption was likely, stating that "it will take several days (rather than hours) for [[magma]] to reach the surface". The greatest extent of the magma intrusion was inferred to be around the [[Sundhnúksgígar]] crater chain, approximately {{convert|3.5|km|abbr=unit}} north of Grindavík. A [[Differential optical absorption spectroscopy|DOAS]] [[spectrometer]] on Húsafell {{IPA|is|ˈhuːsaˌfɛtl̥|}} detected the presence of [[sulfur dioxide]] (SO<sub>2</sub>) in the atmosphere on 13–14 November, indicating that magma was several hundred metres (a few thousand feet) below the surface. Although the number of earthquakes decreased somewhat since 10 November, the IMO was still recording between 700 and 1,000 earthquakes daily by 14 November.<ref name=":0" />

Ground deformation sensors at Festarfjall {{IPA|is|ˈfɛs.tar.fjatl̥|}} and the Svartsengi area recorded that the ground had moved apart by {{convert|120|cm|abbr=unit}}.<ref name="RUV-2023-11-10">{{cite news |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=10 November 2023 |title=Magma intrusion under Grindavík: eruption likely – LIVE |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2023-11-10-magma-intrusion-under-grindavik-eruption-likely-live-396249 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231113083455/https://www.ruv.is/english/2023-11-10-magma-intrusion-under-grindavik-eruption-likely-live-396249 |archive-date=13 November 2023 |access-date=13 November 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref> Satellite measurements recorded the subsidence depth of about {{convert|1|m|abbr=unit}} over a swathe of land approximately 5 km long and 2 km wide ({{convert|5 by 2|km|abbr=unit}}), extending from the Sundhnúksgígar craters to the western side of [[Grindavík]]. The creation of this [[graben]]-like formation on 10 November has enabled scientists to estimate the volume of the magmatic intrusion, which formed in a remarkably short period of six hours, as approximately {{convert|70|e6m3|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 November 2023 |title=Skýr merki um sigdalinn á gervitunglamyndum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/13/skyr_merki_um_sigdalinn_a_gervitunglamyndum/ |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240326220609/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/13/skyr_merki_um_sigdalinn_a_gervitunglamyndum/|archive-date=26 March 2024}}</ref> Further analysis revealed that about {{convert|7000|m3/s|abbr=unit}} flowed into this magma intrusion, providing valuable insights into the dynamics of volcanic processes and the scale of subsurface magma movement.<ref name="Visir-2023-11-13">{{cite news |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |date=13 November 2023 |title=Nýjar myndir staðfesta sig upp á allt að einn metra |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232488670d/nyjar-myndir-stad-festa-sig-upp-a-allt-ad-einn-metra |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231113183411/https://www.visir.is/g/20232488670d/nyjar-myndir-stad-festa-sig-upp-a-allt-ad-einn-metra |archive-date=13 November 2023 |access-date=13 November 2023 |work=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrafnkelsdóttir |first=Linda H. Blöndal |date=8 February 2024 |title=Eitt virtasta vísindatímarit heims fjallar um ofurstreymi kviku undir Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404492 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sigmundsson |first1=Freysteinn |last2=Parks |first2=Michelle |last3=Geirsson |first3=Halldór |last4=Hooper |first4=Andrew |last5=Drouin |first5=Vincent |last6=Vogfjörd |first6=Kristín S. |last7=Ófeigsson |first7=Benedikt G. |last8=Greiner |first8=Sonja H. M. |last9=Yang |first9=Yilin |last10=Lanzi |first10=Chiara |last11=De Pascale |first11=Gregory P. |last12=Jónsdóttir |first12=Kristín |last13=Hreinsdóttir |first13=Sigrún |last14=Tolpekin |first14=Valentyn |last15=Friðriksdóttir |first15=Hildur María |last16=Einarsson |first16=Páll |last17=Barsotti |first17=Sara |title=Fracturing and tectonic stress drive ultrarapid magma flow into dikes |journal=Science |date=15 March 2024 |volume=383 |issue=6688 |pages=1228–1235 |doi=10.1126/science.adn2838 |pmid=38330140 |bibcode=2024Sci...383.1228S |hdl=20.500.11815/5296 |hdl-access=free }}</ref> It was estimated that the subsidence continued at a daily rate of about {{convert|4|cm|abbr=unit}} in the week following the disaster.<ref name="bbc">{{cite news |last=Parker |first=Jessica |date=16 November 2023 |title=Iceland volcano: Peninsula faces 'decades' of instability |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67429872 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231116024123/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67429872 |archive-date=16 November 2023 |access-date=14 November 2023 |publisher=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> A large crack opened up through the town, which old maps indicated was a reactivation of an existing fault. Scientists at the [[University of Iceland]] believe that the fault was created by the last Sundhnúksgígar eruption over 2,000 years ago.<ref name="RUV-2023-11-13">{{cite news |last1=Guðmundsson |first1=Brynjólfur Þór |last2=Jónsson |first2=Þorgils |last3=Guðmundsdóttir Gröndal |first3=Valgerður Gréta |last4=Kristjánsson |first4=Alexander |last5=Hannesdóttir Diego |first5=Hugrún |date=13 November 2023 |title=Óbreytt staða eftir nóttina |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-13-obreytt-stada-eftir-nottina-396811 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231114075842/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-13-obreytt-stada-eftir-nottina-396811 |archive-date=14 November 2023 |access-date=14 November 2023 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Grindavik area annotated.jpg|right|thumb|Annotated view of area involved in 2023 seismic disturbances]]

Sensors emplaced in a borehole in Svartsengi detected the presence of [[sulfur dioxide]] on 16 November 2023, a classic signature of [[magma]] close to the surface.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 November 2023 |title=Brennisteinsgas mælist í Svartsengi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/16/brennisteinsgas_maelist_i_svartsengi/ |access-date=24 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240324223046/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/16/brennisteinsgas_maelist_i_svartsengi/|archive-date=24 March 2024}}</ref> This led the IMO to conclude that the area around the volcanic edifice of Hagafell {{IPA|is|ˈhaː.ɣa.fɛtl̥|}}, approximately {{convert|2|km|abbr=unit}} north of Grindavík, was at the highest level of risk. A rapid {{convert|3|cm|abbr=unit}} uplift of the ground in the Svartsengi area was recorded from 18–21 November, likely indicating an upwelling of magma from a source {{convert|5|km|mi|disp=preunit|or more |abbr=unit}} below the ground. An eruption was still regarded as likely on 21 November, but as seismic activity declined from 24 November, that likelihood also began to decline.<ref name=":0" />

In November 2023, Benedikt Ófeigsson, a geophysicist at the IMO, posited that Fagradalsfjall, rather than Svartsengi, might be the source of volcanic activity near Grindavík, noting the difficulty in assessing the activity's depth, location, and magma volume amidst seismic disturbances. He anticipated that [[InSAR]] satellite imagery would clarify these uncertainties.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=10 November 2023 |title=Kvikan mögulega úr Fagradalsfjalli en ekki Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-10-kvikan-mogulega-ur-fagradalsfjalli-en-ekki-svartsengi-396326/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Supporting Benedikt's hypothesis, Freysteinn Sigmundsson from the University of Iceland, in February 2024, observed that minor seismic activity near Fagradalsfjall indicated potential magma movement, hinting at a possible eruption. He highlighted the magma's potential new pathways, particularly its shift towards the Svartsengi system and the persistent pressure in Fagradalsfjall post-eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórólfsdóttir |first=Sonja Sif |date=20 February 2024 |title=Kvika kann að vera á hreyfingu undir Fagradalsfjalli |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/20/kvika_kann_ad_vera_a_hreyfingu_undir_fagradalsfjall/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240401223132/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/20/kvika_kann_ad_vera_a_hreyfingu_undir_fagradalsfjall/|archive-date=1 April 2024}}</ref> Additional evidence bolstering the theory came from the pattern of land uplift in these systems. In Fagradalsfjall, an uplift began following the end of volcanic activity in early August 2023 and persisted until the end of October 2023. Notably, as this intrusion concluded in Fagradalsfjall, a fresh phase of land uplift initiated in Svartsengi around the same time.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Líkur hafa aukist á að nýr kvikugangur myndist undir Fagradalsfjalli |url=https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/hrodun-a-landrisi-a-reykjanesskaga |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111055325/https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/hrodun-a-landrisi-a-reykjanesskaga |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref> Scientists from [[Uppsala University]] appeared to have confirmed the theory proposed by their counterparts at the University of Iceland.<ref name=":28" /><ref name=":19">{{Cite web |last=Jóhannsson |first=Róbert |date=12 April 2024 |title=Kvikan mögulega að koma úr sama hólfi og þegar gaus við Fagradalsfjall |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/410110 |access-date=12 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=12 April 2024 |title=The magma chamber is not as deep as previously thought |url=https://icelandmonitor.mbl.is/news/news/2024/04/12/the_magma_chamber_is_not_as_deep_as_previously_thou/ |access-date=12 April 2024 |website=[[Iceland Monitor]] |language=en}}</ref>

[[File:Sundhnúksgígar.jpg|right|thumb|Sundhnúksgígar craters seen from the south-west]]

According to Professor Magnús Tumi Guðmundsson, a geophysicist at the University of Iceland, the magma channel, approximately {{convert|2|m|abbr=unit}} wide, had undergone rapid [[Solidification point|solidification]]. Approximately 90% of the magma solidified within 10 to 15 days following the subsidence in November 2023, due to the cold nature of the [[Earth's crust]]. However, some magma near [[Sundhnúksgígar]] remained, posing potential risks for further activity due to the possibility of increased underground pressure. Magnús noted that while magma generally cools quickly, the surrounding rock stays hot and weak, maintaining a state of geological vulnerability in the area for several months, necessitating ongoing caution and monitoring.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=26 November 2023 |title=Meirihluti kvikunnar í ganginum líklega storknaður |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-26-meirihluti-kvikunnar-i-ganginum-liklega-storknadur-398186/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204185646/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-26-meirihluti-kvikunnar-i-ganginum-liklega-storknadur-398186 |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> If an eruption were to occur, it was most likely to take place in the area between Sýlingarfell {{IPA|is|ˈsiːlɪŋk.ar.fɛtl̥|}} and Hagafell mountains.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnúsdóttir |first=Ásta Hlín |date=28 November 2023 |title=Gos líklegast á milli Sýlingarfells og Hagafells |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-28-gos-liklegast-a-milli-sylingarfells-og-hagafells-398290/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223846/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-28-gos-liklegast-a-milli-sylingarfells-og-hagafells-398290 |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Over the span of several weeks, the possibility of an eruption in Eldvörp {{IPA|is|ˈɛlt.vœr̥p|}}, north-east of Grindavík, was also being monitored.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórólfsdóttir |first=Sonja Sif |date=1 November 2023 |title=Virknin færist yfir í Eldvörp |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/01/virknin_faerist_yfir_i_eldvorp/ |access-date=29 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429175738/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/01/virknin_faerist_yfir_i_eldvorp/|archive-date=29 April 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Helgadóttir |first=Ragnhildur |date=12 November 2023 |title=Telur gos líklegra í Eldvörpum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/12/telur_gos_liklegra_i_eldvorpum/ |access-date=29 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240429174232/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/12/telur_gos_liklegra_i_eldvorpum/|archive-date=29 April 2024}}</ref>

Significant land movements were recorded around the Svartsengi area during the subsidence, with a displacement of about {{convert|100|cm|abbr=unit}} to the west and {{convert|25|cm|abbr=unit}} to the north. During the evacuation of Grindavík on 10 November, the ground near Svartsengi initially descended by {{convert|35|cm|abbr=unit}} and subsequently rose by {{convert|25|cm|abbr=unit}}. A [[GPS receiver|GPS station]] at Festarfjall, situated east of Grindavík, underwent a shift of {{convert|60|cm|abbr=unit}} eastward and {{convert|40|cm|abbr=unit}} southward, along with an upward movement of {{convert|12|cm|abbr=unit}}. On the same day, the GPS station in Grindavík, located directly above the magma conduit, moved {{convert|30|cm|abbr=unit}} eastward while dropping {{convert|100|cm|abbr=unit}}, with an additional sinking of {{convert|20|cm|abbr=unit}} occurring in the following days.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sævarsson |first=Sigurður Bogi |date=3 December 2023 |title=Svartsengi færðist til um heilan metra |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/03/svartsengi_faerdist_til_um_heilan_metra/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204013743/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/03/svartsengi_faerdist_til_um_heilan_metra/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== December 2023 earthquakes ===
In December 2023, seismic activity around the Svartsengi area had remained weak, with [[Microearthquake|micro-earthquakes]] concentrated near Hagafell. By 6 December, [[geodetic]] [[Scientific modelling|modelling]] suggested that magma inflow to the dike formed on 10 November 2023 had likely ceased, significantly decreasing the chances of an eruption along that dike. However, magma accumulation beneath Svartsengi continued, keeping the possibility of further dikes or an eruption alive. Inflation in Svartsengi persisted, though at a slightly reduced rate by 13 December 2023, still higher than before the November dike. Seismic activity remained low, with weak activity mostly around Hagafell. On 15 December 460 earthquakes were recorded, with 30 greater than magnitude 1.0, and the largest being magnitude 2.8 near Hagafell. Uplift around Svartsengi continued, indicating ongoing magma accumulation. A [[hazard map]] from 6 December remained valid until 20 December, but conditions were subject to rapid change. As of 16 December, it was too early to say whether magma accumulation at Svartsengi had stopped and the inflation was over, and scientists would continue analysing data, with a new hazard map scheduled for 20 December.<ref name=":0" />

[[File:View from Þorbjörn, Iceland, 20230430 1521 3650.jpg|right|thumb|Hagafell (''left foreground''), viewed from Þorbjörn]]

It was determined that the magma accumulating beneath Svartsengi was most likely feeding the dike beneath Grindavík, oriented along the approximately {{convert|15|km|abbr=unit}} Sundhnúksgígar crater chain, where the magma intrusion occurred. Deformations are still being measured at stations near the dike, but these are now mainly interpreted as due to the land uplift occurring at Svartsengi.<ref name=":0" /> At the beginning of December 2023, recent GPS data from the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO) showed that the land had risen above its level prior to the start of the earthquake series at the end of October 2023. Professor Þorvaldur Þórðarson, a volcanology expert from the University of Iceland, theorised that this elevation could be attributed to a combination of [[Divergent boundary|tectonic movements]] and magma build-up. Notably, the pronounced terrestrial shifts on 10 November 2023 may have facilitated the migration of magma from deeper reservoirs to more superficial ones.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 December 2023 |title=Landið nú risið hærra en fyrir skjálftahrinuna |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/03/landid_nu_risid_haerra_en_fyrir_skjalftahrinuna/ |url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204014030/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/03/landid_nu_risid_haerra_en_fyrir_skjalftahrinuna/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Svartsengi GPS Station.jpg|thumb|A graph from the GPS station in Svartsengi showing vertical ground movement in millimetres between October 2023 and October 2024]]

In the weeks following the [[subsidence]], the volcanic activity at Svartsengi entered a new phase with increased chances of magma propagation and potential eruptions. The observed pattern suggested a possible repetition of events, where magma accumulating beneath Svartsengi feeds a dike beneath Grindavík and north-east towards Sundhnúksgígar, similar to the one formed on 10 November. At the beginning of December 2023, magma was estimated to be accumulating in the chamber under Svartsengi at a rate comparable to the discharge of the [[Elliðaár]] rivers in Reykjavík, or on the order of several cubic m/s (a few hundred cubic ft/s). The magma was believed to be located at a depth of about {{convert|5–6|km|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæmundsdóttir |first=Sunna |date=4 December 2023 |title=Kvika virðist hætt að streyma í kvikuganginn |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232498441d/kvika-virdist-haett-ad-streyma-i-kvikuganginn |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=6 December 2023 |title=Telja minna magn kviku þurfa núna |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/06/telja_minna_magn_kviku_thurfa_nuna/ |url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231207032809/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/06/telja_minna_magn_kviku_thurfa_nuna/ |archive-date=7 December 2023 |access-date=6 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The rate of magma inflow from the [[Earth's mantle]] into the [[magma chamber]] beneath Svartsengi has steadily but gradually decreased since the beginning of the eruptive series and is currently estimated at approximately {{convert|1|m3/s|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":89" />

The recent seismic trends at Svartsengi, characterised by varying intensities and a concentration of activity along a dike, closely resembled the seismic and volcanic patterns experienced during the [[Krafla|Krafla Fires]], which began in 1975.<ref name=":73" /><ref name=":74">{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=23 March 2025 |title=Eldarnir byrjunin á löngu gosskeiði á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-03-23-eldarnir-byrjunin-a-longu-gosskeidi-a-reykjanesskaga-439587 |access-date=23 March 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> During this prolonged eruptive series, spanning nearly nine years, the region experienced 20 distinct magma intrusions, nine of which resulted in eruptions.<ref name=":9" /> These events were marked by varying magma volumes feeding the same dike, leading to different eruption scales. In Svartsengi, [[Geodetic datum|geodetic data]] showed a similar pattern of reduced magma volumes compared to levels observed before the 10 November 2023 dike intrusion, parallelling the smaller magma accumulations at Krafla that were sufficient to trigger new propagations and subsequent eruptions.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnardóttir |first=Lovísa |date=6 December 2023 |title=Minni líkur á eldgosi en fleiri kvikuhlaup enn líkleg |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232499676d/minni-likur-a-eld-gosi-en-fleiri-kviku-hlaup-enn-lik-leg |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231216143454/https://www.visir.is/g/20232499676d/minni-likur-a-eld-gosi-en-fleiri-kviku-hlaup-enn-lik-leg |archive-date=16 December 2023 |access-date=6 December 2023 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

From the beginning of the earthquakes until 10 November, the land at Svartsengi elevated by more than {{convert|10|cm|abbr=unit}} within a span of 16 days. Following a substantial subsidence of {{convert|35|cm|abbr=unit}}, a new phase of activity led to a land rise of {{convert|20|cm|abbr=unit}} over an equivalent 16-day period. This phase reached a peak height of about {{convert|35|cm|abbr=unit}} before the first eruption on 18 December 2023, matching the height recorded before the subsidence.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Halldórsson |first=Skúli |date=8 December 2023 |title=Nærri 30 sentimetrar á mánuði |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/08/naerri_30_sentimetrar_a_manudi/ |url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231210015610/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/08/naerri_30_sentimetrar_a_manudi/ |archive-date=10 December 2023 |access-date=10 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=15 December 2023 |title=Landris hefur "svo gott sem stöðvast" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232504051d/land-ris-hefur-svo-gott-sem-stodvast- |access-date=27 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Typically, the land undergoes a sharp descent before each intrusion and eruption, followed by a gradual ascent thereafter.<ref name=":0" /> The subsidence in the area was caused by an underground movement of magma from Svartsengi, passing beneath Grindavík, and extending towards [[Sundhnúksgígar]] to the north-east.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Magnús Jochum |date=12 February 2024 |title=Landris hafið á ný undir Svartsengi |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242528184d/land-ris-hafid-a-ny-undir-svarts-engi |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== March 2024 earthquakes ===
An earthquake swarm on 2 March 2024 was interpreted as a magma intrusion that did not reach the surface. [[Simulation modeling|Modeling calculations]] indicated that the magma intrusion was approximately {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} long, extending from Stóra-Skógfell {{IPA|is|ˈstou.ra-ˌskouɣ.fɛtl̥|}} to Hagafell. The magma within this intrusion lay at a depth of {{convert|1.2|km|abbr=unit}} at its shallowest point, reaching down to about {{convert|3.9|km|abbr=unit}}. The calculations estimated that approximately {{convert|1.3|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma flowed into [[Sundhnúksgígar]] during the magma transfer, significantly less than previous events where it was estimated that around {{convert|10|e6m3|abbr=unit}} had flowed from Svartsengi into the craters. Before the intrusion, about {{convert|8|e6m3|abbr=unit}} had accumulated in the chamber under Svartsengi.<ref name=":89" /><ref name=":0" /> Magma typically seeks the path of least resistance to the surface, and it is challenging to ascertain what prevented this during the event. Potential obstacles in the magma's path, insufficient volume, or pressure to open a [[fissure vent]], or a combination of these factors could have played a role. The magma intrusion behaved differently from previous transfers, warranting further investigation to enhance understanding of the nature of such events in the area and to anticipate future developments.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |date=8 March 2024 |title=5 kvikuhlaup og 3 eldgos á Sundhnúksgígaröðinni |url=https://www.vedur.is/um-vi/frettir/5-kvikuhlaup-og-3-eldgos-a-sundhnuksgigarodinni |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== April 2025 earthquakes ===
Post-eruption analysis of the 1 April 2025 event, utilising satellite remote sensing data, identified the development of an extensive magmatic dike associated with the eruption. The dike's northern terminus was observed to extend approximately {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} beyond [[Keilir]] {{IPA|is|ˈcʰeiːlɪr̥|}}, while geophysical data indicated shallow subsurface magma storage at a minimum depth of {{convert|1.5|km|abbr=unit}} beneath the surface. A new [[graben]] (subsidence valley) formed north-east of Litla-Skógfell {{IPA|is|ˈlɪtl̥a-ˌskouɣ.fɛtl̥|}} due to faulting above the dike. Svartsengi experienced over {{convert|25|cm|abbr=unit}} of subsidence as magma drained into the dike, while GPS stations in [[Grindavík]] recorded up to {{convert|50|cm|abbr=unit}} of fault displacement, though less severe than the graben formed during the January 2024 eruption. By 4 April, seismicity along the dike had fallen from roughly 100 small earthquakes per hour at its peak to about 20–30 per hour, although low‑level [[Microseism|microseismic]] activity persisted. During the early phase of the event, seismicity clustered at the north-eastern terminus of the intrusion, indicating the potential for magma to breach the surface there. However, as pressure declined over time, the likelihood of surface breakthrough diminished.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 April 2025 |title=Aðeins 1,5 kílómetrar niður á kvikuganginn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/04/03/adeins_1_5_kilometrar_nidur_a_kvikuganginn/ |access-date=23 April 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> A swarm of six earthquakes, each exceeding a magnitude of 3.0, occurred near [[Krýsuvík (volcanic system)|Trölladyngja]] {{IPA|is|ˈtrœtl̥a.tiːŋcja|}} and has been attributed to stress adjustments associated with the intrusion formation. In the April event, the largest earthquake reached a magnitude of 5.3 on the south-westernmost part of the [[Reykjanes Peninsula]] (part of stress adjustments), while the most significant quake in populated areas registered a magnitude of 3.9.<ref name=":77">{{Cite web |date=1 April 2025 |title=New magma instrusion on Sundhnúkur crater row |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/new-magma-instrusion-on-sundhnukur-crater-row |access-date=3 April 2025 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Magnús Jochum |date=6 April 2025 |title=Áfram landris og skjálftar á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252711027d/afram-landris-og-skjalftar-a-reykjanesskaga |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

A few days after the event, GPS data showed renewed uplift beneath Svartsengi, indicating that magma recharge had resumed as it did following earlier eruptions in the series, but at a faster rate than before. Initially, scientists interpreted the evolving GPS displacement chart, along with other indicators, to suggest that magma inflow had stalled. However, the ongoing land uplift has challenged this view and underscored the uncertainty of long-term accumulation trends. Owing to its size, the seismic event has been compared with the magma intrusion of November 2023. Although it extended about {{convert|5|km|abbr=unit}} longer—reaching a total length of {{convert|20|km|abbr=unit}}—it was less than half as voluminous, with approximately {{convert|30|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma released from the Svartsengi chamber. The vast majority of the magma did not reach the surface. Measured by [[line of sight]], the intrusion's north-eastern terminus lay roughly {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} south of [[Route 41 (Iceland)|Route 41]] (Reykjanesbraut) and about {{convert|12|km|abbr=unit}} west of the Capital Region boundary. Following the cessation of both the eruption and intrusion, the six pre-existing hazard zones in and around the [[Sundhnúkur]] area were gradually downgraded—from an initial highest classification of "very high hazard" to "considerable hazard".<ref name=":77" /><ref name=":80">{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=1 April 2025 |title=Gosið í dag bara smá leki úr kvikuganginum |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252709011d/gosid-i-dag-bara-sma-leki-ur-kvikuganginum |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Unnarsson |first=Kristján Már |date=7 April 2025 |title=Kvikugangur frá Krýsuvík gæti náð inn í Heiðmörk |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252711743d/kvikugangur-fra-krysu-vik-gaeti-nad-inn-i-heid-mork |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

== Eruptions ==
=== December 2023 eruption ===
[[File:2023 Grindavik eruption from the air.jpg|thumb|281x281px|The 18 December 2023 eruption as photographed from a helicopter of the Icelandic Coast Guard. At the right in the background, Grindavík, Iceland, is visible]]

On 18 December 2023, the first eruption in the series occurred at approximately 22:17 UTC, following a series of small earthquakes, with initial indicators emerging about 90 minutes earlier.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=19 December 2023 |title=In pictures: Volcano erupts south of Iceland capital Reykjavik |url=https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20231219-iceland-volcano-erupts-south-of-the-capital-reykjavik-following-earthquake-swarm |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219005329/https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20231219-iceland-volcano-erupts-south-of-the-capital-reykjavik-following-earthquake-swarm |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref> The [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO) pinpointed the origin of the eruption near Hagafell,<ref name="cnnerupt">{{Cite web |last1=McCluskey |first1=Mitchell |last2=Ward |first2=Taylor |last3=Yeung |first3=Jessie |date=19 December 2023 |title=Volcano erupts on Iceland's Reykjanes peninsula weeks after town evacuated |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2023/12/18/europe/reykjanes-volcano-erupts-iceland/index.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219003915/https://edition.cnn.com/2023/12/18/europe/reykjanes-volcano-erupts-iceland/index.html |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en-US}}</ref> about {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} north-east of Grindavík, and noted that the eruption stemmed from a fissure with a length of up to {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}}, with lava flowing at a rate of around {{convert|100–300|m3/s|abbr=unit}}<ref name=":26">{{Cite web |last1=Jónsson |first1=Kristján |last2=Hilmarsson |first2=Guðmundur |date=13 February 2024 |title=Hraðasta hraunflæðið til þessa |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/13/hradasta_hraunflaedid_til_thessa/ |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> adding that seismic activity appeared to be moving towards the direction of Grindavík.<ref name="bbcerupt" /> The eruptive fissure followed approximately the line of the [[Sundhnúksgígar]] crater row formed from previous eruptions more than 2,000 years ago.<ref name="Sundhnukur crater row">{{Cite web |date=16 April 2024 |title=One month since the start of the eruption at the Sundhnúkur crater row |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/one-month-since-the-start-of-the-eruption-at-the-sundhnukur-crater-row |access-date=16 April 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref> An Icelandic Civil Defence official informed the nation's public broadcaster, [[RÚV]], that the eruption occurred rapidly and appeared to be "quite a large event".<ref name="bbcerupt" /> The eruption was characterised at the time as the largest in the [[Southern Peninsula (Iceland)|Reykjanes Peninsula]] since the beginning of [[Fagradalsfjall#2019 to 2021 activity and eruptions|eruptive activity in 2021]],<ref name="cnnerupt" /> with [[lava fountain]]s, up to {{convert|100|m|abbr=unit}} high,<ref name="nyt-20231218">{{Cite news |last=Moses |first=Claire |date=18 December 2023 |title=After Weeks of Warnings, Iceland Volcano Erupts in Plumes of Fire |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/18/world/europe/iceland-volcano-eruption-earthquakes.html |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240112165931/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/12/18/world/europe/iceland-volcano-eruption-earthquakes.html |archive-date=12 January 2024 |access-date=19 December 2023 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US }}</ref> and was visible as far away as the capital [[Reykjavík]], {{convert|42|km|abbr=unit}} away.<ref name="bbcerupt">{{Cite web |last1=Bettiza |first1=Sofia |last2=Slow |first2=Oliver |last3=Moloney |first3=Marita |date=20 December 2023 |title=Iceland volcano: Pollution warning for capital after eruption |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67756413 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231218235252/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67756413 |archive-date=18 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref>

Scientists estimated through [[Scientific modelling|model calculations]] that nearly {{convert|20|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma accumulated in the [[Magma chamber|chamber]], located several kilometres (a few miles) deep, before the December 2023 eruption.<ref name=":0" /> Of this, approximately {{convert|12|e6m3|abbr=unit}} ascended to the [[Igneous intrusion|intrusion]], initiating the eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir Gröndal |first=Valgerður Gréta |date=27 December 2023 |title=Líkur á næsta eldgosi aukast með hverjum deginum |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-27-likur-a-naesta-eldgosi-aukast-med-hverjum-deginum-400766/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":25">{{Cite web |last=Harðarson |first=Birgir Þór |date=29 May 2024 |title=Eldgosin á Reykjanesskaga borin saman |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/414005 |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The lava field covered a total area of approximately {{convert|3.4|km2|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":42">{{Cite web |last1=Guðmundsson |first1=Magnús Tumi |last2=Þorsteinsson |first2=Jón Gunnar |date=9 September 2024 |title=Hvað hefur gosið oft á Reykjanesskaga síðan 2021 og hversu stór hafa gosin verið? |url=https://www.visindavefur.is/svar.php?id=87070 |access-date=9 October 2024 |website=Vísindavefurinn |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":27">{{Cite web |date=21 February 2024 |title=Hraunið fór hratt yfir í febrúar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/21/hraunid_for_hratt_yfir_i_februar/ |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> On a single day during the eruption, the release of [[sulfur dioxide]] was approximately {{convert|30000–60000|t|abbr=unit}}, depending on the timeline. This sulfur dioxide subsequently reacted with [[Water vapor|water vapour]] in the atmosphere to form even more toxic [[sulfuric acid]] (H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>). The emission was proportionally about ten times greater than the gas output observed in previous eruptions at Fagradalsfjall.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=19 December 2023 |title="Öðruvísi skepna en við erum búin að horfa á" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/odruvisi_skepna_en_vid_erum_buin_ad_horfa_a/ |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

Following the eruption's onset, the [[Icelandic Coast Guard]] deployed a helicopter to the area to monitor activity.<ref name="cnnerupt" /> Numerous delays were reported at [[Keflavík International Airport]], which remained open. The Blue Lagoon spa, which had reopened the day before, did not take bookings on the night of the eruption.<ref name="guardian">{{Cite web |last1=Bryant |first1=Miranda |last2=Livingstone |first2=Helen |date=19 December 2023 |title=Iceland volcano: eruption begins on Reykjanes peninsula after weeks of activity |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/19/iceland-volcano-eruption-reykjanes-peninsula-grindavik-blue-lagoon |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219000625/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/dec/19/iceland-volcano-eruption-reykjanes-peninsula-grindavik-blue-lagoon |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> Police raised the risk level while the Civil Defence authorities warned the public not to approach the area while emergency personnel assessed the situation.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 December 2023 |title=Iceland volcano erupts weeks after thousands evacuated from nearby town |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/12/19/iceland-volcano-erupts-weeks-after-thousands-evacuated-from-nearby-town |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219021435/http://www.aljazeera.com/news/2023/12/19/iceland-volcano-erupts-weeks-after-thousands-evacuated-from-nearby-town |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] |language=en}}</ref> By 19 December, the scent of smoke and ash was detected as far as {{convert|30|km|abbr=unit}} from the eruption site, raising fears that [[volcanic gas]]es could reach Reykjavík by the next day.<ref name="bbcerupt" /> However, the gas would never extend to dangerous levels there; it could only cause mild discomfort at most.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=19 December 2023 |title=Gosmengun á höfuðborgarsvæðinu í nótt |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232505486d/gosmengun-a-hofud-borgar-svaedinu-i-nott |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> On the same day, the Icelandic Coast Guard rescued a man who had gotten lost near the eruption site using a helicopter.<ref>{{cite news |date=20 December 2023 |title=The Coast Guard helicopter rescued a man lost at the eruption site |url=https://icelandmonitor.mbl.is/news/news/2023/12/20/the_coast_guard_helicopter_rescued_a_man_lost_at_th/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231221015931/https://icelandmonitor.mbl.is/news/news/2023/12/20/the_coast_guard_helicopter_rescued_a_man_lost_at_th/ |archive-date=21 December 2023 |access-date=23 December 2023 |work=[[Iceland Monitor]] |language=en}}</ref> Authorities advised citizens who were in the town during the eruption to evacuate via Route 427 (Suðurstrandarvegur), as the eruption was most likely to threaten Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur).<ref>{{Cite web |date=18 December 2023 |title=Eldgosið "á kórréttum stað" en kemur upp á "verstu sprungu sem gat gosið á" |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/20125/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=Heimildin |language=is}}</ref> Officials said residents of Grindavík would be allowed back into the town.<ref name="Trujillo21D">{{Cite news |last1=Trujillo |first1=Marco |last2=Djurica |first2=Marko |last3=Gronholt-Pedersen |first3=Jacob |last4=Birkebaek |first4=Johannes |date=21 December 2023 |editor-last=Heritage |editor-first=Timothy |title=Residents visit evacuated Icelandic town for first time since volcanic eruption |url=https://www.reuters.com/business/environment/icelandic-volcano-appears-have-died-down-scientists-say-2023-12-21/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://archive.today/20231221133539/https://www.reuters.com/business/environment/icelandic-volcano-appears-have-died-down-scientists-say-2023-12-21/ |archive-date=21 December 2023 |access-date=21 December 2023 |work=[[Reuters]] |language=en}}</ref> The December 2023 eruption, the August–September 2024 eruption, and the July–August 2025 eruption were the only events in the series that caused no damage to infrastructure.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir Gröndal |first=Valgerður Gréta |date=19 December 2023 |title=Innviðir eru ekki í hættu eins og er |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-19-innvidir-eru-ekki-i-haettu-eins-og-er-400163 |access-date=13 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> On 21 December, the IMO officially declared the eruption concluded after a significant decrease in activity.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":53">{{Cite web |last1=Tómasson |first1=Telma |last2=Ísleifsson |first2=Atli |date=21 December 2023 |title=Gosinu við Sundhnúksgíga lokið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232506477d/gosinu-vid-sund-hnuks-giga-lokid |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== January 2024 eruption ===
[[File:Grindavik and the eruption 14 januar 2024.jpg|thumb|281x281px|Aerial photograph of the eruption on 14 January 2024]]

On 14 January 2024, at approximately 07:57 UTC, a second volcanic eruption began about {{convert|400–500|metres|abbr=unit}} north of Grindavík,<ref name="ap-20240114">{{Cite news |last=Di Marco |first=Marco |date=14 January 2024 |title=A volcano erupts in southwestern Iceland, sending lava flowing toward a nearby settlement |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-a7337b61f1df7184d52406416f0a67bf |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117030911/https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-a7337b61f1df7184d52406416f0a67bf |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en-US}}</ref> following seismic activity originating from the Sundhnúksgígar crater about 5 hours earlier.<ref name=":0" /><ref name="abc-20240114">{{Cite news |last=Shalvey |first=Kevin |date=14 January 2024 |title=2nd volcanic fissure opens 'just outside' evacuated Icelandic town, Met office says |url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/volcano-erupts-southwest-iceland/story?id=106360995 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117055517/https://abcnews.go.com/International/volcano-erupts-southwest-iceland/story?id=106360995 |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Although the eruption was described as "not very big",<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigþórsson |first=Atli |date=14 January 2024 |title=Eldgos byrjað við Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/audskilid/402035 |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> fissures appeared on both sides of the anti-lava defences,<ref name="guardian-20240114">{{Cite news |last=Bryant |first=Miranda |date=14 January 2024 |title=Houses set alight as lava from volcano eruption reaches Icelandic town |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/14/volcano-erupts-near-reykjavik-forcing-evacuation-of-residents |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117092933/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/jan/14/volcano-erupts-near-reykjavik-forcing-evacuation-of-residents |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> and it was estimated that lava could reach the town within 24 hours. A successful mission was launched to save several pieces of [[Heavy equipment|heavy machinery]] worth about ISK 800 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|800000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}), which were used to construct the barriers.<ref name="ruv-20240114">{{Cite news |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=14 January 2024 |title=Grindavík eruptions |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2024-01-14-an-eruption-has-begun-north-of-grindavik-401887 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117055448/https://www.ruv.is/english/2024-01-14-grindavik-eruptions-401887 |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Ólafur |date=15 January 2024 |title=Tækin sem björguðust um 800 milljóna króna virði |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/15/taekin_sem_bjorgudust_um_800_milljona_krona_virdi/ |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The lava flow cut off one of the main roads leading into Grindavík,<ref name="bbc14jan">{{Cite news |last=Comerford |first=Ruth |date=15 January 2024 |title=Iceland lava slowing down after day of destruction |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67977591 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117020454/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67977591 |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=15 January 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> and the initial fissure extended up to {{convert|900|metres|abbr=unit}}.<ref name="aj14jan">{{Cite news |date=15 January 2024 |title=Volcano erupts in Iceland, sending lava into fishing town |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/1/15/volcano-erupts-in-iceland-sending-lava-into-fishing-town |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240116201317/https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/1/15/volcano-erupts-in-iceland-sending-lava-into-fishing-town |archive-date=16 January 2024 |access-date=15 January 2024 |work=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] |language=en}}</ref> Prior to the eruption, more than {{convert|11|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had accumulated beneath the Svartsengi area,<ref name=":89" /> with only a small portion of that having been emitted above ground. This buildup likely contributed to the formation of a new [[graben]] valley in the area.<ref name=":0" />

[[File:Hraun við Grindavík.jpg|thumb|Three houses in Grindavík were destroyed by a lava flow.]]

Before the eruption began, scientists observed that magma intrusion seemed to have reached beneath the town of Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2024 |title=Kvika talin vera undir Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/kvika_talin_vera_undir_grindavik/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> A few hours after the first [[fissure vent]] opened in the morning, a second vent approximately {{convert|100|metres|abbr=unit}}<ref name="aj14jan" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2024 |title=Stærri sprungan 900 metra löng |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/staerri_sprungan_900_metra_long/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> emerged around noon, just several tens of metres (a few hundred feet) from the town's outermost houses<ref name="morgun-20240114">{{Cite news |date=14 January 2024 |title=Ný gossprunga opnast við Grindavík |trans-title=A new fissure has opened near Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/ny_gossprunga_opnast_vid_grindavik/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117125044/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/ny_gossprunga_opnast_vid_grindavik/ |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> before ceasing activity a few hours later.<ref name="myndir-ur-lofti-15-02-2024">{{Cite news |date=15 January 2024 |title=Kalt mat Magnúsar Tuma: Engar góðar fréttir |trans-title=Cold assessment of Magnús Tumi: No good news |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-01-15-myndir-ur-lofti-syna-vel-eydilegginguna-eftir-gosid-402259 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240116231701/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-01-15-kalt-mat-magnusar-tuma-engar-godar-frettir-402259/ |archive-date=16 January 2024 |access-date=15 January 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Evacuation orders were reissued in Grindavík, and the Icelandic Coast Guard deployed a helicopter to monitor the situation.<ref name="cnn-20240114">{{Cite news |last1=Faqiri |first1=Shirin Zia |last2=Rios |first2=Michael |last3=Chen |first3=Heather |last4=Brown |first4=Benjamin |date=14 January 2024 |title=Buildings burn as lava from Icelandic volcano eruption flows into evacuated fishing town |url=https://edition.cnn.com/2024/01/13/europe/iceland-volcanic-evacuation-grindavik-intl-hnk/index.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117085922/https://edition.cnn.com/2024/01/13/europe/iceland-volcanic-evacuation-grindavik-intl-hnk/index.html |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[CNN]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Dr. Evgenia Ilyinskaya, a volcanologist from the [[University of Leeds]], suggested that this eruption indicated the Reykjanes Peninsula might be entering a long-term period of frequent eruptions, potentially lasting for centuries. She referred to this period as the New [[Reykjanes Fires]], drawing a parallel to a similar event in the 13th century, which marked the previous active volcanic period on the Reykjanes Peninsula.<ref name="bbc14jan" /><ref name="bbc-20240115">{{Cite news |date=15 January 2024 |editor-last=Turner |editor-first=Lauren |title=Volcanic activity near Grindavik easing after eruption - Met Office |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/live/world-europe-67973655 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117020448/https://www.bbc.com/news/live/world-europe-67973655 |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=15 January 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |pages=en-UK}}</ref>

By 15 January, volcanic activity had "decreased considerably",<ref name="france24-20240115">{{Cite news |date=15 January 2024 |title=Iceland faces 'daunting period' after long-dormant volcanic system awakens |url=https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20240115-iceland-faces-daunting-period-after-long-dormant-volcanic-system-awakens |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240116142204/https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20240115-iceland-faces-daunting-period-after-long-dormant-volcanic-system-awakens |archive-date=16 January 2024 |access-date=15 January 2024 |work=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Associated Press|AP]]}}</ref> and the eruption was declared over on 16 January 2024, just after midnight.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 January 2024 |title=Landris hafið á ný |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/16/landris_hafid_a_ny/ |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> In the aftermath of the eruption, scientists discovered a new graben valley, located further east than the one formed during the November 2023 intrusion. The new valley measured up to {{convert|1|km|abbr=unit}} wide and {{convert|30|cm|abbr=unit}} deep, in contrast to the older valley, which reached up to {{convert|2|km|abbr=unit}} in width and {{convert|1.3|m|abbr=unit}} in depth.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Þorgils |date=16 January 2024 |title=Nýi sigdalurinn allt að kílómetra breiður |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/402537 |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Subsequently, a deep [[Fissure|crack]] also opened within the town of Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Pálsson |first1=Magnús Jochum |last2=Pétursdóttir |first2=Lillý Valgerður |date=19 January 2024 |title=Ný sprunga opnaðist í Grindavík í dag og gosið formlega búið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242517845d/ny-sprunga-opnadist-i-grinda-vik-i-dag-og-gosid-form-lega-buid |access-date=18 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> This eruption was one of the smallest when it finished,<ref name=":54">{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Hallgerður Kolbrún E. |date=16 January 2024 |title=Minnsta en skæðasta eldgosið á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242515770d/minnsta-en-skaedasta-eld-gosid-a-reykja-nes-skaga |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> affecting only about {{convert|0.7|km2|abbr=unit}} and producing roughly {{convert|2|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of lava.<ref name=":42" /><ref name=":25" /><ref name=":27" /> The maximum lava discharge was {{convert|100–200|m3/s|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 January 2024 |title=Eldgos norðan Grindavíkur, niðurstöður mælinga 14. janúar 2024 - uppfært |url=https://jardvis.hi.is/is/eldgos-nordan-grindavikur-nidurstodur-maelinga-14-januar-2024-uppfaert |access-date=20 July 2024 |website=[[University of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref> It was the only eruption that actually reached the town's borders.<ref name=":0" />

=== February 2024 eruption ===
[[File:Eldgos 8. febrúar 2024 Almannavarnir.jpg|thumb|281x281px|A photograph of the eruption on 8 February 2024]]

On 8 February 2024 at 06:03 UTC, the third eruption started north of Sýlingarfell<ref name=":14">{{Cite web |last1=Gísladóttir |first1=Hólmfríður |last2=Jónsdóttir |first2=Margrét Björk |last3=Arnardóttir |first3=Lovísa |last4=Ísleifsson |first4=Atli |last5=Stefánsson |first5=Jón Þór |last6=Ragnarsson |first6=Rafn Ágúst |date=8 February 2024 |title=Vaktin: Eldgos hafið við Sundhnúksgíga |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242526548d/gos-hafid-a-reykja-nesinu |access-date=8 February 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> following intensive seismic activity that began roughly 30 minutes prior.<ref name=":0" /> The eruption produced lava fountains measuring between {{convert|50 and 80|m|abbr=unit}} in height and a [[Eruption column|volcanic plume]] up to {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} high.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 February 2024 |title=Lava erupts for third time on volcano-hit Iceland peninsula |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20240208-icelandic-volcano-erupts-for-third-time-since-december |access-date=8 February 2024 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref> A {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} long fissure opened in the same area as the December 2023 eruption, between the Sundhnúksgígar craters and the hill of Stóra-Skógfell, about {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} north-east of Grindavík. About two-thirds of the lava flowed west into the Svartsengi area, extending up to {{convert|4.5|km|abbr=unit}} from the eruption centre. The lava represented a flow of about {{convert|600|m3/s|abbr=unit}} for the first seven hours.<ref name=":26" /> Unlike previous eruptions on the peninsula, the 8 February eruption produced quantities of black ash mixed with steam, theorised to result from [[groundwater]] boiling and mixing with magma to create ash.<ref name=":14" />

Less than three hours after the eruption began, infrastructure was said to be "in no danger at the moment". However, an hour later, it was estimated that there was a risk of the lava front flowing over the main hot-water pipeline running from the [[Svartsengi power station]]. Five hours after the eruption commenced, authorities declared the situation "very serious", and by the sixth hour, the lava had already flowed over the pipeline, causing serious damage to infrastructure near the Svartsengi power station, although Grindavík remained at a safe distance.<ref name="RUV-2024-02-08">{{cite news |date=8 February 2024 |title=Hjáveitulögn rofnaði - kalt verður í híbýlum næstu daga |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-02-08-eldgos-hafid-a-reykjanesskaga-404261 |accessdate=8 February 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last=Di Marco |first=Marco |date=9 February 2024 |title=A volcano in Iceland is erupting again, spewing lava and cutting heat and hot water supplies |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-1938ec2da163f6d2f5dcdd553aea812d |accessdate=9 February 2024 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> Early on 8 February, seismic tremors forced the evacuation of every building around the Blue Lagoon, just hours before the eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=8 February 2024 |title="Þetta er upplifun lífsins!" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242526625d/-thetta-er-upp-lifun-lifsins- |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Later that day, Keflavík Airport lost its hot-water system, and an unrelated leak soon cut the cold-water line. Cold running water was restored on 9 February<ref name=":83">{{Cite web |last=Sigurðsson |first=Bjarki |date=9 February 2024 |title=Kalda vatnið flæðir aftur um Leifsstöð |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242527420d/kalda-vatnid-flaedir-aftur-um-leifs-stod |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> and hot water on 12 February.<ref name=":82" /> Although flights continued as scheduled, many toilet facilities remained closed owing to a shortage of cold running water; [[Fan heater|fan heaters]] and blankets kept passengers warm as indoor temperatures fell. The airport's electrical power supply was unaffected.<ref name="RUV-2024-02-08" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Logadóttir |first=Sunna Ósk |date=9 February 2024 |title=Hafa lokað klósettum á Keflavíkurflugvelli |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/20830/ |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=Heimildin |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=10 February 2024 |title=Heitavatnsleysi hefur ekki áhrif á flug til og frá Keflavíkurflugvelli |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/heitavatnsleysi-hefur-ekki-ahrif-a-flug-til-og-fra-keflavikurflugvelli |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":84">{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=9 February 2024 |title=Ekkert kalt vatn á Keflavíkurflugvelli og klósettum lokað |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404566 |access-date=29 May 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

Volcanic activity subsided later on eruption day and came to a complete stop in the afternoon of 9 February.<ref>{{cite web |date=9 February 2024 |title=Volcanic eruption in Iceland subsides, though scientists warn more activity may follow |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-blue-lagoon-f6f4405f9ae520d8ea637dccd2a7fa7a |accessdate=9 February 2024 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en-US}}</ref> On 10 February, the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO) formally declared the eruption over.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=10 February 2024 |title=Eldgosinu lokið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242527671d/eld-gosinu-lokid |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> As of today, this eruption has caused the most damage to infrastructure in the series, despite lasting only about one and a half days. The rapid and insidious lava flow caught response teams in the area off guard, as a series of unfortunate events allowed the molten rock to overrun the infrastructures unrestrained.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Oddur Ævar |date=8 February 2024 |title=Hraunflæðið kemur á óvart |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242526746d/hraunflaedid-kemur-a-o-vart |access-date=22 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Although about {{convert|9|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had gathered below Svartsengi just before the event,<ref name=":89" /> the IMO estimated that {{convert|13|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of lava erupted on 8 February alone.<ref name=":42" /><ref name=":25" /> The lava covered about {{convert|4|km2|abbr=unit}} after the conclusion of the eruption.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":27" />

=== March–May 2024 eruption ===
[[File:Eruption near Grindavík on March 16, 2024.png|thumb|281x281px|Aerial photograph of the eruption near Grindavík on 16 March 2024]]

On 16 March 2024, the fourth eruption began at 20:23 UTC,<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kjartansson |first1=Aðalsteinn |last2=Reynisson |first2=Jón Trausti |date=16 March 2024 |title=Myndband sýnir upphaf eldgossins |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/21261/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=Heimildin |language=is}}</ref> with weak initial signs occurring less than an hour earlier between Hagafell and Stóra-Skógfell.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":10" /> Impressive lava fountains formed in the initial hours, reaching heights of several dozen metres (a few hundred feet) in height.<ref>{{cite web |last=Martin |date=17 March 2024 |title=Reykjanes volcano update: New eruption started last night, 4th in row since October |url=https://www.volcanodiscovery.com/reykjanes/news/236819/Reykjanes-volcano-update-New-eruption-started-last-night-4th-in-row-since-October.html#google_vignette |access-date=9 April 2024 |website=Volcano Discovery |language=en}}</ref> Both the Blue Lagoon and Grindavík were evacuated.<ref name=":8">{{Cite web |date=18 March 2024 |title=Icelandic volcano erupts yet again, nearby town evacuated |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/iceland-volcano-eruption-reykjanes-nearby-town-grindavik-evacuated/ |access-date=16 March 2024 |website=[[CBS News]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |date=16 March 2024 |title=Iceland volcano in fourth eruption since December |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/av/world-europe-68587994 |access-date=16 March 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> A {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} fissure opened up,<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2024 |title=A volcano in Iceland is erupting for the fourth time in 3 months, sending plumes of lava skywards |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-reykjanes-peninsula-644e18664ef7fa6de0cfae7853c24223 |access-date=17 March 2024 |website=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en-US}}</ref> producing two lava flows going in different directions. The first was seen to be moving in a westward direction towards the Blue Lagoon and the Svartsengi power plant and reached Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) leading into Grindavík, raising concerns that it could damage [[fibre-optic cable]]s laid on the road and cause internet and [[telecommunications]] disruptions. The second lava flow was seen to be moving south, reaching the eastern protective barriers of Grindavík<ref name="bbcm16">{{Cite web |last=Armstrong |first=Kathryn |date=17 March 2024 |title=Iceland violent volcanic flare-up triggers state of emergency |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-68589594 |access-date=17 March 2024 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> at a speed of {{convert|1|km/h|4=1|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2024 |title=Iceland in state of emergency after volcano erupts, fourth time in 3 months |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/gallery/2024/3/17/iceland-in-state-of-emergency-after-volcano-erupts-fourth-time-in-3-months |access-date=17 March 2024 |website=[[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> The Icelandic Meteorological Office (IMO) said the eruption was "significantly wider" than the eruption of the previous month, while a geophysicist who observed the eruption during a helicopter flight described the eruption as the "most powerful" so far of the current eruption sequence. A [[state of emergency]] was once more declared in the region on the day of the eruption, as had been done during earlier eruptions in the series,<ref name=":17">{{Cite web |date=16 March 2024 |title=Neyðarstig Almannavarna vegna eldgoss milli Hagafells og Stóra Skógfells. |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/neydarstig-almannavarna-vegna-eldgoss/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is, en}}</ref> but it was downgraded later in steps.<ref name=":18">{{Cite web |date=16 July 2026 |title=Af neyðarstigi á hættustig vegna jarðhræringa á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/af-neydarstigi-a-haettustig-vegna-jardhraeringa-a-reykjanesskaga/ |access-date=23 July 2025 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> Volcanic activity substantially diminished in the days and weeks following the onset of the eruption but continued nonetheless.<ref name="bbcm16" />

During the initial phase of the eruption, the lava flow rate surged to between {{convert|1100 and 1200|m3/s|abbr=unit}} within the first hour. However, this intensity rapidly waned, with the ejection rate diminishing to approximately {{convert|100|m3/s|abbr=unit}} within six to eight hours of onset. By 17 March 2024, one day after the eruption began, the newly formed [[lava field]] covered nearly {{convert|6|km2|abbr=unit}}. By 20 March, the average lava output had decreased to around {{convert|15|m3/s|abbr=unit}}. Between 20–27 March, the average lava flow rate reduced to approximately {{convert|7.8|±|0.7|m3/sec|abbr=unit|ft3/sec}}. From 27 March–3 April, the average flow rate was estimated at {{convert|6.6|±|0.3|m3/sec|abbr=unit|ft3/sec}}, further decreasing to {{convert|3.6|±|0.7|m3/sec|abbr=unit|ft3/sec}} between 3–8 April. The lava bed had expanded to cover an area of {{convert|6.14|km2|abbr=unit}} by 9 April, with a volume of {{convert|31.3|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, and only one active [[crater]] remained. Throughout this period, there was a consistent upward movement of land due to magma flow. By the first half of April, the average ejection rate further decreased to approximately {{convert|3–4|m3/s|abbr=unit}}. In the final two weeks leading up to the conclusion of the eruption, the lava ejection rate had diminished to {{convert|1|m3/sec|ft3/sec|disp=preunit|or less |abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=23 May 2024 |title=Eldgos við Sundhnúk - útbreiðsla og rúmmál hrauns - samantekt til og með 8. maí 2024 |url=https://jardvis.hi.is/is/eldgos-vid-sundhnuk-utbreidsla-og-rummal-hrauns-samantekt-til-og-med-8-mai-2024 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[University of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref>

There was an indication that magma was primarily emerging from a deeper chamber and bypassing the intermediate storage at {{convert|4–5|km|abbr=unit}} beneath Svartsengi, previously the exclusive reservoir for the last three eruptions. Such direct access from the deeper source, located at {{convert|8–12|km|abbr=unit}} depth, may have explained the prolonged activity of the eruption.<ref name=":19" /><ref name=":11">{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=20 March 2024 |title=Gosið gæti hafa náð jafnvægi og haldið lengi áfram |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/407910 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> A recent study revealed that this eruption, along with all prior events in the series, has demonstrated "substantial [[Mantle (geology)|mantle-derived]] [[geochemical]] variability" attributable to dynamic magma behaviour in the [[Earth's crust|mid-crust]], thereby complicating efforts to predict future volcanic activity with accuracy.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Matthews |first1=Simon W. |last2=Caracciolo |first2=Alberto |last3=Bali |first3=Enikő |last4=Halldórsson |first4=Sæmundur A. |last5=Sigmarsson |first5=Olgeir |last6=Guðfinnsson |first6=Guðmundur H. |last7=Pedersen |first7=Gro B. M. |last8=Gunnarsson Robin |first8=Jóhann |last9=Marshall |first9=Edward W. |last10=Aden |first10=Araksan A. |last11=Gísladóttir |first11=Bryndís Ýr |last12=Bosq |first12=Chantal |last13=Auclair |first13=Delphine |last14=Merrill |first14=Heini |last15=Levillayer |first15=Nicolas |date=18 October 2024 |title=A dynamic mid-crustal magma domain revealed by the 2023 to 2024 Sundhnúksgígar eruptions in Iceland |journal=Science |volume=386 |issue=6719 |pages=309–314 |doi=10.1126/science.adp8778 |pmid=39325865 |last16=Löw |first16=Noëmi |last17=Rúnarsdóttir |first17=Rebekka Hlín |last18=Johnson |first18=Sóley M. |last19=Steinþórsson |first19=Sveinbjörn |last20=Drouin |first20=Vincent|bibcode=2024Sci...386..309M |hdl=20.500.11815/5042 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

In early April 2024, Professor Þorvaldur Þórðarson, observing the decline in [[thermal imaging]] signals and magma output, suggested the eruption was weakening. The darkening of gases and the halt in land uplift underscored a shift in the magma dynamics, now dominated by the deeper magma source. This pattern echoed the initial stages of the [[Fagradalsfjall#2019 to 2021 activity and eruptions|2021 Fagradalsfjall eruption]], marked by low magma activity. He also posited that a reduction in magma flow to below {{convert|2–3|m3/s|abbr=unit}} would have heralded the cessation of the eruption in the foreseeable future.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kristinsdóttir |first1=Klara Ósk |last2=Kristinsson |first2=Kári Freyr |date=31 March 2024 |title=Reiknar ekki með öðrum atburði í Sundhnúkagígum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/31/reiknar_ekki_med_odrum_atburdi_i_sundhnukagigum/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240401165524/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/31/reiknar_ekki_med_odrum_atburdi_i_sundhnukagigum/|archive-date =1 April 2024}}</ref> Land uplift had halted when the March–May 2024 eruption began but resumed at the beginning of April at a slower pace than after the previous three eruptions.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnúsdóttir |first=Ásta Hlín |date=4 April 2024 |title=Landris mælist á ný og engar vísbendingar um að gosið sé að klárast |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/409262 |access-date=8 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=8 April 2024 |title=Landrisið verulegt og hefur færst í aukana |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/08/landrisid_verulegt_og_hefur_faerst_i_aukana/ |access-date=8 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240408165305/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/08/landrisid_verulegt_og_hefur_faerst_i_aukana/|archive-date =8 April 2024}}</ref> As a result, approximately half of the magma flow was diverted to the shallower magma chamber, while the other half continued to the surface.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=18 April 2024 |title=Kvikan virðist nú skiptast til helminga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/18/kvikan_virdist_nu_skiptast_til_helminga/ |access-date=18 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240418151450/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/18/kvikan_virdist_nu_skiptast_til_helminga/|archive-date =18 April 2024}}</ref> The volume of magma that had accumulated prior to the event was more than {{convert|4|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, which is considerably lower than the volumes observed before other eruptions in the series.<ref name=":89" /> This lower magma volume is attributed to the [[#March 2024 earthquakes|magma intrusion in early March 2024]] that failed to breach the surface approximately two weeks prior to the March–May 2024 eruption.<ref name=":0" />

The eruption commenced with the shortest warning on record—characterised by minimal precursor seismicity and the briefest interval from initial indications to onset—a phenomenon attributed to repeated dike injections along preferential pathways established by earlier [[Igneous intrusion|intrusions]], which progressively reduce resistance to magma ascent and facilitate subsequent eruptions.<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=17 March 2024 |title=Fyrirvarinn nánast enginn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/fyrirvarinn_nanast_enginn/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325204210/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/fyrirvarinn_nanast_enginn/|archive-date =25 March 2024}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Gudmundsson |first=Agust |date=1 April 2025 |title=Multiple dikes make eruptions easy |journal=Journal of Volcanology and Geothermal Research |volume=460 |article-number=108284 |doi=10.1016/j.jvolgeores.2025.108284 |issn=0377-0273|doi-access=free |bibcode=2025JVGR..46008284G }}</ref> Moreover, it was initially noted to be more intense than its predecessors in the series, establishing it as the most energetic volcanic activity observed since the [[2014–2015 eruption of Bárðarbunga|2014–2015 Bárðarbunga eruption]],<ref>{{Cite news |last=Pálsdóttir |first=Inga Þóra |date=16 March 2024 |title=Kröftugra en öll síðustu gos |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/16/kroftugra_en_oll_sidustu_gos/ |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240402205202/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/16/kroftugra_en_oll_sidustu_gos/|archive-date =2 April 2024}}</ref> until it was later surpassed by the May–June 2024 eruption.<ref name=":23">{{Cite web |last1=Magnúsdóttir |first1=Ásta Hlín |last2=Hrafnkelsdóttir |first2=Linda H. Blöndal |date=29 May 2024 |title=Greinilega stærsta gosið í þessari hrinu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/413975 |access-date=1 June 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> As of today, this eruption remains the longest-lasting in the series, persisting for 54 days.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Daðason |first=Kolbeinn Tumi |date=19 March 2024 |title=Eldgosið toppar þrjú síðustu |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242544979d/eld-gosid-toppar-thrju-sidustu |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Access to the eruption area was prohibited, as it was with previous eruptions.<ref name=":22">{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=5 April 2024 |title="Ekki túristagos enn sem komið er" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/05/ekki_turistagos_enn_sem_komid_er/ |access-date=6 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240406134007/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/05/ekki_turistagos_enn_sem_komid_er/|archive-date =6 April 2024}}</ref> However, some tourists have still been caught and turned back,<ref>{{Cite web |date=20 April 2024 |title=Hópur fólks týndist við gosstöðvarnar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/20/hopur_folks_tyndist_vid_gosstodvarnar/ |access-date=21 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240421223953/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/20/hopur_folks_tyndist_vid_gosstodvarnar/|archive-date =21 April 2024}}</ref> though this has not been a significant issue.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsson |first=Atli Steinn |date=9 April 2024 |title=Aðgengi að þessu gosi öðruvísi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/09/adgengi_ad_thessu_gosi_odruvisi/ |access-date=18 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240418152436/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/09/adgengi_ad_thessu_gosi_odruvisi/|archive-date =18 April 2024}}</ref>

After almost 24 hours without any lava activity from the crater, the eruption was officially declared over on 9 May.<ref name="4th end">{{cite news |date=9 May 2024 |title=Eldgosinu er lokið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/09/eldgosinu_er_lokid/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240513143712/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/09/eldgosinu_er_lokid/ |archive-date=13 May 2024 |access-date=13 May 2024 |work=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnardóttir |first=Lovísa |date=9 May 2024 |title=Eldgosinu lokið eftir 54 daga |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242568267d/eld-gosinu-lokid-eftir-54-daga |access-date=11 May 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> By the time it ceased, the lava field had expanded slightly to approximately {{convert|6.2|km2|abbr=unit}}, up from its initial coverage of just under {{convert|6|km2|abbr=unit}} after the first day of the eruption. The total volume of lava produced is estimated at around {{convert|35|e6m3|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":42" /> According to the Institute of Earth Sciences at the University of Iceland, although this eruption was significantly smaller than the [[Fagradalsfjall#2019 to 2021 activity and eruptions|2021 event]], its volume was three times greater than that of the December 2023 and February 2024 eruptions.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 May 2024 |title=Eldgos við Sundhnúk - útbreiðsla og rúmmál hrauns - samantekt til og með 30. apríl 2024 |url=https://jardvis.hi.is/is/eldgos-vid-sundhnuk-utbreidsla-og-rummal-hrauns-samantekt-til-og-med-30-april-2024 |access-date=10 May 2024 |website=[[University of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Ingibjörg Sara |date=8 May 2024 |title=Hratt dregið úr hraunflæði frá upphafi eldgoss |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-05-08-hratt-dregid-ur-hraunflaedi-fra-upphafi-eldgoss-412266 |access-date=18 May 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== May–June 2024 eruption ===
[[File:Eldgos 29 maí 2024 B.jpg|thumb|Eruption on 29 May 2024 just after it started]]

On 29 May 2024 at 12:45 UTC, a fifth eruption commenced following the opening of a fissure near [[Sundhnúksgígar]], after a swarm of earthquakes began in the area nearly 2 hours earlier.<ref name=":0" /> This eruption produced lava fountains reaching heights of {{convert|60–70|m|abbr=unit}} and plumes ascending to an altitude of {{convert|3.5|km|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":0" /> [[Volcanic smog]], consisting of [[Sulfate ion|sulfate ions]], was also detected far from Iceland with [[satellite imagery]]. The fissure was initially estimated to be over {{convert|1|km|abbr=unit}} in length, but roughly an hour and a half later, the fissure had expanded to a width of {{convert|3.4|km|abbr=unit}}, consistent with the pattern observed in previous eruptions.<ref>{{cite news |date=29 May 2024 |title=Stærsta gosið í hrinunni — Grindavík rafmagnslaus |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-05-29-staersta-gosid-i-hrinunni-grindavik-rafmagnslaus-413850 |access-date=29 May 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=Icelandic}}</ref> Evacuations were again ordered in Grindavík and at the Blue Lagoon.<ref name="ajmay29" /><ref>{{Cite news |last=Mackintosh |first=Thomas |date=29 May 2024 |title=Iceland's Blue Lagoon evacuated as volcano erupts |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c844em2ey7eo |access-date=29 May 2024 |work=[[BBC]] |language=en-UK}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Di Marco |first=Marco |date=29 May 2024 |title=An Iceland volcano starts erupting again, spewing lava into the sky |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-grindavik-blue-lagoon-87c95e0f5893e1e3c69634c09d3913d8 |access-date=29 May 2024 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> This eruption followed a week with around 400 recorded earthquakes in the area while more than {{convert|17|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had accumulated underground.<ref name=":89" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Þorgils |date=28 May 2024 |title=20 milljónir rúmmetrar af kviku undir Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/413831 |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=ajmay29>{{Cite news |date=29 May 2024 |title= New volcanic eruption on Iceland's Reykjanes Peninsula |url= https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/5/29/new-volcanic-eruption-on-icelands-reykjanes-peninsula |access-date=29 May 2024 |work=[[Al Jazeera]] |language=en}}</ref> The lava flow in the first four hours of the eruption was estimated to average around {{convert|1500|m3/s|abbr=unit}}, surpassing the previous record holder of the series, the March–May 2024 eruption.<ref name=":0" />

A team from the Icelandic Institute of Natural History and the National Land Survey of Iceland analysed data collected by experts from [[Verkís]], Efla, and Svarmi during a drone flight over the eruption sites on 3 June 2024. The data revealed that the lava field covered an area of {{convert|8.6|km2|abbr=unit}} and had a volume of approximately {{convert|36|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, indicating an average flow rate during that period of close to {{convert|30|m3/s|abbr=unit}}. By 10 June, the lava field had expanded to around {{convert|9.2|km2|abbr=unit}} with a total volume of {{convert|41|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, and the average flow rate had decreased to approximately {{convert|10|m3/s|abbr=unit}}. [[Lava lake|Lava lakes]] formed as a result of lava pooling with little or no [[flow velocity]], particularly against Sýlingarfell.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Örlygsdóttir |first=Urður |date=3 June 2024 |title=Hrauntjarnir hafa myndast við gosið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/414711 |access-date=23 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> In the initial days following the start of the eruption, the number of active craters reduced to three, and about a week later, only one crater remained active until the eruption concluded.<ref name=":0" />

Initial [[geochemical]] data indicates that new and altered magma had accumulated beneath Svartsengi since early April 2024 when the land uplift began, leading up to the May–June 2024 eruption. The magma, distinct from the one feeding the March–May eruption, likely gathered in a separate chamber within the middle crust and exhibited a low [[K2O|K<sub>2</sub>O]]/[[TiO2|TiO<sub>2</sub>]] ratio during the May–June eruption, a first since the [[Fagradalsfjall#2019 to 2021 activity and eruptions|2021 Fagradalsfjall eruption]]. Scientists believe the two magma systems under Svartsengi are interconnected deep within the Earth's crust.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Halldórsson |first1=Sæmundur A. |last2=Marshall |first2=Edward W. |last3=Caracciolo |first3=Alberto |last4=Matthews |first4=Simon |last5=Bali |first5=Enikő |last6=Rasmussen |first6=Maja B. |last7=Ranta |first7=Eemu |last8=Gunnarsson Robin |first8=Jóhann |last9=Guðfinnsson |first9=Guðmundur H. |last10=Sigmarsson |first10=Olgeir |last11=Maclennan |first11=John |last12=Jackson |first12=Matthew G. |last13=Whitehouse |first13=Martin J. |last14=Jeon |first14=Heejin |last15=van der Meer |first15=Quinten H. A. |date=15 September 2022 |title=Rapid shifting of a deep magmatic source at Fagradalsfjall volcano, Iceland |journal=Nature |volume=609 |issue=7927 |pages=529–534 |doi=10.1038/s41586-022-04981-x |pmc=9477742 |pmid=36104557 |doi-access=free |last16=Mibei |first16=Geoffrey K. |last17=Kalliokoski |first17=Maarit H. |last18=Repczynska |first18=Maria M. |last19=Rúnarsdóttir |first19=Rebekka Hlín |last20=Sigurðsson |first20=Gylfi |last21=Pfeffer |first21=Melissa Anne |last22=Scott |first22=Samuel W. |last23=Kjartansdóttir |first23=Ríkey |last24=Kleine |first24=Barbara I. |last25=Oppenheimer |first25=Clive |last26=Aiuppa |first26=Alessandro |last27=Ilyinskaya |first27=Evgenia |last28=Bitetto |first28=Marcello |last29=Giudice |first29=Gaetano |last30=Stefánsson |first30=Andri|bibcode=2022Natur.609..529H }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=6 June 2024 |title=Eldgos við Sundhnúksgíga í lok maí 2024 – fyrstu berg- og jarðefnafræðigögn |url=https://jardvis.hi.is/is/eldgos-vid-sundhnuksgiga-i-lok-mai-2024-fyrstu-berg-og-jardefnafraedigogn |access-date=18 July 2024 |publisher=[[University of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref> Two days after the May–June eruption began, [[sulfur dioxide]] levels in [[Edinburgh]], [[Scotland]] spiked to 2,322 times higher than usual, the highest in about 50 years, due to strong [[Atlantic Ocean#North Atlantic|North Atlantic]] winds. Despite the elevated gas levels, local authorities confirmed that there was no threat to the inhabitants of Edinburgh, situated approximately {{convert|1,368|km|abbr=unit}} from the volcano.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Delaney |first=James |date=13 June 2024 |title=Iceland volcano may have caused Edinburgh sulphur dioxide spike |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cv2201zn2deo |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 June 2024 |title=Íslenskt SO2 í Edinborg: tvöþúsundföld aukning |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/erlent/2024/06/14/islenskt_so2_i_edinborg_tvothusundfold_aukning/ |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Such phenomena were observed during significant fissure eruptions in Iceland, like the [[2014–2015 eruption of Bárðarbunga|2014–2015 Bárðarbunga eruption]] in [[Holuhraun]], which released nearly {{convert|12|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of sulfur dioxide over its six months of activity, surpassing Europe's total annual SO<sub>2</sub> emissions and affecting multiple countries across the continent.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Ilyinskaya |first1=Evgenia |last2=Schmidt |first2=Anja |last3=Mather |first3=Tamsin A. |last4=Pope |first4=Francis D. |last5=Witham |first5=Claire |last6=Baxter |first6=Peter |last7=Jóhannsson |first7=Thorsteinn |last8=Pfeffer |first8=Melissa |last9=Barsotti |first9=Sara |last10=Singh |first10=Ajit |last11=Sanderson |first11=Paul |last12=Bergsson |first12=Baldur |last13=McCormick Kilbride |first13=Brendan |last14=Donovan |first14=Amy |last15=Peters |first15=Nial |last16=Oppenheimer |first16=Clive |last17=Edmonds |first17=Marie |title=Understanding the environmental impacts of large fissure eruptions: Aerosol and gas emissions from the 2014–2015 Holuhraun eruption (Iceland) |journal=Earth and Planetary Science Letters |date=August 2017 |volume=472 |pages=309–322 |doi=10.1016/j.epsl.2017.05.025 |bibcode=2017E&PSL.472..309I }}</ref>

The eruption spread rapidly during the first few hours, extending from Stóra-Skógfell in the north-west to Fiskidalsfjall {{IPA|is|ˈfɪs.kɪ.tals.fjatl̥|}} mountain in the south-east, and reaching as far as Illahraun {{IPA|is|ˈɪtl̥a r̥auːn|}}, west of Grindavík. At its most intense, the eruption released a large amount of ash at the southern end of the fissure due to contact with accumulated [[groundwater]] from recent rain,<ref name=":0" /> resulting in numerous explosions and even more violent interactions than in the February 2024 eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 May 2024 |title=Kröftugar sprengingar við Hagafell: Myndskeið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/29/kroftugar_sprengingar_vid_hagafell_myndskeid/ |access-date=23 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Lava flowing towards Grindavík was deflected by protective barriers, but two of the three main roads leading to the town were cut off.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Di Marco |first=Marco |date=30 May 2024 |title=An Iceland volcano spews red streams of lava toward an evacuated town |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-grindavik-blue-lagoon-87c95e0f5893e1e3c69634c09d3913d8 |access-date=30 May 2024 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> Volcanic activity decreased on 30 May<ref>{{Cite news |date=31 May 2024 |title='Jets of magma': Lava spurts from Iceland volcano, forcing evacuations |url=https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2024/5/31/lava-spurts-from-iceland-volcano-for-second-day-as-its-fissure-widens |access-date=1 June 2024 |work=[[Al Jazeera]] |language=en}}</ref> and stopped on 22 June.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=24 June 2024 |title=Eldgosinu lokið en áfram fylgst með hraunflæði |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/416345 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Lasting for 24 days, the eruption produced a volume of about {{convert|45|e6m3|abbr=unit}}<ref name=":25" /> and covered an area of {{convert|9.3|km2|abbr=unit}}, making it the largest eruption in this series in all aspects at that time.<ref name=":42" /> This record was later beaten by the August–September 2024 eruption.<ref name=":0" /><ref name=":23" />

=== August–September 2024 eruption ===
[[File:Eldgos í ágúst 2024 (014).jpg|thumb|Tourists near Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) observing the eruption the day after it started]]

On 22 August 2024, the sixth eruption in the ongoing volcanic series commenced at approximately 21:25 UTC, following a significant sequence of earthquakes that began more than 30 minutes earlier.<ref name=":43">{{Cite web |date=6 August 2024 |title=Volcanic unrest Grindavík |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/volcanic-unrest-grindavik |access-date=16 October 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":32">{{Cite web |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=22 August 2024 |title=Eruption begins on Reykjanes Peninsula |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2024-08-22-eruption-begins-on-reykjanes-peninsula-420082 |access-date=23 August 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref> The most substantial [[Earthquake|tremor]] in this series registered a magnitude of 4.1, the biggest in the area since December 2023.<ref name=":44">{{Cite web |last=Þórólfsdóttir |first=Sonja Sif |date=22 August 2024 |title=Stærsti jarðskjálftinn á þessu ári |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/22/staersti_jardskjalftinn_a_thessu_ari/ |access-date=28 October 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> About 30 minutes after the eruption began, the newly formed [[Fissure vent|fissure]] was estimated to be around {{convert|1.4|km|abbr=unit}} in length. The fissure rapidly extended, reaching nearly {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} within another 40 minutes. Lava flows from the fissure advanced approximately {{convert|1|km|abbr=unit}} every 10 minutes within the first half an hour, with the average lava flow rate during the initial hours of the eruption estimated up to {{convert|2000|m3/s|abbr=unit}}, marking the highest rate recorded in the series thus far.<ref name=":39">{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=24 August 2024 |title=Stærsta gosið til þessa |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242611919d/staersta-gosid-til-thessa |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Approximately five hours after the eruption began, a new {{convert|1|km|abbr=unit}} fissure opened north of the already active fissure line.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ásgrímsson |first=Þorsteinn |date=23 August 2024 |title=Ný sprunga opnast til norðurs |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/23/ny_sprunga_opnast_til_nordurs/ |access-date=23 August 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> At its peak, scientists estimate that the total extent of the eruptive fissures reached about {{convert|7|km|abbr=unit}}, although not all sections were active simultaneously.<ref name=":43" /><ref name=":33">{{Cite web |date=22 August 2024 |title=Gossprungan um fjórir kílómetrar en hraun ógnar ekki Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-08-22-gossprungan-um-fjorir-kilometrar-en-hraun-ognar-ekki-grindavik-420078 |access-date=23 August 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gísladóttir |first1=Hólmfríður |last2=Valþórsson |first2=Gunnar Reynir |last3=Arnardóttir |first3=Lovísa |last4=Ragnarsson |first4=Rafn Ágúst |date=23 August 2024 |title=Vaktin: Dregið úr virkni en gosið ekki náð jafnvægi |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242611353d/vaktin-inn-vidir-virdast-oruggir-i-bili |access-date=23 August 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Before the eruption began, around {{convert|21|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had accumulated in the magma chamber beneath Svartsengi.<ref name=":89" />

The Blue Lagoon was safely evacuated, with approximately 1,300 guests and staff relocated without incident. The nearby town of Grindavík remained largely unaffected, though residents of over 20 homes were also evacuated as a precaution. Keflavík Airport continued normal operations, but [[Route 41 (Iceland)|Route 41]] (Reykjanesbraut) was temporarily closed to allow emergency response teams better access. The road was later reopened, but with a temporary speed limit of {{convert|50|km/h|abbr=unit}} between the [[Interchange (road)|interchanges]] at Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) and Route 421 (Vogavegur) due to drivers stopping on the wayside of the highway to view the eruption, creating a danger.<ref name=":35">{{Cite web |date=23 August 2024 |title=Göngumaður féll í sprungu í nótt |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/23/gongumadur_fell_i_sprungu_i_nott/ |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Authorities were stationed near the eruption site to restrict access, as the area was considered a hazard zone.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 August 2024 |title=Einhverjir reynt að laumast inn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/22/einhverjir_reynt_ad_laumast_inn/ |access-date=23 August 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Still, many tourists gathered along Route 43 to watch the volcano, and some even walked toward the eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Hermann Nökkvi |date=27 August 2024 |title=Ferðamenn flykkjast á Grindavíkurveg |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/27/myndskeid_ferdamenn_flykkjast_a_grindavikurveg/ |access-date=2 December 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

The Icelandic Coast Guard flew its helicopter to visualise the eruption, along with scientists. Favourable wind conditions during the initial days of the eruption directed volcanic gases southward, toward the ocean. As a result, gases from the volcano were carried with winds to countries like Scotland and Spain.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sverrisson |first=Ólafur Björn |date=27 August 2024 |title=Spánverjar rekja "úldna eggjalykt" til eldgossins |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242613206d/spanverjar-rekja-uldna-eggjalykt-til-eldgossins |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The eruption remained visible from the Capital Region for several days and was even observable from more distant towns.<ref name=":32" /><ref name=":33" /> A few days after the eruption began, the lava started to approach an old bomb practice area in Vogaheiði {{IPA|is|ˈvɔɣa ˌheiðɪ|}}. This area was used by the [[United States Army]] between 1952 and 1960 for practicing with explosives, including [[Mortar (weapon)|mortar bombs]], [[Cannonball|cannonballs]], and small rockets, which can be deadly within a radius of {{convert|300|m|abbr=unit}} if they explode.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ómarsdóttir |first=Alma |date=12 September 2024 |title=Setja klæðningu á vegi sem voru lagðir yfir nýrunnið hraun |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-09-12-setja-klaedningu-a-vegi-sem-voru-lagdir-yfir-nyrunnid-hraun-421729 |access-date=12 September 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Concerns arose because the area is highly polluted, and many unignited explosives remain scattered over an extremely large site. The lava from the eruption was monitored closely to mitigate any potential risks related to these explosives.<ref name=":38">{{Cite web |last=Friðjónsdóttir |first=Hólmfríður Dagný |date=24 August 2024 |title=Hraun rennur yfir gamalt sprengjusvæði með tilheyrandi hættu |url=https://nyr.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-08-24-hraun-rennur-yfir-gamalt-sprengjusvaedi-med-tilheyrandi-haettu-420375 |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Másson |first1=Agnar Már |last2=Sæmundsen |first2=Guðrún Sigríður |date=25 August 2024 |title=Gekk fram á virka sprengju við eldgosið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/25/gekk_fram_a_virka_sprengju_vid_eldgosid/ |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The eruption caused no damage to infrastructure.<ref name=":43" />

[[File:Volcano Iceland 3 september 2024.jpg|thumb|The volcano at one of the last days of the eruption]]

The lava initially flowed mostly to the south and west but, in its last days, flowed almost exclusively to the north toward Route 41 (Reykjanesbraut), with only about {{convert|2.7|km|abbr=unit}} left<ref name=":41">{{Cite web |date=13 September 2024 |title=Brautin gæti lokast á einum degi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/09/13/brautin_gaeti_lokast_a_einum_degi/ |access-date=13 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> and even less distance remaining to the Suðurnesjalína power line, which connects the towns on the Reykjanes Peninsula to the Capital Region.<ref name=":36" /> The eruption was the first in the series to cross into a new municipality, reaching [[Vogar (municipality)|Vogar]] {{IPA|is|ˈvɔɣar̥|}} in the north. The eruption also caused up to {{convert|40|cm|abbr=unit}} of [[subsidence]] due to {{convert|17–27|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma flowing from the reservoir under Svartsengi to the [[Sundhnúksgígar]] crater chain. In total, the erupted lava volume above the surface reached {{convert|61|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, making it approximately {{convert|15|e6m3|abbr=unit}} larger than the May–June 2024 eruption.<ref name=":43" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnason |first=Freyr |date=25 September 2024 |title=Munaði 15 milljónum rúmmetra |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/09/25/munadi_15_milljonum_rummetra/?origin=helstu |access-date=25 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> A few days after the eruption began, the lava flow rate had dropped below {{convert|100|m3/s|abbr=unit}}, and the affected area had already reached {{convert|15.8|km2|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":42" /> By 3 September 2024, only two erupting [[Volcanic crater|craters]] remained active. On 6 September, the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO) officially declared the eruption over. Lasting approximately 14 days, it was the third longest in the recent series of volcanic events.<ref name=":34">{{Cite web |date=6 September 2024 |title=Eldgosinu er lokið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/09/06/eldgosinu_er_lokid/ |access-date=6 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> According to scientists, "it is evident from [[Scientific modelling|model calculations]] that no comparable volume of magma has reached the surface since the onset of seismic activity in the autumn of 2023".<ref name=":43" />

=== November–December 2024 eruption ===
[[File:The November 2024 eruption seen from Reykjavík.jpg|thumb|Eruption visible from Reykjavík on 20 November 2024]]

On 20 November 2024, the seventh eruption in an ongoing volcanic series began at 23:14 UTC, following a series of small earthquakes that started over 40 minutes earlier.<ref name=":50">{{Cite web |date=20 November 2024 |title=Eruption begins on the Sundhnúkur fissure swarm |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/eruption-begins-on-the-sundhnukur-fissure-swarm |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] |language=en}}</ref> A fissure initially measuring {{convert|2|km|abbr=unit}} long formed, extending north-east to {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} in about 2.5 hours, with lava flowing to both the west and east. The eruption's lava flow rate was about {{convert|1300|m3/s|abbr=unit}} in the first hours, which was considerably lower than in the August–September 2024 eruption. Within the first seven hours, the eruption had spread to nearly {{convert|7|km2|abbr=unit}}, and lava quickly approached Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur), crossing it over five hours after the eruption started. By 11 hours, the lava had reached the [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon's]] parking lot and engulfed it, along with a temporary service building. At that point, the lava was advancing at approximately {{convert|100|m|abbr=unit}} per hour. Around {{convert|10|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had flowed from the [[magma chamber]] in the first hours, about half the volume of the previous eruption. After less than 24 hours, only three craters remained active, with the middle one producing the most significant output.<ref name=":49">{{Cite web |date=20 November 2024 |title=Virkni í þremur gígum og dregið hefur úr hraunrennsli |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-20-virkni-i-thremur-gigum-og-dregid-hefur-ur-hraunrennsli-428061 |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> 

By the eighth day, the lava field had reached an area of {{convert|9.1|km2|abbr=unit}}, with a total volume of {{convert|47|e6m3|abbr=unit}} and only one crater active. The rate of lava effusion, while initially declining after the eruption's onset, had stabilised and remained relatively consistent. On the same day, the lava flow rate was measured at approximately {{convert|7–8|m3/s|abbr=unit}}, a substantial decline compared to the eruption's first day. The volcanic activity also released an estimated {{convert|64–71|kg/s|lb/s|abbr=unit}} of [[sulfur dioxide]].<ref name=":56">{{Cite web |date=29 November 2024 |title=Eldgosið það næststærsta í röðinni |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/29/eldgosid_thad_naeststaersta_i_rodinni/ |access-date=29 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> During its second week, the lava primarily flowed south-eastward, advancing toward Sandhóll {{IPA|is|ˈsant.houːtl̥|}} and [[Fagradalsfjall]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Gasmengunar gæti orðið vart í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/29/gasmengunar_gaeti_ordid_vart_i_grindavik/ |access-date=29 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Additionally, the eruption began to stabilise as a balance was reached between the inflow of magma to the reservoir beneath Svartsengi and the effusion of lava at the surface. The sole active crater continued to grow, raising concerns about the potential for structural collapse. By the third week, eruptive activity gradually diminished, accompanied by a steady decline in [[volcanic tremor]] measurements.<ref name=":50" />

The eruption came as a surprise, as the pattern of previous eruptions had led scientists to anticipate the [[Igneous intrusion|magma intrusion]] would continue into December 2024. Unlike earlier events, there was no significant increase in seismic activity in the weeks preceding the eruption, suggesting a potential shift in the volcano's behaviour.<ref name=":50" /> At the onset of the eruption, authorities described its location as "favourable" because it was initially far from infrastructure. However, the situation changed when the lava flow quickly developed a well-defined [[Lava channel|channel]]. This was facilitated by alterations in the landscape caused by previous lava flows, which created conditions conducive to the formation of a robust pathway. As a result, the lava advanced along the northern section of the Svartsengi barrier.<ref name=":49" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jósefsdóttir |first=Sólrún Dögg |date=21 November 2024 |title=Gosið gætið varað í nokkrar vikur |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242653303d/hraunrasin-oheppileg-en-vonar-ad-gardarnir-haldi |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The eruption prompted the evacuation of the Blue Lagoon and more than 50 houses in [[Grindavík]]. Authorities instructed people not to come to the area, which continued to be closely monitored for safety.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Di Marco |first1=Marco |last2=Keyton |first2=David |date=21 November 2024 |title=Volcano on Iceland's Reykjanes Peninsula erupts for the 7th time in a year |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-rekjanes-peninsula-08c7742f017556b03818f35cb50a6e1c |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> Although roughly {{convert|21|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma accumulated in a shallow chamber at a depth of roughly {{convert|5|km|abbr=unit}} beneath Svartsengi prior to the eruption,<ref name=":89" /> about {{convert|49|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of lava was expelled onto the surface, covering an area of approximately {{convert|9|km2|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 December 2024 |title=Heildarflatarmálið níu ferkílómetrar: Landris heldur áfram |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/12/19/heildarflatarmalid_9_km_landris_heldur_afram/?origin=helstu |access-date=19 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> This discrepancy was attributed to magma being sourced from a deeper chamber, with equilibrium gradually forming between magma inflow and outflow. In this system, when magma begins to accumulate again in the shallow chamber, land uplift resumes, typically indicating the preparation for a future eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sverrisson |first=Ólafur Björn |date=7 December 2024 |title=Leiðin fyrir dýpri kviku verður bara greiðfærari |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242661054d/leidin-fyrir-dypri-kviku-verdur-bara-greid-faerari |access-date=9 December 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> As of today, this eruption is the second largest in the ongoing eruptive series, with a total volume approximately {{convert|12|e6m3|abbr=unit}} less than the August–September 2024 eruption, which remains the largest in the series.<ref name=":50" /><ref name=":56" /> 

The lava flow engulfed Blue Lagoon's 350-space parking area and destroyed a small, temporary service building in the zone.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Adam |first1=Darren |date=21 November 2024 |title=Blue Lagoon: This is a new challenge |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2024-11-21-blue-lagoon-this-is-a-new-challenge-428338 |access-date=21 November 2024 |publisher=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref> However, construction workers managed to close an exposed gap in the defence barrier, which had served as the entrance to the Blue Lagoon area, preventing the lava from advancing further into the site. A [[Source water protection|water protection area]] near the geothermal spa was not considered in danger, and power lines experienced only minor disturbances.<ref name=":49" /> Two [[transmission towers]] in the area were later at risk as lava had surrounded the protective filling at their bases, steadily advancing toward their reinforced [[concrete foundation|concrete foundations]] and [[steel framework|steel frameworks]].<ref name=":51">{{Cite web |date=22 November 2024 |title=Vinna að því að verja rafmagnsmöstur við Svartsengi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/22/vinna_ad_thvi_ad_verja_rafmagnsmostur_vid_svartseng/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> In response to the threat, fire crews were requested to the site and deployed an [[Firefighting apparatus|apparatus]] to apply water streams onto the lava flow, aiming to cool its surface, slow its progression, and protect the structural stability of the towers.<ref name=":52">{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=22 November 2024 |title=Rafmagnsmastur í hættu vegna hraunflæðis |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242653948d/rafmagnsmastur-i-haettu-vegna-hraunflaedis |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> On the north-western section of the protective barriers around Svartsengi, lava neared overspilling, prompting the installation of 12 high-powered water cannons along a {{convert|360|m|abbr=unit}} stretch. Connected to excess water discharge from the Svartsengi power station, the cannons provided a continuous supply, effectively cooling the lava and halting its advance. Officials later declared the effort successful. Construction workers also decided to reinforce the barriers in the area by increasing their height.<ref name=":21">{{Cite web |last1=Gunnarsdóttir |first1=Þorgerður Anna |last2=Hannesdóttir Diego |first2=Hugrún |last3=Guðmundsdóttir |first3=Ingibjörg Sara |date=23 November 2024 |title=Gengið mjög vel að kæla hraunið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-23-hraunkaeling-hefur-gengid-framar-vonum-vid-krefjandi-adstaedur-428554 |access-date=24 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":55">{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Ingibjörg Sara |date=24 November 2024 |title=Þurfa um 26 þúsund lítra af vatni á mínútu til að kæla hraunið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-24-thurfa-um-26-thusund-litra-af-vatni-a-minutu-til-ad-kaela-hraunid-428600 |access-date=24 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The last activity from the active crater was observed on the morning of 8 December 2024, with no further activity detected later that day, thereby confirming the end of the eruption.<ref name=":50" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |date=9 December 2024 |title=Eldgosinu er lokið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242661739d/eld-gosinu-er-lokid |access-date=9 December 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

=== April 2025 eruption ===
[[File:April 2025 eruption.png|thumb|The April 2025 eruption seen from a webcam located on Húsafell mountain, east of Grindavík]]

On 1 April 2025, the eighth eruption in an ongoing volcanic series began at 09:44 UTC, following a series of earthquakes that lasted for more than 3 hours.<ref name=":77" /> The seismic swarm began near the Sundhnúkur crater row, accompanied by rapid [[Deformation (volcanology)|deformation]] detected by GPS stations and pressure changes in boreholes operated by [[HS Orka]]. These signals confirmed the onset of a [[Igneous intrusion|magma intrusion]]—the largest since November 2023—with approximately {{convert|30|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma flowing from the Svartsengi magma reservoir into the newly formed dike. By roughly 10:00 UTC, the fissure had already penetrated through the defensive walls north of the town and triggered fault movements in the area. Around 12:35 UTC, the primary fissure had expanded to an estimated length of {{convert|1.2|km|abbr=unit}}. Additionally, a second fissure opened between Grindavík and its protective barriers. Seismic activity below the surface extended the dike around {{convert|20|km|abbr=unit}} north-east, with earthquakes concentrated at its north-eastern end, {{convert|9|km|abbr=unit}} beyond previous eruption sites. Triggered earthquakes up to M3.9 near [[Krýsuvík (volcanic system)|Trölladyngja]] and M5.3 at the south-west tip of [[Reykjanes]] were later linked to stress adjustments from the intrusion, according to the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO).<ref name=":77" /><ref name=":75">{{Cite news |last=Bryant |first=Miranda |date=1 April 2025 |title=Icelandic town and Blue Lagoon spa evacuated after volcanic eruption |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2025/apr/01/grindavik-iceland-and-blue-lagoon-thermal-spa-evacuated-after-volcanic-eruption |access-date=1 April 2025 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref name=":76">{{Cite news |last1=Nierenberg |first1=Amelia |last2=Bjarnason |first2=Egill |date=1 April 2025 |title=Iceland Volcano Eruption Near Grindavik Prompts Closure of Blue Lagoon |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2025/04/01/world/europe/iceland-volcano-eruption-grindavik.html |access-date=1 April 2025 |work=[[The New York Times]] |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref>

The eruption led to the evacuation of both the town and the Blue Lagoon, triggering [[Siren (alarm)|warning sirens]] across the region.<ref name=":75" /><ref name=":76" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=1 April 2025 |title=Eruption underway on the Reykjanes Peninsula |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2025-04-01-eruption-underway-on-the-reykjanes-peninsula-440282 |access-date=1 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref> after it commenced just to the north of protective barriers constructed close to Grindavík. Eight residents chose to remain in the town, as the evacuation was voluntary.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrésdóttir |first=Iðunn |date=1 April 2025 |title=Ítreka að að allir sem eftir eru í Grindavík yfirgefi bæinn |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-04-01-itreka-ad-ad-allir-sem-eftir-eru-i-grindavik-yfirgefi-baeinn-440389 |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> While prevailing winds transported [[Volcanic gas|volcanic pollutants]] toward the north-east during the eruption, the overall quantity emitted was minimal.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 April 2025 |title=Eldgos er hafið |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/eldgos-er-hafid-3 |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> The IMO reported that a [[Water transportation|hot-water pipeline]] broke in northern Grindavík, confirming that significant fault movements had occurred within the town's boundaries. New minor cracks were also detected on the streets within the town. No damage was caused by direct contact with the lava.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=1 April 2025 |title=Áttunda gosið á Sundhnúksgígaröðinni |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-04-01-attunda-gosid-a-sundhnuksgigarodinni-440260 |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> 

Preceding the eruption, the IMO detected an [[earthquake swarm]] beginning at approximately 6:30 UTC centered on the [[Sundhnúkur]] crater row. Seismic signals indicated stronger activity than earlier recent events, suggesting substantial [[magma]] movement beneath the surface. The eruption was described as "only a small leak from the magma intrusion",<ref name=":80" /> as only a fraction of the magma reached the surface. By 16:45 UTC on 1 April, eruptive activity had ceased, and the event was formally declared over;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnar |first=Jakob |date=3 April 2025 |title=Stuttu eldgosi lokið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252709850d/stuttu-eld-gosi-lokid |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> however, incandescent lava remained visible in the vicinity for several hours afterward. As of today, this eruption is the briefest and least powerful in the series, covering only {{convert|0.23|km2|abbr=unit}} and {{convert|0.4|e6m3|abbr=unit}}, with an average thickness of {{convert|1.7|m|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":77" /> In the following hours, although volcanic activity had stopped, seismic activity continued in the newly formed dike for several days, as detailed in the [[#April 2025 earthquakes|April 2025 earthquakes]] section.<ref name=":75" /><ref name=":76" /> More than {{convert|22|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had built up in the shallower magma chamber under the Svartsengi area.<ref name=":89" /> 

=== July–August 2025 eruption ===
[[File:Sundhnúkseldar_16_júlí_2025_007e.jpg|thumb|The eruption observed from the Icelandic Coast Guard's helicopter on 16 July 2025]]

On 16 July 2025 at 03:54 UTC, nearly three hours after a [[Earthquake swarm|swarm]] of {{convert|4–6|km|abbr=unit}} deep earthquakes exposed a south-north dike beneath the crater row, the ninth eruption broke out just south-east of Litla‑Skógfell.<ref name=":85" /> The initial fissure, measuring {{convert|700–1000|m|abbr=unit}}, lengthened to roughly {{convert|2.4|km|abbr=unit}} within eight hours. A parallel {{convert|500|m|abbr=unit}} crack and advancing [[pāhoehoe]] flows expanded the lava field to over {{convert|3.2|km2|abbr=unit}}, as confirmed by [[ICEYE]] satellite imagery. Within 24 hours, the fissure fragmented into 10–12 active [[Volcanic cone|cones]]. By 19 July it had consolidated into about two or three cones and to a single cone by 22 July. As of 23 July, the lava field had expanded to {{convert|3.3|km2|abbr=unit}} in area and {{convert|26.8|e6m3|abbr=unit}} in volume. As in the February 2024 and May–June 2024 eruptions, this eruption interacted with groundwater, violently ejecting grey ash and smoke into the air in certain areas.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 July 2025 |title=Miklir gufustrókar stíga upp |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/16/miklir_gufustrokar_stiga_upp/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Because earlier models had predicted the next eruption in autumn 2025, this mid‑July event caught volcanologists by surprise.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ketilsson |first=Páll |date=16 July 2025 |title="Þetta gos kom okkur á óvart" - stærð undir meðalagi |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/%E2%80%9Ethetta-gos-kom-okkur-a-ovart%E2%80%9C---staerd-undir-medalagi |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":86">{{Cite web |date=16 July 2025 |title=A magma instrusion has started on Sundhnúksgígaröð crater row |url=https://en.vedur.is/about-imo/news/a-magma-instrusion-has-started-on-sundhnuksgigarod-crater-row |access-date=23 July 2025 |website=Icelandic Meteorological Office |language=en}}</ref> Prior to the event, less than {{convert|22|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of magma had pooled in the magma chamber beneath the surface.<ref name=":89" />

An evacuation was carried out in the area, affecting approximately 200 people at the Blue Lagoon<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 July 2025 |title=200 manns gert að yfirgefa hótel Bláa lónsins |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/16/200_manns_gert_ad_yfirgefa_hotel_blaa_lonsins/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> and over 100 residents and visitors in the town of Grindavík, including around 20 to 30 campers at a local campsite.<ref>{{Cite web |last= |first= |date=16 July 2025 |title=Níunda gosið á Sundhnúksgígaröðinni — dregið hefur úr virkni og almannavarnastig lækkað |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448656 |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Chapman |first=Michael |date=16 July 2025 |title=Volcanic Eruption Begins at Sundhnúk Crater Row in Reykjanes |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/news/volcanic-eruption-begins-on-reykjanes-peninsula-near-stora-skogfell/ |publisher=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en |type=No archive allowed}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 July 2025 |title=Iceland volcano eruption forces evacuation of town and iconic geothermal spa |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-blue-lagoon-grindavik-c72f44eb9ecb5f2d87e87fd53ed3b26d |access-date=16 July 2025 |website=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> Two [[United Airlines]] aircraft headed for Keflavík Airport returned to their points of departure on the airline's own initiative, without having received any specific advisories or instructions from Icelandic [[air traffic control]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 July 2025 |title=Flugvélum snúið við vegna gossins |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/17/flugvelum_snuid_vid_vegna_gossins/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> At that time, access to the volcano was permitted, and conditions for visiting the eruption were considered the most favourable since the [[Fagradalsfjall|Fagradalsfjall eruptive series of 2021–2023]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Oddur Ævar |date=21 July 2025 |title=Aðstæðir þær bestu síðan 2021: Bílastæði við eldgosið að fyllast |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252753510d/ad-staedur-thaer-bestu-sidan-2021-bila-staedi-vid-eld-gosid-ad-fyllast |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The location of the eruption, combined with the availability of parking facilities along the peninsula's south coast, attracted large numbers of tourists and other visitors, resulting in full parking lots in the area.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=25 July 2025 |title=Margir ganga að spræku eldgosinu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-07-25-margir-ganga-ad-spraeku-eldgosinu-449490/ |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Some people were even seen walking on the recently cooled lava,<ref>{{Cite web |date=26 July 2025 |title=Gengu yfir nýstorknað og glóandi hraun |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/26/gengu_yfir_nystorknad_og_gloandi_hraun/ |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> most of them foreign tourists.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=27 July 2025 |title=Einkum ferðamenn sem ganga á nýju hrauninu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/449621 |access-date=28 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> This behaviour was also frequently observed during the previous eruptive series,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Reynisson |first=Jón Trausti |date=21 June 2021 |title=Ferðamenn ganga yfir rjúkandi hraunið í Nátthaga |url=https://stundin.is/grein/13614/ |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[Heimildin]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Valsson |first=Andri Yrkill |date=14 August 2022 |title=Þurfa reglulega að stöðva fólk sem æðir út á nýtt hraun |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2022-08-14-thurfa-reglulega-ad-stodva-folk-sem-aedir-ut-a-nytt-hraun/ |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Magnús Jochum |date=19 July 2023 |title=Ferðamenn upp við Litla-Hrút stuttu áður en gígurinn brast: "Þau hafa verið heppin að sleppa" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232441455d/ferda-menn-upp-vid-litla-hrut-stuttu-adur-en-gigurinn-brast-thau-hafa-verid-heppin-ad-sleppa- |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> and the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] warns that it is "life‑threatening", as the thin surface crust can collapse without warning, exposing molten lava beneath.<ref name=":86" /> The eruption did not cause any damage to infrastructure in the surrounding area.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Þorgils |date=16 July 2025 |title=Innviðir ekki í hættu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448818 |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:July–August 2025 eruption recently concluded.jpg|thumb|View of the last active crater from Route 41 (Reykjanesbraut), a few days after the eruption concluded]]

Warnings were issued shortly after the onset of the eruption due to elevated concentrations of [[Volcanic gas|volcanic gases]]. The highest [[Sulfur dioxide|sulfur dioxide (SO<sub>2</sub>)]] levels were recorded in [[Njarðvík]] within a few hours of the eruption, peaking at {{nowrap|{{formatnum:6284}} μg/m<sup>3</sup> ({{formatnum:{{#expr:6284*(24.45/64.066) round 2}}}} ppbv)}},<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ásgrímsson |first=Þorsteinn |date=16 July 2025 |title=Gildi fyrir brennisteinsdíoxíð fóru á rautt |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/16/gildi_fyrir_brennisteinsdioxid_foru_a_rautt/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> compared to typical background concentrations of less than {{nowrap|{{formatnum:5}} μg/m<sup>3</sup> ({{formatnum:{{#expr:5*(24.45/64.066) round 2}}}} ppbv)}}.<ref name=":88">{{Cite web |date=4 October 2025 |title=Loftgæði á Íslandi |url=https://loftgaedi.is/en |access-date=4 October 2025 |website=Air Quality Iceland |language=en}}</ref> In the following days, a sulfur dioxide-rich plume drifted over [[Reykjanesbær]] and into the Capital Region, where SO<sub>2</sub> levels also reached record highs for the current volcanic activity on the Reykjanes Peninsula—exceeding {{nowrap|{{formatnum:2000}} μg/m<sup>3</sup> ({{formatnum:{{#expr:2000*(24.45/64.066) round 2}}}} ppbv)}},<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=21 July 2025 |title=Hæsta gildi brennisteinsdíoxíðs frá upphafi eldsumbrota |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252753459d/haesta-gildi-brenni-steins-dioxids-fra-upp-hafi-elds-um-brota |access-date=4 October 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> compared against the same background levels of less than {{nowrap|{{formatnum:5}} μg/m<sup>3</sup> ({{formatnum:{{#expr:5*(24.45/64.066) round 2}}}} ppbv)}}.<ref name=":88" /> About a week into the eruption, sulfur dioxide emissions from the [[fissure vent]] were around {{convert|100|kg/s|lb/s|abbr=unit}} but dropped significantly the following day to {{convert|25–44|kg/s|lb/s|abbr=unit}}. Within two weeks, volcanic gas emissions from the eruption led to a mild but visible [[haze]] of [[Sulfate|sulfate particles (SO<sub>4</sub>)]] across the country, formed as sulfur dioxide reacted with moisture and oxygen in the atmosphere.<ref name=":86" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 July 2025 |title=Gasmengun á Akureyri: Látið börn ekki sofa úti |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/17/gosmengun_a_akureyri_latid_born_ekki_sofa_uti/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Másson |first=Agnar Már |date=16 July 2025 |title=Blámóða vofir yfir Vestfjörðum og Skagafirði |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252752064d/blamoda-vofir-yfir-vest-fjordum-og-skaga-firdi |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Snævarr |first=Þorgrímur Kári |date=17 July 2025 |title=Varað við gasmengun á Snæfellsnesi og Vestfjörðum |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448889 |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> This led to an increase and temporary burden on clinics and local health centres, as people experiencing various symptoms sought medical advice.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jóhannsson |first=Róbert |date=23 July 2025 |title=Álag á heilsugæslustöðvum vegna mengunar |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/449277 |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Despite the situation, specialists emphasised that the pollution was not a "poison gas".<ref name=":86" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Smári Jökull |date=20 July 2025 |title="Þetta er ekki eiturgas" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252753358d/-thetta-er-ekki-eitur-gas- |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Markúsdóttir |first=Erla María |date=20 July 2025 |title="Það er enginn að fara að detta niður dauður af nokkrum andardráttum" |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/449080 |access-date=27 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> In early August, [[volcanic tremor]] and explosive activity declined, coming to a complete halt on 4 August. On 5 August, the eruption was declared over, as police-operated drone imagery confirmed the absence of activity within the craters.<ref name=":86" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsdóttir |first=Þorgerður Anna |date=5 August 2025 |title=Níunda eldgosinu á Sundhnúksgígaröðinni er lokið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/450192 |access-date=5 August 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

==Wildfires==
During the first weeks following the start of the March–May 2024 eruption, [[wildfire]]s broke out on the Svartsengi area. The fires, unlike the extensive wildfires during the [[Fagradalsfjall#2023 eruptive activity|last Fagradalsfjall eruption in 2023]], which spread across vast distances, were contained within a localised region. However, very dry soil and gentle breezes raised the risk of the fires' spread, especially with lava temperatures soaring between {{convert|700 and 800|C|abbr=unit|sigfig=2}}. Firefighting efforts, characterised by manual labour and the strategic use of [[Tank truck|tanker trucks]]—including a notably powerful truck from the Rescue Team in [[Vík í Mýrdal]]—were carried out by firefighters in Grindavík together with the Fire Protection Services of [[Árnessýsla]] and [[Southern Peninsula (Iceland)|Suðurnes]], as well as the Þorbjörn Rescue Team.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Kristjánsson |first1=Alexander |last2=Friðjónsdóttir |first2=Hólmfríður Dagný |date=29 March 2024 |title="Erum að vinna að því að halda þessu í skefjum" |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/408859 |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=29 March 2024 |title=Glíma við gróðurelda við Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/29/glima_vid_grodurelda_vid_grindavik/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240330131655/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/29/glima_vid_grodurelda_vid_grindavik/|archive-date=30 March 2024|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=1 April 2024 |title=Berjast við gróðurelda vestan megin við gíginn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/01/berjast_vid_grodurelda_vestan_megin_vid_giginn/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240401201826/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/01/berjast_vid_grodurelda_vestan_megin_vid_giginn/|archive-date =1 April 2024}}</ref> Emergency crews successfully gained control over the wildfires at the beginning of April 2024, with most of the flames having been extinguished.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=2 April 2024 |title=Búið að slökkva mestu gróðureldana |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/409017 |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

Minor wildfires broke out in the Svartsengi area again at the beginning of June 2024, shortly after the May–June eruption had commenced. Firefighters reported that while extinguishing the fires was manageable, they had to remain vigilant to prevent the fires from getting out of control. The fires were anticipated due to the dry period in the area following the onset of summer. A few days later, additional fires began in the area, similar to those previously extinguished. The firefighting efforts were well-supported by a strong labour force from Grindavík and additional teams from the [[Capital Region (Iceland)|Capital Region]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=8 June 2024 |title="Þetta er nánast allt eins og við áttum von á" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242582366d/-thetta-er-nanast-allt-eins-og-vid-attum-von-a- |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Valgerður Laufey |date=9 June 2024 |title=Gróðureldar kvikna á fleiri stöðum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/09/grodureldar_kvikna_a_fleiri_stodum/ |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

In late August 2024, wildfires broke out in several areas on the Reykjanes Peninsula due to the lava flow from the August–September 2024 eruption. The fire chief in Grindavík stated that the dry terrain and strong winds made it easier for the fires to spread. The area was used as a military training ground by the United States Army in the post-war period, making it inaccessible to [[Fire engine|fire trucks]]. The fires were initially too close to the lava field for emergency responders to intervene safely, but as they moved farther from the eruption site, more time became available to prepare for extinguishing them.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Diaz |first=Isabel Alejandra |date=24 August 2024 |title=Gróðureldar loga við gosstöðvarnar |url=https://nyr.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-08-24-grodureldar-loga-vid-gosstodvarnar-420356 |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

==Impact==
===Grindavík and residents===
[[File:Aerial view of Grindavík.jpg|thumb|Aerial view of Grindavík before the disaster]]

The town of [[Grindavík]] is currently mostly abandoned due to the volcanic activity and its inhabitants have been evacuated, although they have been permitted to return for short visits to remove personal effects and goods from their homes and businesses. Additionally, homeowners are permitted to reside in their properties. This represents the first time that an entire community has been evacuated in Iceland since the eruption of [[Heimaey]]'s [[Eldfell#The eruption begins|Eldfell in 1973]].<ref name="bbc" /> The town was reopened to the public nearly a year after the disaster.<ref name=":46" /> Presently, it is difficult to predict when Grindavík will become habitable again; however, scientists anticipate that the current conditions could persist for months or even years.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæmundsdóttir |first=Sunna |date=16 January 2024 |title=Ekki búandi í Grindavík næstu mánuði og jafnvel ár |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242515943d/ekki-buandi-i-grinda-vik-naestu-manudi-og-jafn-vel-ar |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Prisco |first=Jacopo |date=28 June 2024 |title=Iceland's volcanic activity may continue for decades, study says |url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/06/28/science/iceland-volcano-eruptions-prediction-study-scn/index.html |access-date=2 July 2024 |website=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> The current civil protection phase in the area is classified as "Alert Phase".<ref name=":18" />

At 23:20 UTC on 10 November 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Pálsson |first1=Magnús Jochum |last2=Jónsdóttir |first2=Margrét Björk |last3=Daðason |first3=Kolbeinn Tumi |last4=Erlingsdóttir |first4=Margrét Helga |last5=Sigurðsson |first5=Bjarki |last6=Sæmundsdóttir |first6=Sunna |last7=Pétursdóttir |first7=Lillý Valgerður |date=10 November 2023 |title=Vaktin: Kvikugangurinn virðist teygja sig undir Grindavík |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232487495d/vaktin-kvikugangurinn-virdist-teygja-sig-undir-grinda-vik |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> the Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management held an emergency briefing to announce a mandatory evacuation in Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 November 2023 |title=Rýming í Grindavík – Neyðarstig Almannavarna |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/ryming-i-grindavik-neydarstig-almannavarna/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnúsdóttir |first=Ásta Hlín |date=11 November 2023 |title=Grindavík rýmd – Tilmæli almannavarna |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-11-grindavik-rymd-tilmaeli-almannavarna-396390/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Prior to this, reports indicated that residents had already relocated due to persistent tremors that disrupted sleep and raised concerns about potential consequences.<ref name=":57">{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Ingibjörg Sara |date=9 November 2023 |title=Lítils háttar tjón í Svartsengi – svefnlaus nótt í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-09-litils-hattar-tjon-i-svartsengi-svefnlaus-nott-i-grindavik-396117 |access-date=30 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> During the evacuation, the [[Icelandic Red Cross]] accommodated approximately 1,700 displaced residents at three emergency centres located in [[Keflavík]], [[Kópavogur]], and [[Selfoss (town)|Selfoss]]. Of these, about 140 individuals spent the night in these centres, while the majority found alternative accommodations independently.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórólfsdóttir |first=Sonja Sif |date=11 November 2023 |title=140 gista í fjöldahjálparstöðvum í nótt |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/140_gista_i_fjoldahjalparstodvum_i_nott/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231207180549/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/140_gista_i_fjoldahjalparstodvum_i_nott/ |archive-date=7 December 2023 |access-date=7 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> All residents in the Grindavík area received an SMS message from [[112 (emergency telephone number)|112]], Iceland's emergency service, with the headline "EVACUATION".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ólafsdóttir |first=Kristín |date=10 November 2023 |title=Allt það helsta um rýminguna: Íbúar loki gluggum og aftengi rafmagnstæki |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232487692d/allt-thad-helsta-um-ryminguna-i-buar-loki-gluggum-og-aftengi-raf-magns-taeki |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=10 November 2023 |title=Neyðarstig og Grindavík rýmd |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/neydarstig-og-grindavik-rymd |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231207180641/https://www.vf.is/frettir/neydarstig-og-grindavik-rymd |archive-date=7 December 2023 |access-date=7 December 2023 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> Notably, two individuals in the town inadvertently slept through the evacuation and were discovered by police the following morning.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Ingibjörg Sara |date=12 November 2023 |title=Tveir sváfu rýmingu í Grindavík af sér |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-12-tveir-svafu-rymingu-i-grindavik-af-ser-396738/ |access-date=1 August 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Evacuated residents from Grindavík have relocated to 24 different municipalities across Iceland, with most now residing in the neighbouring town of [[Reykjanesbær]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Friðjónsdóttir |first=Hólmfríður Dagný |date=10 June 2024 |title="Á meðan við komumst ekki heim verðum við að geta séð heim" |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/415155 |access-date=24 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

Following the 5.2 tremor on 10 November 2023, a [[state of emergency]] was declared, and evacuation plans were put into effect for Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Cappucci |first=Matthew |date=10 November 2023 |title=Evacuations underway in Iceland as possible volcanic eruption looms |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/weather/2023/11/10/reykjanes-iceland-volcano-earthquakes-fagradalsfjall/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111052748/https://www.washingtonpost.com/weather/2023/11/10/reykjanes-iceland-volcano-earthquakes-fagradalsfjall/ |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |language=en-US}}</ref> This culminated in the relocation of nearly 4,000 residents, almost one percent of Iceland's entire population.<ref name="bbc-20231111">{{Cite news |last=Durbin |first=Adam |date=12 November 2023 |title=Iceland volcano: Emergency declared over volcano Fagradalsfjall eruption concerns |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67387827 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111182114/https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-67387827 |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> The Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management deployed the Icelandic Coast Guard Vessel ''[[ICGV Þór (2009)|Þór]]'' to the area "for safety purposes".<ref>{{Cite news |date=11 November 2023 |title=Iceland declares state of emergency over escalating earthquakes and volcano eruption fears |url=https://www.euronews.com/2023/11/11/iceland-declares-state-of-emergency-over-escalating-earthquakes-and-volcano-eruption-fears |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111114044/https://www.euronews.com/2023/11/11/iceland-declares-state-of-emergency-over-escalating-earthquakes-and-volcano-eruption-fears |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |work=[[Euronews]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref> It also closed all roads to Grindavík except for emergency and evacuation purposes.<ref name="bbc-20231111" /> On 13 November, authorities allowed residents to return briefly to the town to retrieve their belongings.<ref>{{Cite news |date=13 November 2023 |title=Residents get brief return to volcano-risk Icelandic town |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20231113-residents-get-brief-return-to-volcano-risk-icelandic-town |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231114031950/https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20231113-residents-get-brief-return-to-volcano-risk-icelandic-town |archive-date=14 November 2023 |access-date=14 November 2023 |work=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref> Boat owners were also allowed to remove their vessels from the harbour. The Icelandic government sought to accommodate displaced residents in holiday homes and little-used properties during the emergency in November and December 2023. The [[subsidence]], faulting and earthquakes have caused widespread damage to properties and infrastructure in the town.<ref name="RUV-2023-11-13" /> Since the November 2023 disaster, subsidence, earthquakes, and lava flow have led to multiple disruptions in the town's electricity and hot-water services.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrésdóttir |first=Iðunn |date=11 November 2023 |title=Rafmagnslaust í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/rafmagnslaust_i_grindavik/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325205535/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/rafmagnslaust_i_grindavik/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigþórsson |first=Atli |date=16 January 2024 |title=Vinna við heitt vatn í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/audskilid/402486 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=24 February 2024 |title=Heitt vatn komið á í Grindavík |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242534104d/heitt-vatn-komid-a-i-grinda-vik |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=25 March 2023 |title=Ekkert rafmagn í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/25/ekkert_rafmagn_i_grindavik/ |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =26 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240326070550/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/25/ekkert_rafmagn_i_grindavik/}}</ref> The seismic activity has received significant coverage by the international media.<ref name=":3">{{Cite news |date=10 November 2023 |title=Iceland declares state of emergency over volcanic eruption threat |url=https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/10/iceland-declares-state-of-emergency-over-volcanic-eruption-threat |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111060532/https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/nov/10/iceland-declares-state-of-emergency-over-volcanic-eruption-threat |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |work=[[The Guardian]] |language=en-UK |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref>

After the establishment of specific housing support in December 2023,<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 March 2025 |title=Opnað fyrir sértækan húsnæðisstuðning fyrir Grindvíkinga |url=https://hms.is/frettir/opnad-fyrir-umsoknir-vegna-natturuhamfara-i-grindavik |access-date=19 March 2025 |website=Housing and Construction Authority |language=is}}</ref> the [[Second cabinet of Katrín Jakobsdóttir|Icelandic government]] expected to assist around 700 residents, who are part of about 200 families, from the original 3,700 population of Grindavík. The project included purchasing up to 210 new apartments through two non-profit housing associations, primarily in the Reykjanes area and the [[Capital Region (Iceland)|Capital Region]]. This was to be supplemented by temporary financial support for increased housing costs, with the entire support system estimated to cost about ISK 220–240 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|220000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) per month, subject to a three-month reassessment with extensions.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Jónsdóttir |first1=Margrét Björk |last2=Pétursson |first2=Heimir Már |date=15 January 2024 |title=Framlengja húsnæðisstuðning við íbúa Grindavíkur og kaupa fleiri íbúðir |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242515288d/fram-lengja-studning-vid-i-bua-grinda-vikur-og-kaupa-fleiri-i-budir |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> This initiative represented a comprehensive effort to provide both immediate and sustainable housing solutions for Grindavík's displaced residents.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 November 2023 |title=Stuðningur við Grindvíkinga vegna húsnæðis |url=https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/11/24/Studningur-vid-Grindvikinga-vegna-husnaedis/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204130610/https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/11/24/Studningur-vid-Grindvikinga-vegna-husnaedis/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=6 December 2023 |website=[[Cabinet of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref> On 1 December 2023, the government, in partnership with the [[Government agencies in Iceland|Government Property Agency]], began seeking additional temporary rental properties from private and corporate owners to assist displaced Grindavík residents as part of a larger pre-established housing support programme that includes [[Wage subsidy|wage]] and [[Subsidized housing|rental]] subsidies.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 December 2023 |title=Óskað eftir leiguhúsnæði fyrir Grindvíkinga |url=https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/12/01/Oskad-eftir-leiguhusnaedi-fyrir-Grindvikinga/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231211031249/https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/12/01/Oskad-eftir-leiguhusnaedi-fyrir-Grindvikinga/ |archive-date=11 December 2023 |access-date=11 December 2023 |website=[[Cabinet of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref> A week later, a platform for Grindavík residents was launched online, targeting temporary housing in the Reykjanes Peninsula, the Capital Region, and neighbouring municipalities. It enabled direct leasing between residents and property owners, supported by government rental subsidies. These measures were some of the first actions taken to support the displaced residents of Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 December 2023 |title=Leigutorg fyrir íbúa Grindavíkur opnað |url=https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/12/08/Leigutorg-fyrir-ibua-Grindavikur-opnad/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231211031248/https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2023/12/08/Leigutorg-fyrir-ibua-Grindavikur-opnad/ |archive-date=11 December 2023 |access-date=11 December 2023 |website=[[Cabinet of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Grindavíkurvegur 2023 A.jpg|thumb|Closure of the road to Grindavík]]

In a collaborative response to the disaster and ongoing uncertainty in Grindavík, Iceland's major banks—[[Arion Bank]]i, [[Íslandsbanki]], and [[Landsbankinn]], in partnership with Finance Iceland—agreed to waive interest and indexation on housing loans for Grindavík residents for three months. This relief applied to loans up to ISK 50 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|50000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}). The measure, implemented in late 2023, aimed to provide equitable support, including options like deferred loan repayments. Each bank further outlined the specifics of these relief measures, ensuring they are tailored to the evolving needs of their customers in Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 November 2023 |title=Fella niður vexti af íbúðalánum Grindvíkinga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/22/fella_nidur_vexti_af_ibudalanum_grindvikinga/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231207180838/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/22/fella_nidur_vexti_af_ibudalanum_grindvikinga/ |archive-date=7 December 2023 |access-date=6 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> While these measures offered substantial relief, property owners facing irreparable damage to their homes were obligated to allocate a segment of their insurance compensation—earmarked primarily for on-site repairs or reconstruction—to cover a disposal fee. In cases where rebuilding at the original location was not permitted, property owners were then allowed to use their insurance proceeds to buy homes elsewhere.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=1 December 2023 |title=Þurfa að greiða förgunargjald fyrir ónýt hús |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-01-thurfa-ad-greida-forgunargjald-fyrir-onyt-hus-398658/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223901/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-01-thurfa-ad-greida-forgunargjald-fyrir-onyt-hus-398658 |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=1 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðjónsson |first=Viðar |date=28 November 2023 |title=10–20 hús ónýt og skoðun enn í gangi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/28/10_20_hus_onyt_og_skodun_enn_i_gangi/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231201093327/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/28/10_20_hus_onyt_og_skodun_enn_i_gangi/ |archive-date=1 December 2023 |access-date=1 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> These measures were later succeeded by the government's acquisition of residential properties in Grindavík at the beginning of 2024.<ref name=":47" />

On 23 February 2024, the Icelandic government enacted a law to acquire all residential real estate in Grindavík, initially estimating the cost at ISK 61 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|61000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 February 2024 |title=Frumvarp um kaup íbúðarhúsnæðis í Grindavík |url=https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2024/02/09/Frumvarp-um-kaup-ibudarhusnaedis-i-Grindavik/ |access-date=13 November 2024 |website=[[Cabinet of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref> However, the current projection indicates the total expenditure will be ISK 75 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|75000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}), including the assumption of all [[Unpaid principal balance|unpaid mortgages]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnardóttir |first=Lovísa |date=24 May 2024 |title=Kostnaður vegna uppkaupa fjórtán milljörðum meiri en áætlað var |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242574864d/kostnadur-vegna-upp-kaupa-fjor-tan-milljordum-meiri-en-a-aetlad-var |access-date=25 May 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":69">{{Cite web |last=Bernharðsdóttir |first=Berghildur Erla |date=18 March 2025 |title=Bæjarstjórn vilji hefja uppbyggingu en ríkisstjórnin doka við |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252702899d/baejarstjornin-vilji-hefja-uppbyggingu-en-rikisstjornin-doka-vid |access-date=19 March 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The operation, under the supervision of the newly established real estate company Þórkatla, aims to relieve the residents' distress and uncertainty following seismic activities since November 2023. The law also granted Grindavík residents until the end of 2024 to decide if they wish to sell their property to the state. Homeowners then have a [[right of first refusal]] to repurchase their properties within three years after the law's enactment.<ref name=":47">{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=23 February 2024 |title=Lög um kaup á húsnæði í Grindavík samþykkt |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242533413d/log-um-kaup-a-hus-naedi-i-grinda-vik-sam-thykkt |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gylfason |first=Georg |date=9 February 2024 |title=Ríkið kaupir íbúðarhúsnæði Grindvíkinga á 61 milljarð króna |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/20845/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Heimildin]] |language=is}}</ref> As of 27 November 2024, Þórkatla, under the government's supervision, had received more than 900 submissions from Grindavík residents, with the vast majority of them completed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Hermann Nökkvi |date=27 June 2024 |title=Fjárfesting verði 75 milljarðar þegar yfir lýkur |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/27/fjarfesting_verdi_75_milljardar_thegar_yfir_lykur/ |access-date=17 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |date=28 June 2024 |title=Þórkatla tekið við 400 eignum |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242590759d/thor-katla-tekid-vid-400-eignum |access-date=17 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnardóttir |first=Lovísa |date=27 November 2024 |title=Alls 914 umsóknir um uppkaup í Grindavík samþykktar |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242656048d/alls-914-umsoknir-um-uppkaup-i-grindavik-samthykktar |access-date=28 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> A total of approximately 930 properties are part of the project, with around 50% of them submitted in the latter half of March 2024, just weeks after the application process opened.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 March 2024 |title=Opna fyrir sölu á íbúðarhúsnæði í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/08/opna_fyrir_solu_a_ibudarhusnaedi_i_grindavik/ |access-date=17 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2024 |title=Volcano erupts again on Iceland peninsula |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20240316-volcano-erupts-again-on-iceland-peninsula-met-office-1 |access-date=17 March 2024 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnadóttir |first=Amanda Guðrún |date=27 March 2024 |title=546 Grindvíkingar vilja selja eignir sínar til ríkisins |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/408785 |access-date=27 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Þórkatla will rent these properties at 25% of [[Suðurnes|Suðurnes's]] market rate to provide temporary housing for residents who might want to live there.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 May 2024 |title=Þórkatla kynnir leiguverð fasteigna í Grindavík. Félagið tekur í dag við lyklum að fyrstu eignunum |url=https://island.is/v/fasteignafelagid-thorkatla/frett/thorkatla-kynnir-leiguverd-fasteigna-i-grindavik-felagid-tekur-i-dag-vid |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=Digital Iceland |language=is}}</ref> While industrial properties and businesses within the town will not be subject to acquisition,<ref name=":69" /> the Icelandic government will continue to offer various forms of aid, including support loans and wage assistance,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Einarsson |first=Þorlákur |date=29 April 2024 |title=Atvinnuhúsnæði ekki keypt upp í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/29/atvinnuhusnaedi_ekki_keypt_upp_i_grindavik/ |access-date=30 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =30 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240430143217/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/04/29/atvinnuhusnaedi_ekki_keypt_upp_i_grindavik/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Hermann Nökkvi |date=17 May 2024 |title=Kostnaðurinn nemur allt að þremur milljörðum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/17/kostnadurinn_nemur_allt_ad_thremur_milljordum/?origin=helstu |access-date=17 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =17 May 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240517225233/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/17/kostnadurinn_nemur_allt_ad_thremur_milljordum/?origin=helstu}}</ref> consistently providing additional benefits to both the town's businesses and the evacuated citizens.<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 April 2024 |title=Mótun fleiri tillagna um stuðning við atvinnulíf í Grindavík |url=http://www.grindavik.is/v/27156 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Grindavík]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 May 2024 |title=Frekari stuðningsaðgerðir fyrir Grindavík kynntar |url=https://www.stjornarradid.is/efst-a-baugi/frettir/stok-frett/2024/05/17/Frekari-studningsadgerdir-fyrir-Grindavik-kynntar/ |access-date=17 July 2024 |website=[[Cabinet of Iceland]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnason |first=Freyr |date=31 May 2024 |title=Áfram launastuðningur til ágústloka |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/31/afram_launastudningur_til_agustloka/ |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsson |first=Brynjólfur Þór |date=22 October 2024 |title=Geta fengið allt að 49 milljónir í stuðningslán |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-10-22-geta-fengid-allt-ad-49-milljonir-i-studningslan-425445 |access-date=23 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

As part of the government's comprehensive plan, which includes efforts by the Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management to prevent freezing damages in houses and ensure that Grindavík remains functional and safe for future habitation,<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2023 |title=Upplýsingagjöf vegna átaks Almannavarna til að fyrirbyggja frostskemmdir í húsum í Grindavík |url=https://www.hsveitur.is/um-okkur/frettir/upplysingagjof-vegna-ataks-almannavarna-til-ad-fyrirbyggja-frostskemmdir-i-husum-i-grindavik/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=HS Veitur |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=8 December 2023 |title=Kaupa 80 íbúðir í fyrsta áfanga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/08/kaupa_80_ibudir_i_fyrsta_afanga/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =1 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240401181320/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/08/kaupa_80_ibudir_i_fyrsta_afanga/}}</ref> up to 100 electricians and plumbers were mobilised to conduct critical repairs and upgrades, mostly from November 2023 to February 2024. This initiative also involved essential infrastructure work to secure and maintain the electrical and plumbing connections between the town and the power station, especially in January 2024, when Grindavík's water main was engulfed by lava from the eruption.<ref name=":60">{{Cite web |date=16 January 2024 |title=Grettistaki lyft í kjölfar eldgoss |url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/grettistaki-lyft-i-kjoelfar-eldgoss/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[HS Orka]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 2024 |title=Þorbjörn fer yfir stöðuna í Grindavík 22. janúar |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/thorbjorn-fer-yfir-stoduna-i-grindavik-22-januar |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> Specialised teams were also deployed to fill in cracks and faults resulting from seismic activity.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 December 2023 |title=Heitt vatn og rafmagn í lagi í Grindavík - hver er staðan á innviðum? |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/heitt-vatn-og-rafmagn-i-lagi-i-grindavik---hver-er-stadan-a-innvidum |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 2024 |title=100 manns við störf: Moka 700 gráða heitu hrauni |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/22/100_manns_vid_storf_moka_700_grada_heitu_hrauni/ |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =1 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240401181320/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/22/100_manns_vid_storf_moka_700_grada_heitu_hrauni/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Oddur Ævar |date=2 March 2024 |title=Píparar og rafvirkjar hús úr húsi í Grindavík |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242524541d/piparar-og-raf-virkjar-hus-ur-husi-i-grinda-vik |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> This effort has persisted to the present day, though it has included intermittent pauses.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sigurðsson |first1=Bjarki |last2=Kjartansson |first2=Kjartan |date=5 September 2024 |title=Fylla upp í sprungur í von um að hægt verði að opna Grindavík aftur |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242617277d/fylla-upp-i-sprungur-i-von-um-ad-haegt-verdi-ad-opna-grinda-vik-aftur |access-date=14 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ómarsdóttir |first=Alma |date=8 September 2024 |title=Setja upp sjö kílómetra af girðingum í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-09-08-setja-upp-sjo-kilometra-af-girdingum-i-grindavik-421404 |access-date=14 September 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

The [[Icelandic Police|State Commissioner of Police]], in consultation with the Police Commissioner in the Reykjanes Peninsula, downgraded the risk level in Grindavík from [[State of emergency|Emergency Phase]] to Alert Phase, which was effective from 23 November 2023 until the eruption on 18 December 2023. This decision, based on a new assessment by the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO), indicated a reduced likelihood of a sudden eruption in Grindavík. Residents and businesses in Grindavík were then permitted to enter the area between 07:00 and 21:00 UTC to retrieve valuables, attend to their properties, and conduct business activities.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 December 2023 |title=Grindvíkingar fá rýmri tíma |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/13/grindvikingar_fa_rymri_tima/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231213141947/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/13/grindvikingar_fa_rymri_tima/ |archive-date=13 December 2023 |access-date=13 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> However, the town remained closed to the general public and unauthorised traffic. Safety measures, including [[Access control|limited vehicle access]] with restriction on certain types of transportation, constraints on essential services and a prepared evacuation plan, were established.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 December 2023 |title=Hættumatskort enn í gildi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/04/haettumatskort_enn_i_gildi/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231205083227/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/04/haettumatskort_enn_i_gildi/ |archive-date=5 December 2023 |access-date=6 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The area, under continuous geological monitoring, is still considered dangerous. Residents were advised to keep records of valuables taken and to contact their insurance companies, and to be cautious as houses might be unsafe.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 November 2023 |title=Hættustig vegna jarðhræringa við Grindavík |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/haettustig-vegna-jardhraeringa-vid-grindavik/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223847/https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/haettustig-vegna-jardhraeringa-vid-grindavik/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 November 2023 |title=Fara af neyðarstigi: Íbúar fá rýmri heimildir |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/22/fara_af_neydarstigi_ibuar_fa_rymri_heimildir/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223847/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/22/fara_af_neydarstigi_ibuar_fa_rymri_heimildir/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Oddur Ævar |date=23 November 2023 |title=Íbúar í Grindavík fá rýmri heimildir á morgun |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232493814d/i-buar-i-grinda-vik-fa-rymri-heimildir-a-morgun |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204105928/https://www.visir.is/g/20232493814d/i-buar-i-grinda-vik-fa-rymri-heimildir-a-morgun |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=4 December 2023 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Following the town's evacuation, a state of emergency (Emergency Phase) was declared with the onset of each subsequent eruption, highlighting the significant risk involved. Upon the end of each eruption, the alert level was systematically lowered to Alert Phase and subsequently, a few days afterward, to Uncertainty Phase.<ref name=":17" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=18 December 2023 |title=Eldgos hafið norðan við Grindavík |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/eldgos-hafid-nordan-vid-grindavik/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2024 |title=Eldgos er hafið á Reykjanesinu |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/eldgos-er-hafid-a-reykjanesinu/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=8 February 2024 |title=Almannavarnir fara upp á neyðarstig vegna rofs á afhendingu á heitu vatni á Suðurnesjum |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/almannavarnir-fara-upp-a-neydarstig-vegna-rofs-a-afhendingu-a-heitu-vatni-a-sudurnesjum/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> Up to 700 residents commute to work in the town daily,<ref name=":22" /><ref name=":67">{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=18 February 2025 |title=Hús rifin í Grindavík – vaxandi goslíkur |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-02-18-hus-rifin-i-grindavik-vaxandi-goslikur-436620 |access-date=19 February 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":68">{{Cite web |last=Þórisdóttir |first=Anna Lilja |date=6 March 2025 |title=Bæjarskrifstofurnar flytja heim |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-03-06-baejarskrifstofurnar-flytja-heim-438260 |access-date=7 March 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> engaging in tasks such as discharging [[Fishing vessel|fishing vessels]] and conducting construction repairs on the harbour following the [[subsidence]] and earthquakes in the early phase of the seismic events.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Dagbjartsson |first=Sigurbjörn Daði |date=24 October 2024 |title=Framkvæmdir við Grindavíkurhöfn sem iðar af lífi |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/framkvaemdir-vid-grindavikurhofn-sem-idar-af-lifi |access-date=24 October 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sævarsson |first=Sigurður Bogi |date=16 March 2025 |title=Eyjabakki mikið skemmdur |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/03/16/eyjabakki_mikid_skemmdur/ |access-date=19 March 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

Since the disaster in November 2023, there have been several instances of tensions and disobedience by the residents of Grindavík. In December 2023, a couple was caught staying at their home for several nights and were threatened with arrest due to the regulations in place at that time.<ref name="guardian" /> On the first day of the May–June 2024 eruption, three residents of Grindavík refused to leave the town. The police did not use force as the evacuation was only an advisory from the authorities. However, the response teams later chose to evacuate, and the three individuals subsequently decided to leave as well since they were the only ones left in the town while the eruption was ongoing close by.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 May 2024 |title=Eldgos hafið og þrír neita að yfirgefa Grindavík |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/22021/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=Heimildin |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kristinsdóttir |first=Klara Ósk |date=30 May 2024 |title=Stóð aldrei til að beita valdi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/30/stod_aldrei_til_ad_beita_valdi/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Furthermore, three [[Lawsuit|lawsuits]] have been filed against the state over access restrictions in Grindavík—by an individual, a [[labor union|labour union]], and a group of employers and [[Tour operator|tour operators]] through a [[class action]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=5 February 2024 |title=Stefnir íslenska ríkinu vegna aðgöngutakmarkana í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-02-05-stefnir-islenska-rikinu-vegna-adgongutakmarkana-i-grindavik-404087 |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðmundsdóttir |first=Ingibjörg Sara |date=7 February 2024 |title=Blaðamannafélag Íslands stefnir ríkinu vegna takmörkunar á aðgengi í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-02-07-bladamannafelag-islands-stefnir-rikinu-vegna-takmorkunar-a-adgengi-i-grindavik-404230 |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Benediktsdóttir |first=Eva Björk |date=18 July 2025 |title=Hafa rætt við lögmann og undirbúa skaðabótamál gegn ríkinu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448988 |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:2025 Grindavik volcanic eruption aftermath aerial view.jpg|thumb|Image taken from an airplane shortly after departing Keflavík Airport on 22 April 2025, showing Grindavík's volcanic aftermath]]

In the aftermath of the November disaster, Grindavík was subjected to stringent regulations, which included specific opening and closing times. Authorities also created a live [[hazard map]] for residents and others in the area, which is regularly updated to reflect the current situation.<ref name=":0" /> Following the 10 January 2024 accident, in which a worker fell into a crack, Civil Defence imposed a temporary ban on all residency and operations in the town, deeming the conditions at the time unacceptable.<ref>{{Cite web |date=13 January 2024 |title=Fyrirskipa brottflutning úr Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/13/fyrirskipa_brottflutning_ur_grindavik/ |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> In late February 2024, the State Commissioner of Police authorised near-unlimited access to the town for designated groups, albeit with a clear emphasis on the fact that "residents and employees enter the town at their own risk". The chief of police further emphasised that Grindavík is ill-suited for children due to the absence of functioning schools, the compromised state of infrastructure, and possible hidden faults in the town. Access was strictly limited to residents, employees, and authorised media personnel for almost a year<ref>{{Cite web |date=11 April 2024 |title=Grindavíkurvegur opinn fyrir íbúa, viðbragðsaðila og starfsfólk |url=http://www.vegagerdin.is/upplysingar-og-utgafa/frettir/grindavikurvegur-opinn-fyrir-ibua-vidbragdsadila-og-starfsfolk |access-date=12 April 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Road Administration|Icelandic Road and Coastal Administration]] |language=is}}</ref> until it opened for the public again in October 2024.<ref name=":46" /> While homeowners are permitted to stay overnight in their homes, the chief of police strongly advises against it.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 February 2024 |title=Ákvörðun lögreglustjórans á Suðurnesjum |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/akvordun-logreglustjorans-a-sudurnesjum/ |access-date=25 March 2023 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=19 February 2024 |title=Grindvíkingum frjálst að dvelja í bænum á eigin ábyrgð |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/19/grindvikingum_frjalst_ad_dvelja_i_baenum_a_eigin_ab/ |access-date=19 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =19 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240419194630/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/19/grindvikingum_frjalst_ad_dvelja_i_baenum_a_eigin_ab/}}</ref> The number of people choosing to stay in Grindavík has increased over the past months, with the number of occupied homes now ranging from 40 to 60.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Smári Jökull |date=16 March 2024 |title="Það er verið að rýma Grindavík" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242543846d/-thad-er-verid-ad-ryma-grindavik- |access-date=19 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnason |first=Freyr |date=16 October 2024 |title=Stóru sprungurnar hættulegastar: Vantar súrefni |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/10/16/storu_sprungurnar_haettulegastar_vantar_surefni/?origin=helstu |access-date=16 October 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Despite this, around 1,100 individuals are still [[Domicile (law)|legally registered]] as residents of the town.<ref name=":68" /> Since the reopening of Grindavík in October 2024, many tourists have visited the town to observe the damage caused by the November 2023 earthquakes. Authorities consider them a high-risk group, as they are less likely to be fully informed about the current situation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=20 October 2024 |title=Börn og ferðamenn sérstakir áhættuhópar í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-10-20-born-og-ferdamenn-serstakir-ahaettuhopar-i-grindavik-425220 |access-date=24 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrafnkelsdóttir |first=Linda H. Blöndal |date=21 October 2024 |title="Við höfum lengið beðið þessa dags" |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-10-21-vid-hofum-lengid-bedid-thessa-dags-425312 |access-date=24 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

The main concern and risk in Grindavík currently comes from the extensive cracks and [[Fault (geology)|faults]] that have appeared, most of which formed during the subsidence in November 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 September 2024 |title=Hætta vegna jarðfalls í sprungur metin töluverð |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/09/19/haetta_vegna_jardfalls_i_sprungur_metin_toluverd/ |access-date=19 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Some of these pre-existing cracks have expanded following subsequent eruptions, and additional new ones have also formed.<ref>{{Cite web |date=15 January 2024 |title=Myndir: Nýjar sprungur í Grindavík og eldri sprungur víkkað |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/15/myndir_nyjar_sprungur_i_grindavik_og_eldri_sprungur/ |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pétursson |first=Vésteinn Örn |date=15 January 2024 |title=Nýjar sprungur myndast og þær eldri stækka |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242515564d/nyjar-sprungur-myndast-og-thaer-eldri-staekka |access-date=21 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Before the November event, authorities were cognisant of existing fissures; however, many structures and pieces of infrastructure were built over these old cracks, disregarding the potential for their reactivation after being dormant for more than 2,000 years. There are seven fracture zones in the Grindavík area; all of which have shown movement since November 2023. The largest crack, Stamphólsgjá, exceeds {{convert|30|m|abbr=unit}} in depth and reaches up to {{convert|3|m|abbr=unit}} in width.<ref name=":87">{{Cite web |last=Ómarsdóttir |first=Alma |date=1 October 2025 |title=Hugmyndir um niðurrif en einnig mikla uppbyggingu í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/455006 |access-date=2 October 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Since the onset of these events, specialists—including those from the [[Icelandic Road Administration]]—have been continuously monitoring and mapping both newly formed and previously unknown or concealed fractures that may pose risks to public safety.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 April 2025 |title=Jarðkönnun Grindavíkur – lokaskýrsla |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/jardkonnun-grindavikur-lokaskyrsla/ |access-date=3 April 2025 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> Their efforts involve the use of advanced tools such as [[Unmanned aerial vehicle|drones]] and various [[Survey instrument|geophysical survey instruments]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 June 2024 |title=Jarðrannsóknir í Grindavík |url=https://www.vegagerdin.is/vegagerdin/starfsemi/frettir/jardrannsoknir-i-grindavik |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Road Administration|Icelandic Road and Coastal Administration]] |language=is}}</ref> Concealed fractures continue to be discovered, as many of the eruptions in the series either dilate pre-existing fractures or generate new ones.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=3 April 2025 |title=Búið að kortleggja sprungur – viðgerðir ófjármagnaðar |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-04-03-buid-ad-kortleggja-sprungur-vidgerdir-ofjarmagnadar-440746 |access-date=3 April 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> On 10 January 2024, an employee engaged in sealing these cracks fell into what was apparently the largest fault, which runs directly through the centre of the town.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schram |first=Höskuldur Kári |date=26 December 2023 |title=Áhrif jarðhræringa mæld á þrívíddarkorti |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-26-ahrif-jardhraeringa-maeld-a-thrividdarkorti-400729/ |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Ísleifsson |first1=Atli |last2=Ólafsdóttir |first2=Kristín |date=10 January 2024 |title=Leita manns sem talinn er hafa fallið í sprungu í Grindavík |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242512999d/leita-manns-sem-talinn-er-hafa-fallid-i-sprungu-i-grinda-vik |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kristjánsson |first=Alexander |date=6 February 2024 |title=Djúp sprunga undir íþróttahúsinu í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404155 |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=10 January 2024 |title=Kortleggja þarf sprungurnar: Gætu verið dýpri en 20 metrar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/10/kortleggja_tharf_sprungurnar_gaetu_verid_dypri_en_2/ |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =26 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240326223458/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/10/kortleggja_tharf_sprungurnar_gaetu_verid_dypri_en_2/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=7 February 2024 |title=Kortlagning á sprungum í Grindavík |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/kortlagning-a-sprungum-i-grindavik/ |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> To further enhance the existing security measures in Grindavík, which involved sending SMS warning messages to residents in the zone, [[Civil defense siren|emergency warning sirens]] underwent testing prior to the March–May 2024 eruption. Warning sirens have not been sounded in Iceland since April 2000 after they were subsequently decommissioned. Instead of warning sirens, a [[Emergency population warning|dial-out system]] was developed for all mobile phones. The warning sirens were, among other things, decommissioned as they were considered expensive and unreliable in operation.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Garðarsdóttir |first=Lára |date=27 February 2024 |title="Enginn hefði trúað því að menn færu aftur í að nota viðvörunarflauturnar" |url=https://www.mannlif.is/frettir/innlent/enginn-hefdi-truad-thvi-ad-menn-faeru-aftur-i-ad-nota-vidvorunarflauturnar/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Mannlíf |language=is}}</ref> The system currently comprises three sirens in Grindavík, with additional sirens installed at the Svartsengi power station and the Blue Lagoon, which will go off in the event of an imminent eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 March 2024 |title=Tilkynning frá lögreglustjóranum á Suðurnesjum – 22.mars 2024 |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/tilkynning-fra-logreglustjoranum-a-sudurnesjum-22-mars/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref>

According to Grindavík's Planning and Environmental Department, more than 60 buildings have been classified as [[Total loss|total losses]] following the November 2023 earthquakes, with demolition already underway.<ref>{{Cite web |date=25 March 2025 |title=Niðurrif að Víðihlíð klárast í vikunni |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/nidurrif-ad-vidihlid-klarast-i-vikunni |access-date=25 March 2025 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |last2=Jónsson |first2=Smári Jökull |date=9 March 2026 |title=Niðurrif ónýtra húsa í Grindavík hafið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20262853319d/nidur-rif-o-nytra-husa-i-grinda-vik-hafid |access-date=11 March 2026 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> At least 40 buildings are expected to be demolished and the project is scheduled for completion by mid-2027.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schram |first=Höskuldur Kári |date=10 January 2026 |title=Byrja að rífa hús í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/463335 |access-date=11 January 2026 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The affected structures include part of a nursing home, a recently renovated elementary school, and a gymnasium where the largest fissure had penetrated the inside football pitch.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnason |first=Freyr |date=18 June 2024 |title=Íþróttahúsið Hópið rifið – "Mikil eftirsjá" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/18/ithrottahusid_hopid_rifid_mikil_eftirsja/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnúsdóttir |first=Ásta Hlín |date=20 June 2024 |title=Rífa þarf fjölda húsa í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/416135 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Additionally, there have been discussions about converting one of the affected buildings into a museum dedicated to the tragedies in Grindavík, highlighting the largest fault.<ref name=":67" /><ref name=":37">{{Cite web |last=Sigurðsson |first=Bjarki |date=8 September 2024 |title=Skoða að breyta Hópinu í safn |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242618271d/skoda-ad-breyta-hopinu-i-safn |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Certain buildings will not be permitted to be reconstructed or expanded on their original sites<ref name=":87" /> and some structures will be methodically dismantled and subsequently reassembled at alternative locations.<ref name=":67" /> Along one street, nearly all buildings are slated for demolition. Remedial works to restore the town to a safe and functional condition were projected for completion in the first half of 2025,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þorláksson |first=Tómas Arnar |date=16 July 2024 |title=Hefja viðgerðir á næstu dögum þrátt fyrir óvissuástand |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242597466d/hefja-vid-gerdir-a-naestu-dogum-thratt-fyrir-o-vissu-a-stand |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> with a total estimated cost of ISK 470 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|470000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}), split between the state at ISK 440 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|440000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) and the municipality at ISK 30 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|30000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref>{{Cite web |date=7 August 2024 |title=Ráðast í viðgerð innviða fyrir 470 milljónir króna |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/radast-i-vidgerd-innvida-fyrir-470-milljonir-krona |access-date=7 August 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> A future [[Town planning|municipal land-use plan]] has also been proposed that, among other measures, prohibits new construction on mapped fracture zones.<ref name=":87" /> A decision was made to fully reopen Grindavík to the public on the morning of 21 October 2024, nearly a year after the disaster began,<ref name=":46">{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=21 October 2024 |title=Grindavík opnuð: "Fólk verður að vera á varðbergi" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/10/21/grindavik_opnud_folk_verdur_ad_vera_a_vardbergi/?origin=helstu |access-date=21 October 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Adam |first=Darren |date=16 October 2024 |title=Grindavík to reopen - but go at your own risk |url=https://www.ruv.is/english/2024-10-16-grindavik-to-reopen-but-go-at-your-own-risk-424865 |access-date=16 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Yablonski |first=Steven |date=16 October 2024 |title=Grindavik reopens to residents nearly 1 year after explosive volcanic eruptions began in Iceland |url=https://www.foxweather.com/extreme-weather/grindavik-iceland-reopens-volcano-evacuation-blue-lagoon |access-date=16 October 2024 |website=[[FOX Weather]] |language=en-US}}</ref> although previous safety advice from authorities remain in effect. To further emphasise the town's return towards normal function following the public reopening, the Grindavík [[Town hall|municipal office]] also returned to Grindavík in March 2025, after its temporary relocation to Reykjavík in the wake of the November 2023 disaster.<ref name=":68" /> However, certain hazardous areas of the town remain fenced off. Three road closure gates, which had controlled all traffic into Grindavík since 10 November 2023, were removed, but could be reinstated if Alert Phase or [[State of emergency|Emergency Phase]] is reactivated.<ref name=":37" /><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sigurðsson |first1=Bjarki |last2=Kjartansson |first2=Kjartan |date=5 September 2024 |title=Fylla upp í sprungur í von um að hægt verði að opna Grindavík aftur |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242617277d/fylla-upp-i-sprungur-i-von-um-ad-haegt-verdi-ad-opna-grinda-vik-aftur |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurþórsdóttir |first=Sunna Karen |date=16 October 2024 |title=Grindavík opnuð aftur en fólk verður á eigin ábyrgð |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-10-16-grindavik-opnud-aftur-en-folk-verdur-a-eigin-abyrgd-424862 |access-date=16 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

The earthquakes in Grindavík had a profound impact on infrastructure and residences, leading to over 500 reports of property damage to the Natural Catastrophe Insurance of Iceland.<ref>{{Cite web |date=12 March 2024 |title=Status of damage assements in Grindavik |url=https://island.is/en/o/nti/news/status-of-damage-assements-in-grindavik |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=Digital Iceland |language=en}}</ref> Of these, at least 74 properties were deemed uninhabitable,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=5 March 2024 |title=Minnst 74 hús í Grindavík gjörónýt |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/406630 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> with three being completely destroyed by direct lava contact.<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2024 |title=Syðri gossprungan gott sem hjöðnuð |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/401853 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> As of November 2023, the [[insurance fund]] held approximately ISK 57 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|57000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}),<ref name=":40">{{Cite web |last=Gísladóttir |first=Hólmfríður |date=12 September 2024 |title=Heildartjón á mannvirkjum áætlað allt að 16 til 17 milljarðar |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242620041d/heildartjon-a-mannvirkjum-aaetlad-allt-ad-16-til-17-milljardar |access-date=12 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> with a total of over ISK 15 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|15000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) anticipated to be [[disbursed]] for claims related to the ongoing seismic and volcanic activity.<ref name=":45">{{Cite web |date=8 November 2024 |title=Skýrsla |url=https://www.althingi.is/altext/pdf/155/s/0331.pdf |access-date=8 November 2024 |website=Althing |publisher=[[Althing]] |language=is}}</ref> Two weeks following the beginning of the events, a thorough damage assessment was initiated and is still ongoing. The estimated damages to residential properties in the town are approximately ISK 6.5 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round| 6500000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref name=":40" /> Initially, the total cost of all structural damages was projected to reach ISK 10 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|10000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}),<ref>{{Cite web |last=Björnsson |first=Arnar |date=10 December 2023 |title=Tjónið í Grindavík gæti numið allt að 10 milljörðum |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-10-tjonid-i-grindavik-gaeti-numid-allt-ad-10-milljordum-399317 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231210190621/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-10-tjonid-i-grindavik-gaeti-numid-allt-ad-10-milljordum-399317 |archive-date=10 December 2023 |access-date=10 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> but current estimates suggest it could rise to between ISK 16 and 17 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|16000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}} and {{To USD round|17000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 May 2024 |title=Eignir NTHÍ duga ekki fyrir því sem henni er skylt að eiga |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-05-16-eignir-nthi-duga-ekki-fyrir-thvi-sem-henni-er-skylt-ad-eiga-412905 |access-date=17 May 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Since November 2023, the Icelandic government's total direct expenditure on the natural disasters in Grindavík and the surrounding area has reached roughly ISK 87 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|87000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref name=":69" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsdóttir |first=Þorgerður Anna |date=18 September 2025 |title=Náttúruhamfarir höfðu veruleg áhrif á afkomu hins opinbera |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/453788 |access-date=18 September 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

===Blue Lagoon===
[[File:BlueLagoon Noface.jpg|right|thumb|The Blue Lagoon and the Svartsengi power station behind it]]

The [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon]] is currently open, with operational hours subject to adjustments based on [[Weather forecasting|forecasts]] of wind conditions and gas emissions, as well as any potential threats to nearby roads from volcanic activity.<ref name=":15">{{Cite web |last=Bjarnadóttir |first=Amanda Guðrún |date=6 April 2024 |title=Ferðamenn flykkjast í Bláa lónið á fyrsta opnunardegi frá gosi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/409463 |access-date=6 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":20">{{Cite web |last1=Gunnarsson |first1=Oddur Ævar |last2=Pétursdóttir |first2=Lillý Valgerður |date=14 April 2024 |title=Hafa tapað milljörðum vegna endurtekinna lokana |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242556707d/hafa-tapad-milljordum-vegna-endur-tekinna-lokana |access-date=14 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The hot springs area has repeatedly been closed and evacuated due to the earthquakes and eruptions. Although it has not been significantly damaged, lava has frequently engulfed nearby roads, necessitating the construction of new ones. Following the November 2023 earthquakes, the area was closed to the public for over two months due to safety concerns.<ref name=":62" /><ref name=":63" /> The springs were closed for three weeks beginning with the onset of the March–May 2024 eruption due to their proximity to the volcanic activity and the continuous release of toxic gases.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórhallsson |first=Markús L. |date=26 March 2024 |title=Há gildi brennisteinsdíoxíðs við Bláa lónið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-03-26-ha-gildi-brennisteinsdioxids-vid-blaa-lonid-408564 |access-date=27 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=27 March 2024 |title=Hættuleg gasmengun kemur í veg fyrir opnun Bláa lónsins |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242549117d/haettu-leg-gasmengun-kemur-i-veg-fyrir-opnun-blaa-lonsins |access-date=1 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Towards the end of the same year, the springs remained inaccessible for more than two weeks due to the November–December eruption, which caused the engulfment of the parking lot and nearby roads.<ref name=":64" /><ref name=":65" /> The lava field has partially encircled the Blue Lagoon area, with the nearest lava front less than {{convert|200|m|abbr=unit}} away from the facility, which is safeguarded by an engineered barrier.<ref name=":0" /> By December 2024, more than a year after the seismic and volcanic activity began in November 2023, the Blue Lagoon remained closed for a total of four months, accounting for approximately one-third of the time since the events.<ref name=":59">{{Cite web |last=Benediktsdóttir |first=Eva Björk |date=8 December 2024 |title=Bláa lónið opið á ný – verið lokað í samtals fjóra mánuði síðasta árið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-12-08-blaa-lonid-opid-a-ny-verid-lokad-i-samtals-fjora-manudi-sidasta-arid-430465 |access-date=8 December 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

The site's management announced the site's closure to visitors from 9–16 November 2023 as a precaution following the earthquakes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Buckley |first=Julia |date=10 November 2023 |title=Iceland's Blue Lagoon closed as 1,000 earthquakes hit in 24 hours |url=https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/iceland-earthquakes-eruption-blue-lagoon/index.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231111081203/https://www.cnn.com/travel/article/iceland-earthquakes-eruption-blue-lagoon/index.html |archive-date=11 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |work=[[CNN]] |language=en-US}}</ref> Rocks dislodged by the earthquakes were reported to have fallen onto roads in the area, and 30 guests left the resort following a magnitude 4.8 tremor in the early hours of 9 November. In addition to these safety concerns, the closure was also implemented to reduce increased stress on staff. The management has assured that all staff will receive their full salaries during the closure period. Furthermore, guests who were evacuated as a result of the tremor will be provided with a full refund.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Bjarnason |first=Egill |date=9 November 2023 |title=Iceland's Blue Lagoon spa closes temporarily as earthquakes put area on alert for volcanic eruption |url=https://apnews.com/article/blue-lagoon-iceland-volcano-tourist-attraction-96b8fb04f289d261abde82751fdd1ae5 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231109175612/https://apnews.com/article/blue-lagoon-iceland-volcano-tourist-attraction-96b8fb04f289d261abde82751fdd1ae5 |archive-date=9 November 2023 |access-date=11 November 2023 |work=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Karlsson |first=Ari Páll |date=9 November 2023 |title=Bláa lóninu lokað |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-09-blaa-loninu-lokad-396079/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231218143607/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-09-blaa-loninu-lokad-396079 |archive-date=18 December 2023 |access-date=18 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Helga Árnadóttir, Director of Sales, Operations and Services at the Blue Lagoon, confirmed that the seismic activity at Svartsengi caused no visible damage or structural impairment to their facilities. The buildings, designed for earthquake resilience, remained intact and structurally sound.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=16 November 2023 |title=Engar sjáanlegar skemmdir |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/16/engar_sjaanlegar_skemmdir/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231117094024/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/16/engar_sjaanlegar_skemmdir/ |archive-date=17 November 2023 |access-date=22 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Although the geothermal spa itself remained undamaged during the first year following the onset of the disaster, its adjacent parking lot, which accommodated approximately 350 spaces along with a bus parking area and taxi stand, was overtaken by lava during the November–December 2024 eruption. No vehicles were present at the time. A temporary service facility, made from [[Prefabricated building|prefabricated]] [[Shipping container architecture|shipping containers]] and positioned on a portion of the parking lot after the erection of a protective barrier, was also destroyed by advancing lava during the eruption.<ref name=":48">{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsdóttir |first=María Sigrún |date=21 November 2024 |title=Bílastæði og þjónustuhús Bláa lónsins undir hraun |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-21-bilastaedi-og-thjonustuhus-blaa-lonsins-undir-hraun-428310 |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Additionally, a nearby [[helipad]] faced significant risk as the lava front progressed.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 November 2024 |title=Líklegt að farið verði af neyðarstigi í dag |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/22/liklegt_ad_farid_verdi_af_neydarstigi_i_dag/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> In the weeks that followed, a new temporary parking lot was constructed, though it was considerably smaller, offering around 150 parking spaces. Experts noted that a significant portion of the infrastructure in the area would likely have been destroyed if not for the timely construction of the defensive barriers.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrésdóttir |first=Iðunn |date=22 November 2024 |title=Væri komið yfir innviði ef ekki væru varnargarðar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/22/vaeri_komid_yfir_innvidi_ef_ekki_vaeru_varnargardar/ |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Following the engulfment of its old parking lot in late 2024, the Blue Lagoon began redevelopment of its surrounding facilities and environment, with construction underway and completion expected in spring 2027.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnarson |first=Baldur |date=13 September 2025 |title=Mikil uppbygging við Bláa lónið |url=https://www.mbl.is/vidskipti/frettir/2025/09/13/mikil_uppbygging_vid_blaa_lonid/ |access-date=18 September 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

The administrators of the Blue Lagoon extended the closure announcement five times after the evacuation in November 2023 due to continuing geological activity. Following permission from the chief of police, it reopened on 17 December 2023.<ref name=":62">{{Cite web |date=13 December 2023 |title=Framlengdu lokunina til 17. desember |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/13/framlengja_lokunina_til_17_desember/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231213182154/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/13/framlengja_lokunina_til_17_desember/ |archive-date=13 December 2023 |access-date=13 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Tómas |first=Ragnar |date=14 November 2023 |title=Blue Lagoon to Remain Closed Until November 30 |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/news/iceland-eruption-blue-lagoon-to-remain-closed-until-november-30/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231115194340/https://www.icelandreview.com/news/iceland-eruption-blue-lagoon-to-remain-closed-until-november-30/ |archive-date=15 November 2023 |access-date=15 November 2023 |website=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=28 November 2023 |title=Tímabundin lokun framlengd til 7. desember |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/28/timabundin_lokun_framlengd_til_7_desember/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231129083549/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/28/timabundin_lokun_framlengd_til_7_desember/ |archive-date=29 November 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=5 December 2023 |title=Stutt framlenging lokunar Bláa lónsins |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/05/stutt_framlenging_lokunar_blaa_lonsins/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231206064242/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/05/stutt_framlenging_lokunar_blaa_lonsins/ |archive-date=6 December 2023 |access-date=5 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Prior to reopening, the staff were trained in evacuation procedures to ensure preparedness for any emergencies, while the administration prepared an evacuation process, expected to take around two hours in case of emergency. Additionally, guests were to be informed about the current situation.<ref name=":63">{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=17 December 2023 |title=Ferðamenn vandlega upplýstir og starfsfólk þjálfað í rýmingu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-17-ferdamenn-vandlega-upplystir-og-starfsfolk-thjalfad-i-rymingu-399838/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231217205520/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-17-ferdamenn-vandlega-upplystir-og-starfsfolk-thjalfad-i-rymingu-399838 |archive-date=17 December 2023 |access-date=18 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Despite the advertised closure, staff members at the Blue Lagoon were observed in the spa's geothermal waters prior to the official reopening, assessing conditions.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Oddur Ævar |date=9 December 2023 |title=Meintir baðgestir í Bláa lóninu voru starfsmenn |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232501012d/meintir-badgestir-i-blaa-loninu-voru-starfs-menn |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231209113434/https://www.visir.is/g/20232501012d/meintir-badgestir-i-blaa-loninu-voru-starfs-menn |archive-date=9 December 2023 |access-date=9 December 2023 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The Blue Lagoon remained open for just two days before it closed again on 18 December 2023 after the first eruption commenced only {{convert|2–3|km|abbr=unit}} away.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Hallgerður Kolbrún E. |date=19 December 2023 |title=Bláa lónið lokað til 28. desember |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232505511d/blaa-lonid-lokad-til-28.-desem-ber |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219142342/https://www.visir.is/g/20232505511d/blaa-lonid-lokad-til-28.-desem-ber |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> All guests and staff were evacuated from the site roughly one to two hours before the eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Ólafur |date=19 December 2023 |title=Bláa lónið lokað: Allir farnir þegar gaus |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/blaa_lonid_lokad_allir_farnir_thegar_gaus/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219142324/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/blaa_lonid_lokad_allir_farnir_thegar_gaus/ |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=19 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The Blue Lagoon reopened on 6 January 2024, with all facilities back in operation by 10 January. However, it was shut down once again on 14 January and promptly evacuated, but reopened on 20 January.<ref name="ap-20240116">{{Cite news |last=Di Marco |first=Marco |date=16 January 2024 |title=Iceland faces daunting period after lava from volcano destroys homes in fishing town, president says |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-grindavik-eruption-8202c1c858baabb18251a4fa032bcac9 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240116005630/https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-grindavik-eruption-8202c1c858baabb18251a4fa032bcac9 |archive-date=16 January 2024 |access-date=16 January 2024 |publisher=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en-US}}</ref> It was safely evacuated at the start of the third eruption on 8 February 2024<ref>{{Cite news |date=8 February 2024 |title=Iceland volcano erupts again, molten rocks spew from fissures |url=https://newsinfo.inquirer.net/1901566/iceland-volcano-erupts-again-molten-rocks-spew-from-fissures |access-date=8 February 2024 |publisher=[[Philippine Daily Inquirer]] |language=en |agency=Reuters}}</ref> but the hot springs were subsequently cut off by a fast-moving lava flow engulfing the northernmost stretch of Route 426 (Norðurljósavegur), its main access road.<ref name="RUV-2024-02-08" /> On 16 February, the Blue Lagoon reopened after the [[Icelandic Road Administration]] constructed a new gravel road within the defence barriers surrounding the hot springs, restoring access that had been cut off by a lava flow from the recent eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Friðjónsdóttir |first=Hólmfríður Dagný |date=16 February 2024 |title=Opna að nýju eftir fjórðu lokunina - bókanir aðeins undir meðaltali |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/405185 |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 February 2024 |title=Vegagerðin vinnur að gerð nýs vegar innan varnargarða við Svartsengi |url=http://www.vegagerdin.is/upplysingar-og-utgafa/frettir/grindavikurvegur |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Road Administration|Icelandic Road and Coastal Administration]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Blue Lagoon's parking area.png|thumb|Blue Lagoon's new parking area inside the protective barriers]]

The Blue Lagoon was once again evacuated and temporarily closed on 2 March 2024 in anticipation of an imminent eruption<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ásgrímsson |first=Þorsteinn |date=2 March 2024 |title=600-800 í Bláa lóninu þegar rýming hófst |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/02/600_800_i_blaa_loninu_thegar_ryming_hofst/ |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =4 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240404015144/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/02/600_800_i_blaa_loninu_thegar_ryming_hofst/}}</ref> but reopened two days later after no eruption occurred.<ref>{{Cite web |date=4 March 2024 |title=Allar starfsstöðvar Bláa lónsins opnaðar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/04/allar_starfsstodvar_blaa_lonsins_opnadar/ |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =4 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240404015139/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/04/allar_starfsstodvar_blaa_lonsins_opnadar/}}</ref> The Blue Lagoon was evacuated and closed indefinitely on 16 March due to the onset of the fourth eruption. However, it reopened on 6 April after Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) was rebuilt following its engulfment by lava on 17 March.<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2024 |title=Rýming Bláa lónsins gekk vel |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/ryming-blaa-lonsins-gekk-vel |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=21 March 2024 |title=Óbreyttur kraftur á eldgosinu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-03-21-obreyttur-kraftur-a-eldgosinu-408036 |access-date=28 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":15" /> Following the fifth eruption, the Blue Lagoon was evacuated and closed 29 May–2 June 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=1 June 2024 |title=Bláa lónið opnar aftur á morgun |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242578907d/blaa-lonid-opnar-aftur-a-morgun |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 June 2024 |title=Popular geothermal spa in Iceland reopens to tourists after nearby volcano stabilises |url=https://apnews.com/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-blue-lagoon-reopened-7250f246a04851f21b8b7a48e52d8dc1 |access-date=3 June 2024 |website=[[Associated Press|AP]] |language=en}}</ref> It closed again on 8 June due to another lava engulfment of Route 43, but the road was rebuilt, and the facility reopened on 14 June.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=3 June 2024 |title=Leggja vegi yfir nýrunnið hraunið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-06-03-leggja-vegi-yfir-nyrunnid-hraunid-414760 |access-date=28 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 June 2024 |title=Búið að opna Bláa lónið á nýjan leik |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/14/buid_ad_opna_blaa_lonid_a_nyjan_leik/?utm_medium=Social&utm_campaign=mbl.is&utm_source=Facebook&fbclid=IwY2xjawGL5GhleHRuA2FlbQIxMQABHVbFAKJfr9zT6VYxaOlzt0tmtypLQQqo7yunTZWFHGSM17Rbw0sFzA2hYw_aem_31sSd5f4zrKwxrq3z-7lrw#Echobox=1718361057 |access-date=28 October 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The Blue Lagoon was evacuated once more when the sixth eruption began on 22 August 2024, but it reopened just two days later on 24 August.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 August 2024 |title=Fjöldi fólks að yfirgefa Bláa lónið og Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/22/fjoldi_folks_ad_yfirgefa_blaa_lonid_og_grindavik/ |access-date=28 October 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Árnason |first1=Eiður Þór |last2=Arnardóttir |first2=Lovísa |last3=Pétursson |first3=Vésteinn Örn |date=23 August 2024 |title=Fara af neyðarstigi og hleypa fólki í Bláa lónið og til Grindavíkur |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242611659d/fara-af-neydarstigi-og-hleypa-folki-i-blaa-lonid-og-til-grinda-vikur |access-date=10 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> On 20 November 2024, the facility closed following an eruption. The next day, lava inundated the main parking lot in front of the geothermal spa and the northern section of Route 426 (Norðurljósavegur).<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðjónsson |first=Viðar |date=21 November 2024 |title="Ný áskorun": Skoða möguleika í bílastæðamálum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/21/yfir_300_staedi_foru_undir_hraun/?origin=helstu |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The Blue Lagoon partially reopened on 6 December, approximately 16 days after its closure, with a full reopening occurring a few days later. Due to lava flow rendering the original parking area inaccessible, a temporary [[Shuttle bus|shuttle service]] was implemented, transporting visitors from [[Grindavík]] to the facility.<ref name=":64">{{Cite web |date=6 December 2024 |title=Bláa lónið opnar á ný í dag |url=https://vb.is/frettir/blaa-lonid-opnar-a-ny-i-dag/ |access-date=6 December 2024 |website=[[Viðskiptablaðið|VB]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":59" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Dagbjartsson |first=Sigurbjörn Daði |date=7 December 2024 |title=Bláa lónið ekki svo langt frá fullum afköstum |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/blaa-lonid-ekki-svo-langt-fra-fullum-afkostum |access-date=7 December 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> This arrangement remained in effect until a new parking lot, situated north of the Blue Lagoon and within the protective barriers, became available approximately one week after the reopening, following the rebuilding of Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) north of Svartsengi.<ref name=":65">{{Cite web |last=Þorláksson |first=Tómas Arnar |date=15 December 2024 |title=Nýtt bílastæði hafi kostað gríðarlegt fjármagn |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242664271d/nytt-bila-staedi-hafi-kostad-gridar-legt-fjar-magn |access-date=16 December 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=16 December 2024 |title=Rýkur úr varnargörðum sem verja nýtt bílastæði Bláa lónsins |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/12/16/rykur_ur_varnargordum_sem_verja_nytt_bilastaedi_bla/ |access-date=16 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Following a brief and minor eruption on 1 April 2025, the geothermal spa closed for the day and resumed operations on 2 April.<ref>{{Cite web |date=1 April 2025 |title=Búið að rýma Bláa lónið |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/buid-ad-ryma-blaa-lonid?fbclid=IwY2xjawJkLqlleHRuA2FlbQIxMQABHutOjMClSIZv5nUY7hDgROVtlyixV5ibaLXQcP9NJT_jn5f7fzH-v03jp6jz_aem_6ZhRdZXzhAPMxl4_kwDV5w |access-date=10 April 2025 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ísleifsson |first=Atli |date=2 April 2025 |title=Grindvíkingum hleypt inn í bæinn á ný |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252709405d/grindvikingum-hleypt-inn-i-baeinn-a-ny |access-date=12 April 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Later that same year, the Blue Lagoon was closed and evacuated early on 16 July due to the imminent eruption, but reopened again in the evening of the same day.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsdóttir |first=Þorgerður Anna |date=16 July 2025 |title=Grindvíkingar fá að fara heim og Bláa lónið hefur verið opnað |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/448873 |access-date=23 July 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> As with previous evacuations, guests of the Blue Lagoon's Retreat Hotel were temporarily relocated to other hotels.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Halldórsdóttir |first=Magnea Marín |date=16 July 2025 |title=Öllum gestum komið fyrir á öðrum hótelum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/07/16/ollum_gestum_komid_fyrir_a_odrum_hotelum/ |access-date=26 July 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

The chief of police stated that maintaining operations at the Blue Lagoon is scarcely justifiable amidst an ongoing eruption due to the risk of [[air pollution]] compromising public health.<ref>{{Cite web |date=22 March 2024 |title=Starfsemi í Bláa lóninu "vart forsvaranleg" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/22/starfsemi_i_blaa_loninu_vart_forsvaranleg/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325022201/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/22/starfsemi_i_blaa_loninu_vart_forsvaranleg/}}</ref> Administrators, in consultation with authorities, regularly assessed whether it was safe to reopen the geothermal spa.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Friðjónsdóttir |first=Hólmfríður Dagný |date=26 March 2024 |title=Líklegt að lokun Bláa lónsins verði framlengd aftur |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/408615 |access-date=6 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Hallgerður Kolbrún E. |date=4 April 2024 |title=Funda um opnun Bláa lónsins í fyrramálið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242552047d/funda-um-opnun-blaa-lonsins-i-fyrra-malid |access-date=6 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> On 20 March 2024, a staff member at the Blue Lagoon was hospitalised due to symptoms of [[Toxicity#Environmental hazards|gas poisoning]] amidst the eruption that began on 16 March, which had resulted in elevated levels of [[sulfur dioxide]]. The illness occurred while the employee was working at the hot springs.<ref name=":12">{{Cite web |last=Þórisdóttir |first=Anna Lilja |date=21 March 2024 |title=Starfsmaður Bláa lónsins fékk gaseitrun |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/408085 |access-date=21 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Though initial reports expressed concern for their health, the staff member ultimately made a full recovery.<ref name=":13">{{Cite web |date=21 March 2024 |title=Brugðið en starfsmaðurinn á batavegi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/21/brugdid_en_starfsmadurinn_a_batavegi/ |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =2 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240402221949/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/21/brugdid_en_starfsmadurinn_a_batavegi/}}</ref>

In early August 2024, firefighters conducted drills using newly acquired high-capacity fire hoses to apply geothermal [[effluent]] from the Blue Lagoon onto solidified lava. These hoses, procured at a cost of nearly ISK 500 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|500000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) from the [[United Kingdom]], extend to about {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}} and discharge approximately {{convert|750|L/s|gal/s|abbr=unit}}, with a vertical reach of up to {{convert|150|m|abbr=unit}}—substantially surpassing the previous system's {{convert|35|m|abbr=unit}} elevation. This sophisticated equipment was purchased following successful field trials conducted in late June 2024, during which the application of water to active lava flows yielded promising results. In response to these outcomes, authorities opted to invest in this advanced [[water pumping]] technology. Additionally, the fire department established a new role, "Lava Cooling Systems Manager", to oversee the operation and maintenance of the specialised equipment.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ómarsdóttir |first=Alma |date=2 August 2024 |title=Nýr hraunkælingarbúnaður settur upp til að verja Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/418915 |access-date=10 August 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=3 August 2024 |title=Tíu tommu slöngur til að kæla hraunið |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/tiu-tommu-slongur-til-ad-kaela-hraunid |access-date=11 August 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sverrisson |first=Ólafur Björn |date=8 August 2024 |title=Dæla vatni úr Bláa lóninu á margföldum krafti |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242605661d/daela-vatni-ur-blaa-loninu-a-marg-foldum-krafti |access-date=10 August 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

In the north-western section of the protective barriers surrounding the Svartsengi area, which includes the Blue Lagoon, the situation became critical during the November–December 2024 eruption as lava approached the point of overspilling. To mitigate the risk, 12 high-powered monitoring [[Water cannon|water cannons]] were installed along a {{convert|150|m|abbr=unit}} stretch of the barrier's top edge, although not all were utilised simultaneously. Unlike conventional [[fire engine]] systems, these cannons were directly connected to excess water discharge from the [[Svartsengi power station]], with a continuous supply potential of approximately {{convert|433|L/s|gal/s|abbr=unit}}. This excess water, a byproduct of the power station that also formed the [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon]], was used in a system implemented in collaboration with multiple [[Fire department|fire departments]] to effectively cool the lava, successfully stabilising the flow and halting its progression. Officials subsequently declared the intervention a success as the lava ceased further encroachment.<ref name=":21" /><ref name=":55" />

===Svartsengi power station===
[[File:Blue Lagoon Iceland hdsr 2019 10 19 9999 332.jpg|right|thumb|The Svartsengi power station in 2019]]

The [[Svartsengi power station]] was unoccupied and remotely controlled from the [[Reykjanes power station]] during the initial months following the November 2023 disaster. In 2024, the station was evacuated multiple times due to imminent eruptions and other perceived threats to staff safety.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=18 March 2024 |title=Orkuverið rýmt vegna mengunar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/18/orkuverid_rymt_vegna_mengunar/ |access-date=23 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =23 May 2024|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20240523025230/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/18/orkuverid_rymt_vegna_mengunar/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=21 May 2024 |title=Starfsfólk sent heim í öryggisskyni |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/21/starfsfolk_sent_heim_i_oryggisskyni/ |access-date=23 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =23 May 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240523184101/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/21/starfsfolk_sent_heim_i_oryggisskyni/}}</ref> It experienced minimal impact from the earthquakes, with the only damage being "the interior furnishings and exterior walls, as well as considerable cracking in pathways and remote areas of the site". The power station continued to operate at its usual production levels for hot and cold water, as well as electricity,<ref name=":6">{{Cite web |last=Ketilsson |first=Páll |date=1 December 2023 |title=Þrekvirki unnið við að halda framleiðslunni órofinni og efla á sama tíma varnir fyrirtækisins |url=https://www.vf.is/vidskipti/threkvirki-unnid-vid-ad-halda-framleidslunni-orofinni-og-efla-a-sama-tima-varnir-fyrirtaekisins |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231208031400/https://www.vf.is/vidskipti/threkvirki-unnid-vid-ad-halda-framleidslunni-orofinni-og-efla-a-sama-tima-varnir-fyrirtaekisins |archive-date=8 December 2023 |access-date=8 December 2023 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> despite sustaining minor damage to the hot-water pipeline leading to Grindavík in the days preceding the 10 November 2023 earthquake swarm.<ref name=":57" /> Approximately 30,000 residents on the peninsula—representing about eight percent of Iceland's population—face the risk of losing essential heating if lava engulfs the facility, a situation that would persist until a new power plant is constructed.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pomrenke |first=Erik |date=6 November 2023 |title=Information Meeting Held on Potential Eruption |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/news/information-meeting-held-on-potential-eruption/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240103004139/https://www.icelandreview.com/news/information-meeting-held-on-potential-eruption/ |archive-date=3 January 2024 |access-date=3 January 2024 |website=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en}}</ref> In November 2023, construction commenced on earth barriers designed to protect the power station by redirecting potential future lava flows. Additionally, transmission towers were elevated to accommodate the presence of the barriers.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2023 |title=Vinna varnargarða í kappi við tímann |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/21/vinna_varnargarda_i_kappi_vid_timann/ |access-date=5 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Rasmussen |first1=Louise |last2=Birkebaek |first2=Johannes |date=31 January 2024 |title=Race against time: Icelanders build towering structures to stop lava |url=https://www.csmonitor.com/Environment/2024/0131/Race-against-time-Icelanders-build-towering-structures-to-stop-lava |access-date=31 January 2024 |website=[[Christian Science Monitor]] |language=en-US |agency=Reuters}}</ref>

Construction on an extension of the power plant began in late 2022 following extensive preparation, with an initial projected completion date of late 2025. Despite the start of events in November 2023, including earthquakes and volcanic eruptions, there was almost no change to the original plans,<ref>{{Cite web |date=8 September 2024 |title=Hornsteinn í héraði í hálfa öld |url=https://www.vf.is/vidskipti/hornsteinn-i-heradi-i-halfa-old |access-date=5 October 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=11 October 2024 |title=Góður gangur í Svartsengi |url=https://www.vf.is/vidskipti/godur-gangur-i-svartsengi |access-date=11 October 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> and the project was successfully completed on schedule at the end of 2025.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Sjöunda orkuverið gangsett í Svartsengi|url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/sjoeunda-orkuverid-gangsett-i-svartsengi/|website=[[HS Orka]]|access-date=1 December 2025|language=is|first=|last=|date=1 December 2025}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=Afrek að verkáætlun hafi staðist samhliða níu eldgosum|url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252811417d/af-rek-ad-verk-a-aetlun-hafi-stadist-sam-hlida-niu-eld-gosum|website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]]|date=2 December 2025|access-date=2 December 2025|language=is|first=Lovísa|last=Arnardóttir}}</ref> Before the January 2024 eruption, [[HS Orka]], the operator of the Svartsengi power station, introduced a novel early-warning system that may be a first in volcanic monitoring. This system issued an essential alert more than four hours before the eruption. Since November 2023, the boreholes, pre-dating their use in the advanced system, have been pivotal in predicting volcanic activities. Detecting pressure fluctuations, these boreholes have reliably signalled all eruptions in the series, demonstrating their pre-existing potential in volcanic surveillance and safety.<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 January 2024 |title=Með puttann á púlsinum í aðdraganda eldgoss |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/kynning/2024/01/24/med_puttann_a_pulsinum_i_addraganda_eldgoss/ |access-date=24 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325000515/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/kynning/2024/01/24/med_puttann_a_pulsinum_i_addraganda_eldgoss/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=5 February 2024 |title=Borholur gerðu viðvart um eldgosin |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404094 |access-date=24 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pétursson |first=Vésteinn Örn |date=3 March 2024 |title=Verulega líkur á öðru innskoti á allra næstu dögum |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242537202d/veru-legar-likur-a-odru-inn-skoti-a-allra-naestu-dogum |access-date=24 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> The boreholes in the Svartsengi area not considered likely to affect the timing or characteristics of volcanic eruptions. Most of the boreholes extend to depths of approximately {{convert|2.5|km|abbr=unit}},<ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Ólafur |date=28 January 2026 |title=Kerfið hlaðið til næstu áratuga eða -hundraða |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2026/01/28/kerfid_hladid_til_naestu_aratuga_eda_hundrada/ |access-date=28 January 2026 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> whereas the magma chamber beneath the area is estimated to begin at a depth of around {{convert|4|km|abbr=unit}}.<ref name=":89" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pálsson |first=Ólafur |date=9 February 2026 |title=Boranir hafa engin áhrif á jarðhræringar |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2026/02/09/boranir_hafa_engin_ahrif_a_jardhraeringar/ |access-date=10 February 2026 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> [[Fibre-optic cable]]s have also been used to detect and characterise seismic and volcanic activity—tracking tremors, dike intrusions, and other magmatic processes.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Li |first1=Jiaxuan |last2=Biondi |first2=Ettore |last3=Heimisson |first3=Elías Rafn |last4=Puel |first4=Simone |last5=Zhai |first5=Qiushi |last6=Zhang |first6=Shane |last7=Hjörleifsdóttir |first7=Vala |last8=Wei |first8=Xiaozhuo |last9=Bird |first9=Elijah |last10=Klesh |first10=Andy |last11=Kamalov |first11=Valey |last12=Gunnarsson |first12=Theodór |last13=Geirsson |first13=Halldór |last14=Zhan |first14=Zhongwen |date=24 April 2025 |title=Minute-scale dynamics of recurrent dike intrusions in Iceland with fibre-optic geodesy |url=https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.adu0225 |journal=Science |volume=388 |issue=6752 |pages=1189–1193 |doi=10.1126/science.adu0225|pmid=40273283 |bibcode=2025Sci...388.1189L |url-access=subscription }}</ref>

During the January 2024 eruption, lava flowed over both the hot water and cold [[water pipelines]] connecting the Svartsengi power station to Grindavík. These pipelines sustained damage, resulting in a temporary service cutoff. Prior to the January 2024 eruption, preparations were underway to install a new, more resilient hot-water pipeline as a precaution against future lava flows. However, the protective covering was not yet completed, leaving both pipelines exposed when lava flowed over them. Despite the damage, the hot water supply was partially restored through the recent pipeline within two days, even though it had buckled under the intense heat of the lava, causing a significant leak of {{convert|40–50|L/s|gal/s|abbr=unit}} from the water main—approximately half of the total supply capacity to Grindavík.<ref name="myndir-ur-lofti-15-02-2024" /><ref name=":60" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=2 February 2024 |title=Afkastageta í Svartsengi í hámarki og mögulega þarf að skammta heitt vatn |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/afkastageta-i-svartsengi-i-hamarki-og-mogulega-tharf-ad-skammta-heitt-vatn |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> Around six weeks later, a new bypass pipeline was constructed over the recently formed lava field, similar to the operation carried out north of the Svartsengi area following the February 2024 eruption.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 February 2024 |title=Lokað fyrir heitt vatn í Grindavík á meðan hjáveitulögn verður tengd. |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/lokad-fyrir-heitt-vatn-i-grindavik-a-medan-hjaveitulogn-verdur-tengd/ |access-date=10 December 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref> As with all defence barriers, the project was overseen by [[Civil defense|Civil Defence]].<ref name=":61" /> Simultaneously, an underground power cable was severely affected by the January 2024 eruption due to the immense heat and pressure exerted by the overlying lava, causing a temporary outage in half of the town. However, as the town was largely uninhabited at the time, the impact of the outage did not constitute an emergency. Grindavík operated on reserve power while the situation unfolded. To enhance the town's electricity resilience, a new overhead power line on [[Transmission tower|pylons]] was constructed above the recent lava field, restoring power more than a week later. This design allows for quicker repairs should future lava flows damage the infrastructure.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Þorgils |date=22 January 2024 |title=Rafmagn komið á Grindavík um nýja loftlínu |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-01-22-rafmagn-komid-a-grindavik-um-nyja-loftlinu-403130 |access-date=9 December 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 January 2024 |title=Rafmagn komið á Grindavík um loftlínu yfir hraunið |url=https://www.hsveitur.is/um-hs-veitur/frettir/almennar-frettir/rafmagn-komid-a-grindavik-um-loftlinu-yfir-hraunid/ |access-date=9 December 2024 |website=HS Veitur |language=is}}</ref>

The eruption of 8 February 2024 produced a lava flow that came close to the power station. It cut off the northern access road to the geothermal plant (though the southern access road was unaffected) and destroyed part of the Njarðvíkuræð [[pipeline]] supplying hot water from Svartsengi to [[Reykjanesbær]], [[Suðurnesjabær]], [[Vogar]], and [[Grindavík]].<ref name="RUV-2024-02-08" /> Around 20,000 people in the area were reported to have been cut off from the hot water supply. Residents of the peninsula were warned to use hot water and electricity sparingly. Schools similarly affected were closed. [[Keflavík Airport]] also suffered utility interruptions on the day of the volcanic event. The hot-water system went offline, and later that same day a separate fault in the cold-water main further disrupted services. Cold-water flow wasn't reinstated until 9 February,<ref name=":83" /> and hot water only resumed on 12 February.<ref name=":82" /> Although flight schedules remained unaffected, a number of [[Public toilet|lavatories]] stayed closed because of the cold-water shortage; staff placed [[Fan heater|portable heaters]] and handed out blankets as the indoor temperature dropped. The airport's electrical network, however, continued to operate without issue.<ref name=":84" /> Despite significant damage caused by the eruption on local infrastructure, the Capital Region was unaffected as it operates within a different [[geothermal energy]] [[Hot water distribution system|distribution system]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Helgadóttir |first=Ragnhildur |date=9 February 2024 |title=Heitt vatn til höfuðborgarsvæðisins kemur úr fjórum áttum |url=https://heimildin.is/grein/20832/ |url-access=subscription |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=Heimildin |language=is}}</ref> The Icelandic government declared a [[state of emergency]] in response to the hot water crisis.<ref name=":16">{{Cite news |last1=Armstrong |first1=Kathryn |last2=Casey |first2=Ian |date=8 February 2024 |title=State of emergency declared in Iceland after volcanic eruption |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-68237327 |access-date=8 February 2024 |work=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> While the [[Reykjanes power station]] and the wider Icelandic grid can supply electricity if the Svartsengi line is cut off, the capability to deliver hot water, a critical function of the Svartsengi power station, cannot be replicated.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Gunnarsson |first1=Oddur Ævar |last2=Bernharðsdóttir |first2=Berghildur Erla |date=16 November 2023 |title=Orkuverinu í Svartsengi verði fjarstýrt til áramóta |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20232490272d/orku-verinu-i-svarts-engi-verdi-fjar-styrt-til-ara-mota |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231203043854/https://www.visir.is/g/20232490272d/orku-verinu-i-svarts-engi-verdi-fjar-styrt-til-ara-mota |archive-date=3 December 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=7 November 2023 |title=Varaleiðir settar upp og fjarstýring gerð möguleg |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/07/varaleidir_settar_upp_og_fjarstyring_gerd_moguleg/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223859/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/07/varaleidir_settar_upp_og_fjarstyring_gerd_moguleg/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=30 November 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> At the time of the eruption, temperatures in the area reached as low as {{convert|-14|C|abbr=unit|sigfig=2}}. The shortages contributed to the closure of schools, public pools and sport facilities in the area on 9 February.<ref>{{Cite web |date=9 February 2024 |title=Icelanders race to repair damage after volcano damage |url=https://www.france24.com/en/live-news/20240209-icelanders-race-to-repair-damage-after-volcano-damage |access-date=9 February 2024 |website=[[France 24]] |language=en |agency=[[Agence France-Presse|AFP]]}}</ref>

[[File:Hot-water pipeline and power lines in Svartsengi.png|thumb|Hot-water pipeline and power lines leading from Svartsengi power station through the protective barriers]]

The repair and reconnection of Njarðvíkuræð hot-water pipeline was completed on the afternoon of 9 February 2024, approximately 30 hours after it was breached, restoring the supply of hot water to the wider [[Reykjanes|Reykjanes area]]. Workers managed to splice together the undamaged sections of the old pipeline with the new underground system, often working through the night.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Vatn komið aftur á Njarðvíkuræðina |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/vatn_komid_aftur_a_njardvikuraedina/ |access-date=9 February 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =9 February 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240209210800/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/vatn_komid_aftur_a_njardvikuraedina/}}</ref> In the weeks leading up to the February 2024 eruption, workers had been constructing an underground bypass pipeline parallel to an older one, designed to be more resistant to damage from lava flows. This was done before any lava had reached the area. The plan was to link the intact section of the old pipeline, unaffected by the lava, to the new underground pipeline at both ends, outside the lava field formed in the February eruption.<ref name=":61">{{Cite web |date=22 January 2024 |title=Njarðvíkuræðin að hluta í jörð til að verjast hraunrennsli |url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/njardvikuraedin-ad-hluta-i-joerd-til-ad-verjast-hraunrennsli/ |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[HS Orka]] |language=is}}</ref> Despite these efforts, the newly constructed bypass pipeline collapsed under the pressure of the lava flow late evening on 9 February.<ref>{{Cite web |date=10 February 2024 |title=Leggja nýja hjáveitulögn yfir hraunið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/10/leggja_nyja_hjaveitulogn_yfir_hraunid/ |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =10 February 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240210163849/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/10/leggja_nyja_hjaveitulogn_yfir_hraunid/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Þórhallsson |first=Markús Þ. |date=10 February 2024 |title=Hjáveitulögnin fór í sundur — vinna við nýja tekur nokkra daga |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404645 |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Immediately, workers started to construct a new pipeline, which was placed on top of the fresh lava. Around 50 hours later, on 12 February, water began flowing through the new pipeline.<ref name=":82">{{Cite web |last=Valsson |first=Andri Yrkill |date=12 February 2024 |title=Heitt vatn streymir í tanka og hitaveitan fer í gang |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404795 |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=12 February 2024 |title=Vatn tekið að streyma í heitavatnstanka á Fitjum |url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/vatn-tekid-ad-streyma-i-heitavatnstanka-a-fitjum/ |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[HS Orka]] |language=is}}</ref> A few weeks after the success, workers completed burying the new pipeline in the fresh lava, ensuring it is now underground and protected from potential future lava flows.<ref>{{Cite web |date=27 February 2024 |title=Njarðvíkuræðin tilbúin undir næsta gos |url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/njardvikuraedin-tilbuin-undir-naesta-gos/ |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[HS Orka]] |language=is}}</ref> Lava from the May–June 2024 eruption came within {{convert|700|m|abbr=unit}} of the power station's pipeline in the north, although it caused no danger. Authorities stated that they were prepared for the possibility of lava flowing over the pipeline, as it had been reinforced with [[soil]] fill.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=9 June 2024 |title=Áætlanir gera ráð fyrir að hraun fari yfir lagnir |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242582585d/a-aetlanir-gera-rad-fyrir-ad-hraun-fari-yfir-lagnir |access-date=25 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> In the November–December 2024 eruption, lava flowed over the pipeline without causing any damage, although it was stated that it would have been "very serious" if the pipeline had been compromised.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gísladóttir |first=Hólmfríður |date=21 November 2024 |title=Verði að koma í ljós hvort fergjun Njarðvíkuræðar heldur |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242652842d/verdi-ad-koma-i-ljos-hvort-fergjun-njardvikuraedar-heldur |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bjarnason |first=Freyr |date=21 November 2024 |title=Mjög alvarlegt ef Njarðvíkuræð gefur sig |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/21/mjog_alvarlegt_ef_njardvikuraed_gefur_sig/ |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

The prospect of protecting the Njarðvíkuræð hot-water pipeline leading from the plant was considered extremely challenging, as it was situated above ground on [[Stilts (architecture)|stilts]]. Prior to the eruption in February 2024, plans were established to bury a new underground bypass pipeline, shielding it from potential lava damage, and preparing it for potential connection to the ends of the old pipeline in the event of engulfment.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Thorlacius |first=Ragnhildur |date=20 December 2023 |title=Byrjað á hjáveituaðgerð á Njarðvíkuræð |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-20-byrjad-a-hjaveituadgerd-a-njardvikuraed-400324/ |access-date=8 February 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The eruption on 8 February resulted in the destruction of a segment of the old pipeline just north of Svartsengi and the subsequent collapse of a section of the new underground bypass pipeline on 9 February.<ref name="RUV-2024-02-08" /> Svartsengislína power line leading from the power station had previously been protected with barriers around the base of the [[Transmission tower|transmission towers]]; when the same eruption encircled the structures north of the power station, causing no damage.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Einarsdóttir |first=Gréta Sigríður |date=9 February 2024 |title=Svartsengislína slapp með skrekkinn |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/404612 |access-date=4 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Following the February 2024 eruption, authorities decided to alter the route of the power line north of Svartsengi and construct three new transmission towers farther east of the original path. This adjustment was made to reduce the risk of future damage from lava flows.<ref name=":58">{{Cite web |date=13 February 2024 |title=Framkvæmdir við þrjú möstur í Svartsengislínu ganga vel |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/framkvaemdir-vid-thrju-mostur-i-svartsengislinu-ganga-vel |access-date=5 December 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Hallgerður Kolbrún E. |date=27 February 2024 |title=Vona að jarðvegur verndi nýja lögn renni hraun aftur sömu leið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242535338d/vona-ad-jard-vegur-verndi-nyja-logn-renni-hraun-aftur-somu-leid |access-date=5 December 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> However, the same power line south of the power station was severely damaged in the May–June 2024 eruption, as several transmission towers caught fire from the intense heat of the advancing lava, and one ultimately collapsed after its foundation was compromised.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Stefánsson |first=Jón Þór |date=30 May 2024 |title=Myndasyrpa: Vegurinn endar í hrauninu |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242578087d/f/skodanir |access-date=26 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=4 June 2024 |title=Rafmagn kemst á Grindavík síðar í vikunni |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/04/rafmagn_kemst_a_grindavik_sidar_i_vikunni/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> West of this zone, a [[guy-wire]] from a mast at a [[NATO]] [[telecommunications facility]] snapped due to the proximity of the same lava flow, posing a temporary threat to the station. A barrier had previously been constructed specifically to protect the telecommunications station.<ref name=":0" />

In early summer 2024, a new low-temperature [[Well#Drilled wells|geothermal well]] was discovered as part of an experimental project led by [[HS Orka]], [[Verkís]], [[Iceland GeoSurvey]], and more contractors, under the leadership of the Ministry of the Environment, Energy, and Climate. The initiative was launched following an emergency in February 2024, when the entire [[Southern Peninsula (Iceland)|Suðurnes Peninsula]] lost its hot water supply for several days due to lava flow disrupting the main pipeline from the Svartsengi power station to local towns. Prior to this incident, authorities had not planned to identify new backup boreholes. The newly discovered well, located in the Rockville Zone north of Keflavík Airport—an area formerly occupied by a United States Army [[radar station]] from 1953 to 1997—produces approximately {{convert|50|L/s|gal/s|abbr=unit}} of water at temperatures exceeding {{convert|85|C|abbr=unit|sigfig=2}}. This breakthrough in geothermal resource development has been hailed as a significant enhancement to the peninsula's energy security. In addition to this discovery, two other boreholes have been drilled at separate locations to prepare for the construction of more emergency [[Heating plant|heating plants]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=31 January 2025 |title=Neyðarhitaveita á Suðurnesjum |url=https://www.hsveitur.is/um-hs-veitur/frettir/almennar-frettir/neydarhitaveita-a-sudurnesjum/ |access-date=15 February 2025 |website=HS Veitur |language=is}}</ref> These boreholes are the first to be drilled in the area since the Svartsengi power station was established in the late 1970s. With the new well, it is now possible to supply hot water to local towns should the Svartsengi power station be disrupted—or in the worst-case scenario, if the station were to be engulfed by an eruption. It is important to note, however, that the output from this emergency borehole is limited and is primarily intended to prevent freezing damage to pipes in local properties. The first of these heating plants became operational in February 2025, with another currently under development.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=21 June 2024 |title=Ný heitavatnshola gjörbreytir stöðunni á Suðurnesjunum |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242587894d/ny-heita-vatns-hola-gjor-breytir-stodunni-a-sudur-nesjunum |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsson |first=Þorgils |date=23 June 2024 |title=Ný borhola gæti tryggt hita á Suðurnesjum ef aftur lokast fyrir Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/416304 |access-date=19 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kjartansson |first=Kjartan |date=9 September 2024 |title=Geta haldið bæjum á Reykjanesi frostfríum með nýfundnum jarðhita |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242618626d/geta-haldid-baejum-a-reykja-nesi-frost-frium-med-ny-fundnum-jard-hita |access-date=9 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=14 February 2025 |title=Nýjung notist aðeins í neyðartilvikum |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-02-14-nyjung-notist-adeins-i-neydartilvikum-436293 |access-date=15 February 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Earlier, in November 2023, efforts had already begun in the municipality of [[Garður]] to establish a backup [[water source]], serving as a precautionary measure in case the water collection facility near the Blue Lagoon is affected by future lava flows.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sumarliðadóttir |first=Karlotta Líf |date=19 November 2023 |title="Þetta eru gríðarlega miklir hagsmunir" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/19/thetta_eru_gridarlega_miklir_hagsmunir/ |access-date=5 December 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

During the November–December 2024 eruption, two transmission towers north of Svartsengi were at significant risk as lava drew closer to the protective filling at their bases, slowly approaching their [[concrete foundation|concrete foundations]] and [[steel frame|steel frames]].<ref name=":51" /> One of the towers was eventually destroyed by the advancing lava flow;<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=25 November 2024 |title=Vinna við að koma upp nýju rafmagnsmastri við Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-25-vinna-vid-ad-koma-upp-nyju-rafmagnsmastri-vid-svartsengi-428777 |access-date=25 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> however, it was an older structure belonging to the original power line route.<ref name=":58" /> Mitigation measures included deploying a [[fire truck]] to direct water jets onto the lava flow, with the goal of reducing its speed or altering its course to preserve the tower's structural stability.<ref name=":52" /> Severe thermal exposure from the lava caused the [[Electrical conductor|electrical conductors]] to tear and separate, temporarily disrupting [[Mains electricity|electricity supply]] to Grindavík, a town that has faced frequent [[Power outage|power outages]] during the ongoing volcanic series activity.<ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2024 |title=Viðgerðir munu taka nokkra daga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/21/vidgerdir_munu_taka_nokkra_daga/ |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Signýjardóttir |first=Ástrós |date=21 November 2024 |title=Rafmagnsleysi í Grindavík en vonast til að Njarðvíkuræð haldi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-21-rafmagnsleysi-i-grindavik-en-vonast-til-ad-njardvikuraed-haldi-428211 |access-date=21 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> In response, the power supply was automatically rerouted to the [[Reykjanes power station]] as a [[fail-safe]] backup system, ensuring near constant electricity delivery to the peninsula. Subsequently, a decision was made to construct a taller transmission tower at the inner edge of the defence barriers, with plans to elevate the existing electrical conductors by {{convert|12–14|m|abbr=unit}} above ground outside the barrier to mitigate the risk of future lava flow damage.<ref name=":21" /><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 November 2024|title=Lagnir undir hrauni halda vel|url=https://www.hsorka.is/um-okkur/frettir/almennar-frettir/lagnir-undir-hrauni-halda-vel/|access-date=24 November 2024|website=[[HS Orka]]|language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=21 November 2024 |title=Njarðvíkuræðin undir hrauni |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/njardvikuraedin-undir-hrauni |access-date=23 November 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> During the November–December 2024 eruption, lava advanced toward a [[Source water protection|water collection area]] operated by HS Veitur, which supplies cold water to [[Reykjanesbær]] and [[Suðurnesjabær]] on the Reykjanes Peninsula. Despite its proximity, the site was not at immediate risk.<ref name=":49" />

=== Barriers and roads ===
[[File:Protective barrier at Grindavík.jpg|thumb|A design of the first phase of the protective barriers around Grindavík]]

A strategic plan for the construction of protective barriers had been established well in advance of the recent seismic disturbances in the area.<ref name=":4" /> The initiation of this plan was placed on hold, as there was no pressing threat of a volcanic eruption, coupled with the significant environmental repercussions involved. However, on 10 November 2023, with earthquake activity intensifying, a fleet of [[lorries]] began transporting materials to the designated site near the Blue Lagoon and the Svartsengi power station. This operation was promptly suspended due to concerns that a potential eruption later that day could put workers in danger.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Másson |first=Agnar Már |date=11 November 2023 |title=Varnargarðar við Grindavík í endurskoðun |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/varnargardar_vid_grindavik_i_endurskodun/ |access-date=14 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =13 December 2023|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20231213133148/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/11/varnargardar_vid_grindavik_i_endurskodun/}}</ref><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Sigtryggsdóttir |first1=Fjóla G. |last2=Hrafnsdóttir |first2=Hörn |last3=Steingrímsson |first3=Jón Haukur |last4=Guðmundsson |first4=Ari |title=Experience in diverting and containing lava flow by barriers constructed from in situ material during the 2021 Geldingardalir volcanic eruption |journal=Bulletin of Volcanology |date=18 March 2025 |volume=87 |issue=4 |article-number=28 |doi=10.1007/s00445-025-01806-3|doi-access=free |bibcode=2025BVol...87...28S }}</ref> The construction work was officially resumed on 13 November, following the [[Althing|Althing's]] approval of a bill proposed by then-Prime Minister [[Katrín Jakobsdóttir]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite web |title=534/154 stjórnarfrumvarp: vernd mikilvægra innviða á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.althingi.is/altext/154/s/0534.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223910/https://www.althingi.is/altext/154/s/0534.html |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |website=[[Althing]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last1=Signýjardóttir |first1=Ástrós |last2=Kristjánsson |first2=Alexander |date=14 November 2023 |title=Hægt að hefja framkvæmdir við varnargarða |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-14-haegt-ad-hefja-framkvaemdir-vid-varnargarda-396970/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223905/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-11-14-haegt-ad-hefja-framkvaemdir-vid-varnargarda-396970 |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=30 November 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Following this, workers began assembling filler materials into barriers at the planned sites.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=14 November 2023 |title=Undirbúningur á fullu við gerð varnargarða |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/14/undirbuningur_a_fullu_vid_gerd_varnargarda/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223922/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/14/undirbuningur_a_fullu_vid_gerd_varnargarda/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The construction of the barriers was almost complete when the 18 December 2023 eruption occurred. Once it was safe for workers to resume, they were quickly sent back to the site to finish the remaining filling work on the barriers.<ref name=":7">{{Cite news |last=Ásgrímsson |first=Þorsteinn |date=19 December 2023 |title=Heimilt að rjúfa veginn og fylla nú í skarðið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/heimilt_ad_rjufa_veginn_og_fylla_nu_i_skardid/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219032132/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/heimilt_ad_rjufa_veginn_og_fylla_nu_i_skardid/ |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=20 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

Construction of protective barriers around Grindavík, for which plans were already in place prior to the eruptions,<ref name="mbl-20231123">{{Cite news |date=23 November 2023 |title=Hönnun fleiri varnargarða fyrir Grindavík lokið |trans-title=Design of additional defence walls for Grindavík completed |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/23/honnun_fleiri_varnargarda_fyrir_grindavik_lokid/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223915/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/23/honnun_fleiri_varnargarda_fyrir_grindavik_lokid/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=3 January 2024 |work=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> officially began on 2 January 2024.<ref name="ruv-20240102">{{Cite news |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=2 January 2024 |title=Byrjað að reisa varnargarða við Grindavík |trans-title=Commenced the construction of defence walls at Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-01-02-byrjad-ad-reisa-varnargarda-vid-grindavik-401054/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240110145733/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-01-02-byrjad-ad-reisa-varnargarda-vid-grindavik-401054 |archive-date=10 January 2024 |access-date=3 January 2024 |work=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The project, initiated from the eastern end, involved erecting a barrier extending over {{convert|2|km|abbr=unit}}. The first phase of construction aimed to reach an average height of around {{convert|4|m|abbr=unit}}, although this may vary in different locations. The design of the barrier, guided by [[Simulation modeling|lava flow simulations]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Magnúsdóttir |first=Ásta Hlín |date=19 December 2023 |title=Samkvæmt nýju hraunflæðilíkani nálgast hraun Grindavíkurveg á jóladag |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-19-samkvaemt-nyju-hraunflaedilikani-nalgast-hraun-grindavikurveg-a-joladag-400281/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231220044611/https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-19-samkvaemt-nyju-hraunflaedilikani-nalgast-hraun-grindavikurveg-a-joladag-400281 |archive-date=20 December 2023 |access-date=20 December 2023 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> specifically targeted the areas at highest risk.<ref name="mbl-20240102">{{Cite news |last=Másson |first=Agnar Már |date=2 January 2024 |title=Vinna hafin við Grindavíkurgarðinn |trans-title=Work has begun at Grindavíkurgarðinn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/02/vinna_hafin_vid_grindavikurgardinn/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240117131346/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/02/vinna_hafin_vid_grindavikurgardinn/ |archive-date=17 January 2024 |access-date=3 January 2024 |work=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> On 21 March 2024, the advancing lava began to fill a [[stone quarry]] previously utilised for sourcing materials for the barriers. However, workers are still able to obtain materials from alternative locations.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |date=21 March 2024 |title=Hraun fossar ofan í Melhólsnámu: "Bagalegt" að missa námuna |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242546513d/hraun-fossar-ofan-i-melholsnamu-baga-legt-ad-missa-namuna |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=22 March 2024 |title=Dregið hefur úr gosvirkninni í nótt |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/408186 |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Additionally, recently solidified lava from the eruption was used as a resource for assembling the protective structures.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=26 March 2024 |title=Nýrunnið hraun notað til að hækka varnargarðana |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242548796d/ny-runnid-hraun-notad-til-ad-haekka-varnar-gardana |access-date=26 March 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> In late March, authorities decided to raise the height of the barriers north-east of Grindavík in response to the thickening lava field from the March–May 2024 eruption, which posed a risk of overspill. Construction crews worked around the clock on the project.<ref>{{Cite news |date=21 March 2024 |title=Ætla að hækka varnargarða |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/21/aetla_ad_haekka_varnargarda/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325023724/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/21/aetla_ad_haekka_varnargarda/}}</ref> By the end of April 2024, lava began to creep over the same section of the barriers, though it posed no immediate threat.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Rafn Ágúst |date=27 April 2024 |title=Hraun mjakast yfir varnargarð |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242562773d/hraun-mjakast-yfir-varnar-gard |access-date=28 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> At the beginning of October 2025, authorities announced another increase in the height of the barriers north of Grindavík, raising them by {{convert|2–3|m|abbr=unit}} along a {{convert|450|m|abbr=unit}} section.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Kristjánsson |first=Alexander |date=8 October 2025 |title=Varnargarðar við Grindavík verða hækkaðir |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/455613 |access-date=9 October 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Davíðsdóttir |first=Erla María |date=10 October 2025 |title=Vinna hafin við að hækka varnargarðana við Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/455839 |access-date=10 October 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

Construction of another barrier commenced on 6 May 2024, following approval from then-Justice Minister [[Guðrún Hafsteinsdóttir]]. This decision was made after she received a [[memorandum]] from the Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management a week earlier. Initially intended to be the final initiative by the Icelandic government regarding the eruptive series in [[Sundhnúksgígar]],<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=16 May 2024 |title="Síðasta stóra verkefnið" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/16/sidasta_stora_verkefnid/ |access-date=17 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =17 May 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240517173055/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/16/sidasta_stora_verkefnid/}}</ref> the May–June 2024 eruption necessitated an additional protective barrier.<ref name=":29" /> This new barrier, located inside the perimeter of the existing barriers and closer to Grindavík, was designed to proactively safeguard against potential lava overflow, particularly in scenarios involving thin-flowing [[Lava#Pāhoehoe|pāhoehoe lava]]. The structure stands {{convert|5|m|abbr=unit}} high and extends between {{convert|0.8 and 1|km|abbr=unit}} in length.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=6 May 2024 |title=Vinna hafin við gerð nýs varnargarðs |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/06/vinna_hafin_vid_gerd_nys_varnargards/ |access-date=7 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =7 May 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240507235557/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/06/vinna_hafin_vid_gerd_nys_varnargards/}}</ref> A team of approximately 30 to 35 individuals worked day shifts on this project,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Egilsdóttir |first=Urður |date=10 May 2024 |title=Goslok hafa ekki áhrif á vinnuna |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/10/goslok_hafa_ekki_ahrif_a_vinnuna/ |access-date=10 May 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =10 May 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240510170609/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/05/10/goslok_hafa_ekki_ahrif_a_vinnuna/}}</ref> completing the barrier in June 2024.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bárðarson |first=Hilmar Bragi |date=30 May 2024 |title=Unnið af kappi við varnargarða austan byggðarinnar í Grindavík |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/unnid-af-kappi-vid-varnargarda-austan-byggdarinnar-i-grindavik |access-date=24 July 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref>

On 14 January<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2024 |title=Myndskeið: Hraun runnið yfir Grindavíkurveg |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/myndskeid_hraun_runnid_yfir_grindavikurveg/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325021100/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/14/myndskeid_hraun_runnid_yfir_grindavikurveg/}}</ref> and 29 May 2024,<ref name=":30">{{Cite web |date=30 May 2024 |title=Hraun runnið yfir vegi við Grindavík |url=https://www.vegagerdin.is/vegagerdin/starfsemi/frettir/hraun-runnid-yfir-vegi-vid-grindavik |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Road Administration|Icelandic Road and Coastal Administration]] |language=is}}</ref> two separate lava flows engulfed Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) south of the Svartsengi power station and the Blue Lagoon. On 8 February,<ref name=":70">{{Cite web |date=8 February 2024 |title=Hraun flæðir yfir Grindavíkurveg |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/08/hraun_flaedir_yfir_grindavikurveg/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325021026/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/08/hraun_flaedir_yfir_grindavikurveg/}}</ref> 17 March,<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2024 |title=Myndskeið: Flæðir yfir Grindavíkurveg og nálgast Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/myndskeid_flaedir_yfir_grindavikurveg_og_nalgast_gr/ |access-date=25 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =25 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240325112126/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/myndskeid_flaedir_yfir_grindavikurveg_og_nalgast_gr/}}</ref> 8 June,<ref name=":31">{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=8 June 2024 |title=Hraun runnið að Grindavíkurvegi |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242582329d/hraun-runnid-ad-grindavikurvegi |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> and 21 November<ref name=":71">{{Cite web |last=Pétursson |first=Vésteinn Örn |date=21 November 2024 |title=Magnaðar myndir sýna Grindavíkurveg undir hrauni |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242652974d/magnadar-myndir-syna-grinda-vikur-veg-undir-hrauni |access-date=27 November 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> 2024, different lava flows overtook the same road north of these landmarks. Route 426 (Norðurljósavegur) was covered by lava on 8 February<ref name=":70" /> and 21 November<ref name=":71" /> 2024 north of Svartsengi, and on 29 May<ref name=":30" /> south of Svartsengi in the same year. On 29 May 2024,<ref name=":30" /> Route 425 (Nesvegur) was overtaken by lava for the first time. In total, lava flowed over three different roads, five different sections, during five distinct eruptions, for a total of nine occurrences. These incidents repeatedly severed Grindavík's main access to the northern peninsula, necessitating the rapid construction of temporary [[Gravel road|gravel roads]] over freshly cooled lava, with road alignments sometimes adjusted to accommodate the altered [[terrain]]. To date, approximately {{convert|9–10|km|abbr=unit}} of new roads have been constructed, though most have been permanently lost due to repeated lava flows over the same sections.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðjónsson |first=Viðar |date=27 November 2024 |title=Leggja nýjan veg í gegnum glóandi hraunið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/27/leggja_nyjan_veg_i_gegnum_gloandi_hraunid/ |access-date=27 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":72">{{Cite web |last=Davíðsdóttir |first=Erla María |date=20 March 2025 |title=Tveir öflugir vegir á Reykjanesskaga forgangsatriði til framtíðar |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-03-20-tveir-oflugir-vegir-a-reykjanesskaga-forgangsatridi-til-framtidar-439304 |access-date=20 March 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Defence barrier north of Grindavík.jpg|thumb|Protective barrier constructed between Grindavík and Þorbjörn]]

In the early stages of the March–May 2024 eruption, the encroaching lava threatened Route 427 (Suðurstrandarvegur) as it amassed against the eastern protective barrier of the town and advanced towards the sea to the south.<ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2024 |title=Suðurstrandarvegur í hættu |url=http://www.vegagerdin.is/upplysingar-og-utgafa/frettir/sudurstrandarvegur-i-haettu |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[Icelandic Road Administration|Icelandic Road and Coastal Administration]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=17 March 2024 |title="Suðurstrandarvegur road is in danger" |url=https://icelandmonitor.mbl.is/news/news/2024/03/17/sudurstrandarvegur_road_is_in_danger/ |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[Iceland Monitor]] |language=en}}</ref> This put two of the three crucial roadways linking Grindavík at risk, sparing only Route 425 (Nesvegur). Concerns grew as there was a chance the lava would make it to the ocean near the road, potentially causing minor explosions<ref>{{Cite web |date=16 March 2024 |title=Ólíklegt að hraun nái til sjávar |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/407472 |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> and emitting toxic gases like [[Hydrochloric acid|hydrochloric acid (HCl)]] due to [[chemical reaction]]s with the sea.<ref>{{Cite news |date=17 March 2024 |title=Yrði 20 klukkustundir að ná yfir veginn |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/yrdi_20_klukkustundir_ad_na_yfir_veginn/ |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =3 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240403013229/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/03/17/yrdi_20_klukkustundir_ad_na_yfir_veginn/}}</ref> A zone extending {{convert|500|m|abbr=unit}} from the anticipated point of marine entry was deemed critically dangerous to human safety. However, these risks were allayed when the lava halted approximately {{convert|300|m|abbr=unit}} from Route 427, just days after the eruption began.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ísleifsson |first=Atli |date=18 March 2024 |title=Hraunið á um 330 metra í Suðurstrandarveg |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242544425d/hraunid-a-um-330-metra-i-sudur-strandar-veg |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Since then, the lava front has remained unchanged.<ref name=":0" />

On the evening of 20 June 2024, lava began to overspill a section of the barrier north-west of Sýlingarfell mountain after accumulating north and south of the protected Blue Lagoon and Svartsengi area.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jósefsdóttir |first=Sólrún Dögg |date=29 May 2024 |title="Varnargarðarnir stóðu sig mjög vel" |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242577508d/-varnargardarnir-stodu-sig-mjog-vel- |access-date=25 July 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Before the overspill, workers had already begun efforts to stop or slow down the lava by [[Bulldozer|bulldozing]] old soil over it and using at least three [[Fire engine|firefighting trucks]] along with equipment from [[Keflavík International Airport|Keflavík Airport]] and Grindavík to pump water via [[Fire hose|fire hoses]], with "quite good results".<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bárðarson |first=Hilmar Bragi |date=21 June 2024 |title=Myndir - Hraun rennur yfir varnargarð við Svartsengi |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/myndir---hraun-rennur-yfir-varnargard-vid-svartsengi |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Geirsdóttir Håkansson |first=Ellen |date=21 June 2024 |title=Hraunkæling hafin á ný fyrir ofan Svartsengi |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/06/21/hraunkaeling_hafin_a_ny_fyrir_ofan_svartsengi/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> This marks the first such lava mitigation attempt since the [[Eldfell#The eruption begins|1973 eruption in Heimaey]].<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Sigurðsson |first1=Grétar Þór |last2=Ómarsdóttir |first2=Alma |date=18 June 2024 |title=Grípa til hraunkælingar í fyrsta sinn frá Heimaeyjargosi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/415955 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> New roads were not constructed until the eruption ceased later in June. Most have already been rebuilt with [[Base course|base layers]] and [[Wearing course|gravel wearing courses]] over the newly solidified lava. The completion time of the project depended on the temperature of the lava.<ref name=":40" /><ref name=":24">{{Cite web |last=Hrólfsson |first=Ragnar Jón |date=17 July 2024 |title=Vinna við varnargarða hefur kostað 5,5 milljarða hingað til |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/417908 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> In the event of an imminent lava flow approaching the defence barriers, gaps in the protective wall that serve as road access points are temporarily sealed using bulldozers to maintain the integrity of the barrier. Once the volcanic hazard has subsided and the area is deemed safe, the gaps are cleared and reopened to restore transportation access.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Jónsdóttir |first=Margrét Björk |date=8 June 2024 |title=Hraun runnið að Grindavíkurvegi |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242582329d/hraun-runnid-ad-grindavikurvegi |access-date=31 August 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

During the May–June 2024 eruption, the largest to date at that time, lava flowed over three road sections on the day it began: Route 43 (Grindavíkurvegur) south of Svartsengi, the southernmost part of Route 426 (Norðurljósavegur), and for the first time, Route 425 (Nesvegur) just west of the town.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 May 2024 |title=Hraun farið yfir Nesveg einnig |url=https://www.almannavarnir.is/frettir/miklar-likur-a-ad-nesvegur-lokist-fljotlega/ |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=Department of Civil Protection and Emergency Management |language=is}}</ref><ref name=":30" /> Around 10 days after the eruption began, on 8 June, lava also flowed over the northern part of Route 43.<ref name=":31" /> Moreover, the eruption threatened Route 427 (Suðurstrandarvegur) for a period of time. In mid-June, authorities observed that the lava was nearing the top of the north-eastern section of the Svartsengi barrier, which safeguards the power plant and the [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon]]. Consequently, they decided to heighten the existing barriers, which have now reached a height of {{convert|25|m|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsdóttir |first=Þorgerður Anna |date=15 July 2024 |title=Bætt í varnargarðana í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/417763 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> However, as the lava began to breach these barriers, they resolved to construct a new one within the existing defence [[perimeter]] in the Svartsengi area to provide enhanced protection for these critical infrastructures. They also decided to close a geographical gap between the mountain Þorbjörn and the area surrounding Hagafell,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurðsson |first=Benedikt |date=22 July 2024 |title=Vinnu við nýjan varnargarð við það að ljúka |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/418193 |access-date=25 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> as well as reinforce the existing barriers most at risk of lava overflow.<ref name=":29">{{Cite web |last1=Ómarsdóttir |first1=Alma |last2=Hannesdóttir Diego |first2=Hugrún |date=21 June 2024 |title=Reisa nýjan varnargarð fyrir innan þann sem hraun rennur yfir |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/416159 |access-date=18 July 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Bárðarson |first=Hilmar Bragi |date=8 August 2024 |title=Styrkja varnargarð vestan við Hagafell |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/styrkja-varnargard-vestan-vid-hagafell |access-date=11 August 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref> 

After the August–September 2024 eruption, it became clear that the lava field was fairly close to [[Route 41 (Iceland)|Route 41]] (Reykjanesbraut), Iceland's critical road linking the capital Reykjavík to Keflavík International Airport. Volcanologist Þorvaldur Þórðarson has cautioned that a new eruption on the Reykjanes Peninsula could result in lava reaching Route 41 within a day, or even just a few hours, if it matches the intensity of the most powerful eruptions in the volcanic series. Such an occurrence has not been documented in this area over the past 13,000 years.<ref name=":45" /> While no decisions have yet been made regarding the construction of protective barriers, plans are in place to safeguard the road if and when the lava flow threatens it.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Bergsson |first=Óskar |date=27 August 2024 |title=Áætlanir til um varnargarða |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/27/aaetlanir_til_um_varnargarda/ |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The closest lava flow is currently about {{convert|2.7|km|abbr=unit}} from this vital infrastructure.<ref name=":41" /><ref name=":36">{{Cite web |last=Ketilsson |first=Páll |date=25 August 2024 |title=Stöðug virkni í gosinu - nyrsta hrauntungan um 3 km. frá Reykjanesbraut og 1900 m frá Snorrastaðatjörnum |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/stodug-virkni-i-gosinu---nyrsta-hrauntungan-um-3-km-fra-reykjanesbraut-og-1900-m-fra-snorrastadatjornum |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurðsson |first=Bjarki |date=7 September 2024 |title=Hraun gæti náð að Reykjanesbraut á skömmum tíma í næsta gosi |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242617864d/hraun-gaeti-nad-ad-reykjanesbraut-a-skommum-tima-i-naesta-gosi |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Protective barriers for the town of [[Vogar (municipality)|Vogar]], located north of Route 41, were considered after [[Simulation modeling|lava flow simulations]]<ref name=":72" /> indicated a potential, though unlikely, risk of lava reaching the area in the future.<ref>{{Cite web |date=19 December 2023 |title=Varnargarðar við Voga ein sviðsmynda |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/varnargardar_vid_voga_ein_svidsmynda/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231219220542/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/12/19/varnargardar_vid_voga_ein_svidsmynda/ |archive-date=19 December 2023 |access-date=20 December 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=9 February 2024 |title=Varnargarðar við Reykjanesbraut ein sviðsmynd |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/varnargardar_vid_reykjanesbraut_ein_svidsmynd/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240210053752/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/09/varnargardar_vid_reykjanesbraut_ein_svidsmynd/ |archive-date=10 February 2024 |access-date=10 February 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=29 February 2024 |title=Vogar líklega ekki í hættu |url=https://www.vf.is/frettir/vogar-liklega-ekki-i-haettu |access-date=23 November 2024 |website=[[Víkurfréttir|VF]] |language=is}}</ref>

[[File:Opening in protective barrier.png|thumb|Opening in protective barrier for transport into and out of the Svartsengi area; bulldozers push in material to seal it during eruptions]]

In the November–December 2024 eruption, lava advanced along the northern and north-western sides of Svartsengi's protective barriers, completely submerging the Blue Lagoon's entire parking area and the main section of the Route 426 (Norðurljósavegur) access road. While no vehicles were damaged, a temporary [[Prefabricated building|prefabricated service building]] constructed from [[Shipping container architecture|shipping containers]] was engulfed by the lava.<ref name=":48" /> Late on the second day of the November–December 2024 eruption, authorities decided to raise the north-western section of the barrier protecting the Blue Lagoon and the Svartsengi power station by {{convert|3–4|m|abbr=unit}}, as the lava had nearly reached the barrier's height.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Guðmundsdóttir |first1=Ingibjörg Sara |last2=Ómarsdóttir |first2=Alma |date=24 November 2024 |title=Verkinu langt í frá lokið |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-24-verkinu-langt-i-fra-lokid-428636 |access-date=24 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> At the beginning of 2025, the barriers surrounding the Svartsengi area reached a maximum height of {{convert|17|m|abbr=unit}}.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Arnljótsdóttir |first=Þórdís |date=12 February 2025 |title=Varnargarðar tilbúnir fyrir eldgos |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2025-02-12-varnargardar-tilbunir-fyrir-eldgos-436132 |access-date=13 February 2025 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> Construction crews also utilised newly solidified lava to augment the barrier. Workers operated around the clock to complete the project.<ref name=":21" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurþórsdóttir |first=Sunna Karen |date=22 November 2024 |title=Hraunið nánast komið upp í sömu hæð og varnargarðarnir við Svartsengi |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-11-22-hraunid-nanast-komid-upp-i-somu-haed-og-varnargardarnir-vid-svartsengi-428475 |access-date=22 November 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ægisson |first=Egill Aaron |date=24 November 2024 |title=Varnargarðar hækkaðir um 3 til 4 metra |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/11/24/varnargardar_haekkadir_um_3_til_4_metra/ |access-date=24 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

In response to these developments, the Icelandic government implemented a 0.008% additional [[property tax]], levied on the fire insurance value of properties, to fund the construction of lava barriers on the Reykjanes Peninsula and safeguard local infrastructure from potential volcanic eruptions. The temporary tax, which is intended to last for three years, came into effect on 1 January 2024, and is projected to raise nearly ISK 1 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|1000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) annually. The tax is scheduled to expire on 31 December 2026.<ref name=":5" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ćirić |first=Jelena |date=13 November 2023 |title=Iceland's Parliament Proposes Tax to Fund Lava Barriers |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/nature-travel/icelands-parliament-proposes-tax-to-fund-lava-barriers/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231128054659/https://www.icelandreview.com/nature-travel/icelands-parliament-proposes-tax-to-fund-lava-barriers/ |archive-date=28 November 2023 |access-date=29 November 2023 |website=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Andrésdóttir |first=Iðunn |date=13 November 2023 |title=Telur landsmenn tilbúna að leggja sitt af mörkum |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/13/telur_landsmenn_tilbuna_ad_leggja_sitt_af_morkum/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231204223856/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/13/telur_landsmenn_tilbuna_ad_leggja_sitt_af_morkum/ |archive-date=4 December 2023 |access-date=30 November 2023 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> The total cost for all defence barriers amounts to ISK 10–11 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|10000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}–{{To USD round|11000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}).<ref name=":69" /><ref name=":72" /> Approximately {{convert|14|km|abbr=unit}} of defence barriers have been constructed to date, using around {{convert|3|e6m3|abbr=unit}} of excavated material.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Guðjónsson |first=Viðar |date=29 November 2024 |title="Kveðjustundirnar eru farnar að verða erfiðari" |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2023/11/13/telur_landsmenn_tilbuna_ad_leggja_sitt_af_morkum/ |access-date=29 November 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

== Injuries and fatality ==
On 10 January 2024, just before the second eruption, a worker went missing while finalising the filling of Grindavík's largest fault, after the ground suddenly collapsed midway down the fissure, dragging the top fill and the worker into its depths.<ref name="visir-20241113">{{Cite news |last1=Ragnarsson |first1=Rafn Ágúst |last2=Bernharðsdóttir |first2=Berghildur Erla |date=13 January 2024 |title=Hrun í miðri sprungu dró manninn með sér |trans-title=The collapse in the middle of the fissure dragged the man along with it |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242513685d/hrun-i-midri-sprungu-dro-manninn-med-ser |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240111192501/https://www.visir.is/g/20242513685d/hrun-i-midri-sprungu-dro-manninn-med-ser |archive-date=11 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> At the time of this incident, there were no witnesses present. A colleague, finding no one upon return, reported the disappearance, initiating a search. Despite utilising [[3D scanning|3D scanners]]<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hilmarsson |first=Guðmundur |date=12 January 2024 |title=Láta þrívíddarskanna síga niður sprunguna |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/12/lata_thrividdarskanna_siga_nidur_sprunguna/ |access-date=28 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =28 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240328234919/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/12/lata_thrividdarskanna_siga_nidur_sprunguna/}}</ref> and [[underwater drone]]s in the fissure, which is {{convert|40|m|4=|abbr=unit}} deep with water found at {{convert|20|m|abbr=unit}}, the worker was not located due to the fissure's treacherous confines.<ref>{{Cite web |last1=Guðmundsson |first1=Brynjólfur Þór |last2=Ómarsdóttir |first2=Alma |last3=Holm |first3=Haukur |last4=Björnsson |first4=Arnar |date=11 January 2024 |title=Engin ummerki um manninn sem féll í sprungu í Grindavík |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/401600 |access-date=28 March 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> The search, which included up to 70 rescue workers from the [[Icelandic Association for Search and Rescue]], was terminated on 12 January due to the hazardous conditions. Following the search, [[Government agencies in Iceland|The Administration of Occupational Safety and Health]] opened an investigation into the incident, which spanned several months.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnhildardóttir |first=Hólmfríður María |date=12 January 2024 |title=Vinnueftirlitið rannsakar slysið í Grindavík |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/12/vinnueftirlitid_rannsakar_slysid_i_grindavik/ |access-date=28 March 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =28 March 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240328234922/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/01/12/vinnueftirlitid_rannsakar_slysid_i_grindavik/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurþórsdóttir |first=Sunna Karen |date=1 October 2024 |title=Slysið í Grindavík: Áhættumat skorti, skipulag óreiðukennt og spurt hvort þetta hafi verið áhættunnar virði |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2024-10-01-slysid-i-grindavik-ahaettumat-skorti-skipulag-oreidukennt-og-spurt-hvort-thetta-hafi-verid-ahaettunnar-virdi-423440 |access-date=2 October 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> With the individual presumed deceased,<ref name="missing">{{Cite news |last1=Sigurðsson |first1=Bjarki |date=13 January 2024 |title=Ekki hægt að réttlæta áframhaldandi leit |trans-title=It is not possible to justify continued searching |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242514430d/ekki-haegt-ad-rettlaeta-aframhaldandi-leit |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240113133524/https://www.visir.is/g/20242514430d/ekki-haegt-ad-rettlaeta-aframhaldandi-leit |archive-date=13 January 2024 |access-date=14 January 2024 |work=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> this incident represents the first death in Iceland associated with a volcanic eruption since the [[Eldfell#The eruption begins|Heimaey eruption in 1973]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Guðmundsson |first1=Gunnar |last2=Larsen |first2=Guðrún |year=2016 |title=Áhrif eldgosa á heilsu manna á Íslandi. Yfirlitsgrein |url=https://www.laeknabladid.is/media/tolublod/1738/PDF/f02.pdf |journal=Læknablaðið |language=is, en |volume=102 |issue=10 |pages=433–441 |doi=10.17992/lbl.2016.10.101 |pmid=27813483 |via=}}</ref>

On 20 March 2024, an incident at the Blue Lagoon resulted in an employee suffering from [[Toxicity#Environmental hazards|gas poisoning]], attributed to a substantial release of [[sulfur dioxide]] from the March–May 2024 eruption, which was about {{convert|3|km|abbr=unit}} from the geothermal spa. Despite security personnel being equipped with portable [[gas meter]]s and the existence of several fixed gas meters across the hot springs area, the specific area where the incident occurred lacked proper monitoring. The affected employee required hospitalisation but eventually made a full recovery. The incident prompted the Blue Lagoon management to undertake a comprehensive reassessment of the site's safety measures. In response to the situation, police were dispatched to inspect the facility.<ref name=":12" /><ref name=":13" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæberg |first=Árni |date=21 March 2024 |title=Starfsmaðurinn á batavegi og stjórnendum brugðið |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242546500d/starfs-madurinn-a-bata-vegi-og-stjorn-endum-brugdid |access-date=3 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

On the night of 23 August 2024, shortly after midnight, a hiker who had walked from [[Route 41 (Iceland)|Route 41]] (Reykjanesbraut) to the eruption site fell into a small crack and broke his leg. He was later rescued by emergency personnel. Despite previous warnings from authorities emphasising the dangers of walking in the area at night, many individuals—often tourists arriving from Keflavík Airport—continued to attempt the trek. Authorities had warned that navigating the area in complete darkness or bad weather conditions is "extremely difficult and dangerous"<ref name=":35" /> due to numerous rifts that are hard to detect. Additionally, part of the area is an old bomb practice site, containing numerous unexploded [[Shell (projectile)|ordnance]].<ref name=":38" /> In the immediate aftermath of the incident, authorities continued to patrol the area to prevent further injuries or even potential fatalities.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sverrisson |first=Ólafur Björn |date=23 August 2024 |title=Fótbrotnaði í nótt á leið að gosstöðvum |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242611554d/fot-brotnadi-i-nott-a-leid-ad-gos-stodvum |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Gunnarsson |first=Hermann Nökkvi |date=23 August 2024 |title=Fótbrotnaði eftir fall ofan í sprungu |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/23/fotbrotnadi_eftir_fall_ofan_i_sprungu/ |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Sæmundsen |first=Guðrún Sigríður |date=23 August 2024 |title=Gossvæðið mjög hættulegt og mikið sprungið |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/08/23/gossvaedid_mjog_haettulegt_og_mikid_sprungid/ |access-date=8 September 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref>

== International media coverage and tourism ==
The recent volcanic eruptions on the Reykjanes Peninsula created a negative impact on [[tourism in Iceland]] due to misleading [[Media coverage|news coverage]] by foreign media. Foreign media outlets focused on the state of emergency declared in Grindavík,<ref name=":3" /><ref name=":16" /> which gave the impression that the entire country was unsafe. This led to cancellations and a decrease in new bookings and prompted Business Iceland to correct misconceptions and promote Iceland as a safe and unique destination, producing a Q&A video in collaboration with the [[Icelandic Meteorological Office]] (IMO), and launching a marketing campaign in December 2023. Furthermore, during her tenure as Minister of Culture and Business Affairs, [[Lilja Alfreðsdóttir]] allocated ISK 100 million ({{US$|{{To USD round|100000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) to a special marketing campaign. Lína Petra Þórarinsdóttir, Head of Tourism at Business Iceland, believes that the negative impact of the volcanic eruptions can be turned into a positive in the long term, with Iceland being promoted as a unique destination with a special natural environment.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ragnarsson |first=Jón Ísak |date=19 March 2024 |title=Umfjöllun um eldsumbrot vísar ferðamönnum frá |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242545363d/um-fjollun-um-elds-um-brot-visar-ferda-monnum-fra |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

Birgir Jónsson, serving as CEO of now-defunct [[Play (airline)|Play Airlines]] at the time, claimed that extensive international media coverage of the volcanic eruptions in Grindavík caused substantial financial damage to Icelandic tourism, costing billions of [[Icelandic króna|krónas]] (tens of millions of [[Dollar|dollars]]), in lost [[revenue]]. He voiced concerns over the exaggerated portrayal by some foreign news outlets, using imagined headlines such as "Iceland on fire" and "Poison gas over Reykjavík" to illustrate how such depictions contribute to portraying an unnecessarily alarming image of Iceland.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Unnarsson |first=Kristján Már |date=23 February 2024 |title=Milljarðatjón vegna umfjöllunar um jarðhræringar við Grindavík |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20242533891d/milljardatjon-vegna-um-fjollunar-um-jard-hraeringar-vid-grinda-vik |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref> Although some international reports warned of toxic gas moving toward the Capital Region,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hutchinson |first=Bill |date=20 December 2023 |title=Iceland volcano eruption triggers toxic air warning |url=https://abcnews.go.com/International/iceland-volcano-eruption-toxic-air-warning/story?id=105806785 |access-date=7 April 2024 |website=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last1=Moody |first1=Oliver |last2=Clatworthy |first2=Ben |last3=Sandeman |first3=George |last4=Burgess |first4=Kaya |date=19 December 2023 |title=Iceland volcano pollution fears as gas may blow over Reykjavik |url=https://www.thetimes.com/world/article/iceland-volcano-eruption-live-grindavik-reykjavik-700np3x36 |url-access=subscription |access-date=7 April 2024 |work=[[The Times]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> later analyses confirmed the eruption's emissions posed no significant toxic risk to the area.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Birgisdóttir |first=Gunnhildur Kjerúlf |date=20 December 2023 |title=Búast má við einhverri gosmengun í byggð í dag |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-20-buast-ma-vid-einhverri-gosmengun-i-byggd-i-dag-400391/ |access-date=7 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> A tourist orientated article inaccurately depicted Grindavík as a ghost town,<ref>{{Cite web |last=Hall |first=Laura |date=17 October 2025 |title=The Icelandic town abandoned to the volcanoes |url=https://www.bbc.com/travel/article/20251015-the-icelandic-town-abandoned-to-the-volcanoes |access-date=30 October 2025 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=en-UK}}</ref> even though hundreds of people commute to the town daily for work and tourists continue to visit.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Chapman |first=Michael |date=25 October 2025 |title=Grindavík Officials Condemn BBC Report on "Ghost Town" |url=https://www.icelandreview.com/news/grindavik-officials-condemn-bbc-report-on-ghost-town/ |access-date=30 October 2025 |website=[[Iceland Review]] |language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hálfdanarson |first=Björn Diljan |date=26 October 2025 |title=BBC bregst við brigslum Grindvíkinga |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2025/10/26/bbc_bregst_vid_brigslum_grindvikinga/ |access-date=30 October 2025 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is}}</ref> Since November 2023, the [[Blue Lagoon (geothermal spa)|Blue Lagoon]] has also faced challenges similar to those of Play Airlines, incurring losses of ISK 7–8 billion ({{US$|{{To USD round|7000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}–{{To USD round|8000000000|ISL|2024|sf=2}}}}) due to frequent closures.<ref name=":20" /><ref name=":59" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Ægisson |first=Hörður |date=3 May 2025 |title=Beinn kostnaður Bláa lónsins vegna jarðhræringa nálgast um átta milljarða |url=https://www.visir.is/g/20252721461d/beinn-kostnadur-blaa-lonsins-vegna-jard-hraeringa-nalgast-um-atta-milljarda |url-access=subscription |access-date=3 May 2025 |website=[[Vísir.is|Vísir]] |language=is}}</ref>

Jóhannes Þór Skúlason, Managing Director of the Icelandic Travel Industry Association, said the earthquakes and volcanic eruptions on the Reykjanes Peninsula negatively impacted tourist traffic during the holidays. This led to a decrease in visitors over Christmas and New Year after the coverage led people both to call off trips to Iceland<ref>{{Cite web |last=Schram |first=Höskuldur Kári |date=26 December 2023 |title=Færri ferðamenn út af eldsumbrotum á Reykjanesskaga |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/2023-12-26-faerri-ferdamenn-ut-af-eldsumbrotum-a-reykjanesskaga-400728/ |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref> and lower-than-expected demand in January and February 2024 compared to 2023.<ref>{{Cite web |date=5 February 2024 |title=Ferðamenn veigra sér við að koma til Íslands |url=https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/05/ferdamenn_veigra_ser_vid_ad_koma_til_islands/ |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[Morgunblaðið|MBL]] |language=is|archive-date =2 April 2024|archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20240402020158/https://www.mbl.is/frettir/innlent/2024/02/05/ferdamenn_veigra_ser_vid_ad_koma_til_islands/}}</ref> According to figures by [[Statistics Iceland]], registered guest nights in January 2024 were 13% fewer than in January 2023, or 390,000 instead of 450,000. Bjarnheiður Hallsdóttir, chairman of the same association, attributed the decline in part to the changed attitude towards travelling to Iceland, including the media coverage of the earthquakes in Grindavík.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sigurðsson |first=Benedikt |date=11 March 2024 |title=Ísland að kólna |url=https://www.ruv.is/frettir/innlent/407088 |access-date=2 April 2024 |website=[[RÚV]] |language=is}}</ref>

==Reactions==
In a televised address following the 14 January 2024 eruption, then-President [[Guðni Th. Jóhannesson]] said that "a daunting period of upheaval has begun on the Reykjanes Peninsula", but urged the need to continue taking "actions that are within our power", while "hope for as good an outcome as possible".<ref name="ap-20240116" /> He also urged citizens to "stand together and have compassion" for those displaced by the eruption. Then-Prime Minister [[Katrín Jakobsdóttir]] said the eruption was a "black day for all of Iceland", but added that "the sun will rise again", and expressed solidarity with those affected.<ref name="bbc14jan"/>

